window.UNITS=[{"id":"u1","title":"Finance Foundations & Markets","day":1,"summary":"What a financial manager does, why the goal is shareholder wealth, how business forms are taxed, how markets and regulators work, and how interest rates are built.","lesson":[{"h":"1. What a financial manager actually does","body":"<p><strong>Corporate finance</strong> is the study of how a business raises money and spends it wisely. Picture Brightline Bakery, a company that wants to open a second location. Somebody has to answer three big questions, and those three questions are the whole job of the financial manager.</p><ol><li><strong>Capital budgeting</strong> (the investment decision): which long-term assets should we buy? Opening the second store, buying a new oven, or launching a delivery van all fall here.</li><li><strong>Capital structure</strong> (the financing decision): how should we pay for it? Borrow from a bank (debt), sell ownership (equity), or use profits we already kept (retained earnings)? The mix of debt and equity is the capital structure.</li><li><strong>Working capital management</strong> (the short-term decision): how do we handle day-to-day cash, inventory, what customers owe us, and what we owe suppliers, so we can always pay the bills?</li></ol><p>Memory hook: <em>What to buy, how to pay, and how to keep the lights on.</em> The CFO, treasurer (cash, debt, risk) and controller (accounting, taxes) are the usual finance team. Remember that the <em>controller</em> looks backward at the books while the <em>treasurer</em> looks forward at raising and managing cash.</p>"},{"h":"2. The goal: maximize shareholder wealth, not just profit","body":"<p>The textbook goal of the firm is to <strong>maximize shareholder wealth</strong>, which in practice means maximizing the <strong>current market price of the firm's stock</strong>. Why not simply maximize profit? Because profit alone ignores three things that matter.</p><ul><li><strong>Timing:</strong> $100,000 of profit this year is worth more than $110,000 of profit in eight years, because money today can be invested.</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> a shaky $200,000 profit can be worth less than a safe $150,000 profit.</li><li><strong>Cash vs. accounting profit:</strong> accounting earnings can be dressed up (for example by delaying maintenance) while real cash flow cannot.</li></ul><p>Stock price reflects the <em>size, timing and risk of all future cash flows</em>, so it captures all three. That is why finance says: take every project whose value is greater than its cost, and judge managers by what happens to the share price.</p><p><strong>Stakeholders</strong> (employees, customers, communities) matter, and ethical firms treat them well, but the exam goal is still shareholder wealth because shareholders are the residual owners who get paid last.</p>"},{"h":"3. The agency problem and corporate governance","body":"<p>In a small shop the owner makes every decision. In a big corporation, thousands of shareholders (the <strong>principals</strong>) hire managers (the <strong>agents</strong>) to run things. The <strong>agency problem</strong> is the risk that managers act in their own interest instead of the owners' interest. A CEO who buys a luxury jet, builds an empire through a poor acquisition, or avoids any risky-but-profitable project to protect her job is imposing an <strong>agency cost</strong>.</p><p>Agency costs include the cost of monitoring (audits, board oversight), bonding, and the value lost when managers still pick the wrong actions. Ways to align interests:</p><ul><li><strong>Pay for performance:</strong> stock options, restricted stock and bonuses tied to share price.</li><li><strong>Board of directors:</strong> elected by shareholders to hire, fire and pay the CEO. Independent directors help.</li><li><strong>Threat of takeover:</strong> a badly run firm's stock falls, making it a cheap target for a hostile bidder who will replace management.</li><li><strong>Proxy fights and shareholder activism</strong>, plus monitoring by auditors, analysts and big institutional investors.</li><li><strong>Creditors' covenants:</strong> lenders add rules to loans, because there is also an agency conflict between <em>shareholders and bondholders</em> (shareholders may be tempted to take bigger risks after borrowing).</li></ul><p><strong>Corporate governance</strong> is the set of rules, boards and incentives that keep this system honest.</p>"},{"h":"4. Business forms: liability and taxes","body":"<p>The form you choose decides two things: <strong>who is personally on the hook for debts</strong> and <strong>how profits are taxed</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Sole proprietorship:</strong> one owner, easiest to start. Unlimited personal liability. Income is taxed once, on the owner's personal return. Hard to raise money; ends when the owner does.</li><li><strong>Partnership:</strong> two or more owners. In a general partnership every partner has unlimited liability. A limited partnership has at least one general partner and limited partners whose loss is capped at what they invested. Pass-through taxation (no entity-level tax).</li><li><strong>LLC (limited liability company):</strong> owners (members) get limited liability <em>and</em> by default pass-through taxation. Flexible and popular for small firms.</li><li><strong>C corporation:</strong> a separate legal entity. Limited liability, easy to raise capital and transfer shares, unlimited life. Downside: <strong>double taxation</strong>, because the corporation pays tax on profit and shareholders pay tax again on dividends.</li><li><strong>S corporation:</strong> a corporation that elects pass-through treatment, so income is taxed once on owners' returns while keeping limited liability. Limits: no more than <strong>100 shareholders</strong>, only <strong>one class of stock</strong>, and shareholders must be eligible individuals (U.S. citizens or residents, certain trusts).</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> a C corporation earns $200,000 before tax. At a 21% corporate rate it keeps $158,000. If it pays all of that as a dividend and the owner pays 15% on dividends, the owner nets $134,300. As an S corporation, the owner reports the $200,000 on her own return; at a 24% personal bracket she keeps $152,000. Same business, different tax result.</p>"},{"h":"5. Financial markets and institutions","body":"<p>A <strong>financial market</strong> is where savers with extra money meet borrowers who need it. The big dividing lines:</p><ul><li><strong>Primary vs. secondary:</strong> in the <strong>primary market</strong> a company sells <em>new</em> securities and the company receives the cash (an IPO, or initial public offering, is the first time a company sells stock to the public). In the <strong>secondary market</strong> investors trade <em>existing</em> securities with each other (NYSE, Nasdaq); the company gets no cash from those trades, but the market gives liquidity and a price signal.</li><li><strong>Money vs. capital markets:</strong> the <strong>money market</strong> trades short-term debt that matures in <em>one year or less</em> (Treasury bills, commercial paper, certificates of deposit). The <strong>capital market</strong> trades long-term securities: bonds and stocks.</li><li><strong>Dealers vs. brokers:</strong> a <strong>dealer</strong> holds inventory and trades for its own account, earning the <em>bid-ask spread</em> (buy at the bid, sell at the higher ask). A <strong>broker</strong> just matches buyers and sellers and earns a commission, never owning the security.</li><li><strong>Investment banks</strong> help firms sell new securities. In a firm-commitment <strong>underwriting</strong> the bank buys the whole issue and resells it, taking on price risk and earning the <em>underwriting spread</em>.</li></ul><p>Other institutions: commercial banks (take deposits, make loans), insurance companies, pension funds, and mutual funds. They are <em>financial intermediaries</em>, pooling many small savings into large investments.</p>"},{"h":"6. Regulation and ethics","body":"<p>Markets only work if people trust them, so U.S. law requires honest disclosure.</p><ul><li><strong>Securities Act of 1933:</strong> regulates the <em>primary</em> market. Companies must register new securities and give investors a <strong>prospectus</strong> with the facts.</li><li><strong>Securities Exchange Act of 1934:</strong> regulates the <em>secondary</em> market, created the <strong>SEC</strong> (Securities and Exchange Commission), and requires ongoing reports (10-K annually, 10-Q quarterly, 8-K for major events). It also bans market manipulation and covers insider trading.</li><li><strong>Sarbanes-Oxley (SOX, 2002):</strong> passed after Enron and WorldCom. The CEO and CFO must personally certify the financial statements, the PCAOB oversees auditors, and Section 404 requires management to assess internal controls. Auditors face limits on selling consulting to audit clients.</li><li><strong>Dodd-Frank (2010):</strong> passed after the 2008 crisis. Created the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, added the Volcker Rule (limits on banks' proprietary trading), required say-on-pay votes, and created whistleblower rewards.</li></ul><p><strong>Insider trading</strong> means trading on <em>material, nonpublic</em> information. If a VP learns of an unannounced merger and buys stock, or tips a friend, that is illegal. Ethics in finance means putting investors' interests first and being honest, beyond just obeying the law.</p>"},{"h":"7. Interest rates: real, nominal, and the premiums","body":"<p>An interest rate is the price of borrowing money. Two key ideas:</p><ul><li><strong>Nominal rate</strong> = the quoted rate. <strong>Real rate</strong> = the rate after removing inflation, i.e., your actual gain in buying power.</li><li><strong>Fisher effect:</strong> <code>(1 + nominal) = (1 + real) x (1 + inflation)</code>. The quick approximation is nominal = real + inflation. The exact real rate is <code>(1 + nominal) / (1 + inflation) - 1</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> a CD pays 8% nominal while inflation is 3%. Exact real rate = 1.08 / 1.03 - 1 = 4.85% (the shortcut says about 5%).</p><p>A bond's quoted rate is built from a stack of pieces:</p><p><code>r = r* + IP + DRP + LP + MRP</code></p><ul><li><strong>r*</strong>: the real risk-free rate (about what you earn with no risk and no inflation).</li><li><strong>IP</strong>: inflation premium (expected inflation).</li><li><strong>DRP</strong>: default risk premium (chance the borrower does not pay; Treasuries have none).</li><li><strong>LP</strong>: liquidity premium (extra for securities that are hard to sell quickly).</li><li><strong>MRP</strong>: maturity risk premium (extra for long-term bonds, whose prices swing more when rates change).</li></ul><p>A short-term Treasury bill is roughly <code>r* + IP</code>. A long-term Treasury adds MRP. A corporate bond adds DRP and LP on top. Example: r* = 2%, IP = 3%, MRP = 1% gives a 6% Treasury bond; add DRP 1.5% and LP 0.5% for an 8% corporate bond.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["The financial manager makes three kinds of decisions: capital budgeting, capital structure, and working capital management.","The main goal of the firm is to maximize shareholder wealth, which means maximizing the current stock price.","Profit maximization is a weaker goal because it ignores the timing of cash flows and the risk of those cash flows.","The agency problem is a conflict of interest between owners, called principals, and managers, called agents.","Agency costs can be reduced with stock options, an independent board of directors, the threat of takeover, and monitoring by auditors and lenders.","A sole proprietorship and a general partnership have unlimited personal liability, while corporations and LLCs give owners limited liability.","A C corporation suffers double taxation, because profits are taxed at the company level and again when paid out as dividends.","An S corporation passes income through to owners so it is taxed once, but it is limited to 100 shareholders and one class of stock.","In the primary market a company sells new securities and receives the cash, while in the secondary market investors trade with each other.","The money market deals in debt maturing in one year or less, and the capital market deals in long-term bonds and stocks.","The Securities Act of 1933 covers new issues, the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 created the SEC, Sarbanes-Oxley demands executive certification, and Dodd-Frank created the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.","The Fisher effect says one plus the nominal rate equals one plus the real rate times one plus inflation, and a bond rate is the real rate plus premiums for inflation, default, liquidity, and maturity risk."],"examples":[{"q":"A bank CD pays a nominal rate of 8% per year. Expected inflation is 3%. What is the exact real rate of return, and how does it compare with the quick approximation?","steps":["Fisher effect: (1 + nominal) = (1 + real) x (1 + inflation).","Solve for real: real = (1 + nominal) / (1 + inflation) - 1.","real = 1.08 / 1.03 - 1 = 1.048544 - 1 = 0.048544, or 4.85%.","Quick approximation: nominal - inflation = 8% - 3% = 5%. Close, but the exact answer is slightly lower."],"answer":"The exact real rate is about 4.85% (approximation: 5%)."},{"q":"The real risk-free rate is 2%, expected inflation is 3%, and the maturity risk premium on 10-year bonds is 1%. A corporate 10-year bond from Delta Foods carries a default risk premium of 1.5% and a liquidity premium of 0.5%. What yield do the 10-year Treasury bond and the Delta Foods bond offer?","steps":["Treasury bond has no default or liquidity premium: r = r* + IP + MRP = 2% + 3% + 1% = 6%.","Corporate bond adds DRP and LP: r = 6% + 1.5% + 0.5% = 8%.","Check the full stack: 2 + 3 + 1.5 + 0.5 + 1 = 8%."],"answer":"Treasury bond: 6.0%. Delta Foods bond: 8.0%."},{"q":"Taylor Tools earns $200,000 before taxes this year. As a C corporation it would pay 21% corporate tax and distribute everything left as dividends taxed at 15% to its sole owner. As an S corporation, the owner would be taxed at 24% on the pass-through income. How much does the owner keep under each form?","steps":["C corporation: after corporate tax = 200,000 x (1 - 0.21) = 158,000.","Owner's dividend tax: 158,000 x 0.15 = 23,700, so the owner keeps 158,000 - 23,700 = 134,300.","S corporation: 200,000 x (1 - 0.24) = 152,000.","Difference = 152,000 - 134,300 = 17,700 more for the owner under the S corporation (this is double taxation in action)."],"answer":"C corporation: $134,300. S corporation: $152,000."}],"videos":[{"title":"Agency problem corporate finance explained","query":"agency problem agency costs shareholders vs managers corporate finance"},{"title":"Primary vs secondary market, money vs capital market","query":"primary market vs secondary market money market capital market explained"},{"title":"Fisher effect and real vs nominal interest rate","query":"Fisher effect real vs nominal interest rate explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Three decisions of the financial manager","b":"Capital budgeting (what to buy), capital structure (how to finance), working capital management (day-to-day cash)."},{"f":"Primary goal of the firm in finance","b":"Maximize shareholder wealth, i.e., the current market price of the stock."},{"f":"Why is profit maximization an inferior goal?","b":"It ignores timing of cash flows, risk, and can be distorted by accounting choices."},{"f":"Agency problem","b":"Managers (agents) may act in their own interest instead of the shareholders' (principals')."},{"f":"Agency costs","b":"Costs of monitoring, bonding, and lost value from managers' self-serving decisions."},{"f":"Ways to reduce agency problems","b":"Stock options and bonuses, independent board, takeover threat, auditors, creditor covenants."},{"f":"Sole proprietorship: liability and tax","b":"Unlimited personal liability; income taxed once on the owner's return."},{"f":"Limited partner","b":"Passive partner whose loss is capped at the amount invested."},{"f":"LLC","b":"Limited liability for owners plus pass-through taxation by default."},{"f":"C corporation tax issue","b":"Double taxation: corporate profit tax, then tax on dividends."},{"f":"S corporation limits","b":"Max 100 shareholders, one class of stock, eligible (mostly U.S. individual) shareholders; pass-through tax."},{"f":"Primary market","b":"Where issuers sell new securities and receive the proceeds (e.g., an IPO)."},{"f":"Secondary market","b":"Investors trade existing securities; the issuer receives no cash from the trade."},{"f":"Money market vs capital market","b":"Money market: debt maturing in one year or less. Capital market: long-term bonds and stocks."},{"f":"Dealer vs broker","b":"Dealer trades from own inventory and earns the bid-ask spread; broker matches parties for a commission."},{"f":"Investment bank underwriting","b":"Bank buys a new issue from the firm and resells it, bearing price risk for a spread."},{"f":"Securities Act of 1933","b":"Regulates primary market; requires registration and a prospectus."},{"f":"Securities Exchange Act of 1934","b":"Regulates secondary markets; created the SEC; requires 10-K and 10-Q reports."},{"f":"Sarbanes-Oxley Act (2002)","b":"CEO/CFO certify financials, PCAOB oversees auditors, Section 404 internal controls."},{"f":"Dodd-Frank Act (2010)","b":"Created CFPB, Volcker Rule, say-on-pay, whistleblower awards after the 2008 crisis."},{"f":"Insider trading","b":"Illegal trading on material nonpublic information."},{"f":"Fisher effect formula","b":"(1 + nominal) = (1 + real) x (1 + inflation); approx. nominal = real + inflation."}],"pairs":[["Agency cost","Loss from managers not serving owners"],["Primary market","Where new securities are sold by issuers"],["Money market","Debt maturing in one year or less"],["Dealer","Trades from inventory; earns bid-ask spread"],["Underwriter","Bank that buys and resells new issues"],["Fisher effect","Nominal rate = real rate plus inflation"],["Insider trading","Trading on material nonpublic information"],["Pass-through taxation","Business income taxed only on owners' returns"]],"traps":["Trap: Maximizing profit and maximizing stock price are the same goal. Truth: Stock price reflects timing, size and risk of cash flows; profit does not.","Trap: When you buy shares of a company on the NYSE, the company gets your money. Truth: That is the secondary market, so the seller gets the cash; only a primary offering funds the firm.","Trap: An LLC is double taxed like a C corporation. Truth: An LLC gets limited liability and pass-through taxation by default.","Trap: The 1934 Act regulates new stock offerings. Truth: The 1933 Act covers new issues (primary); the 1934 Act covers secondary trading and created the SEC.","Trap: Real rate equals nominal minus inflation exactly. Truth: That is only an approximation; the exact real rate is (1 + nominal) / (1 + inflation) - 1."],"mnemonic":"Bond rate premiums: 'I Don't Like Mondays' on top of the real rate r* = IP (inflation), DRP (default), LP (liquidity), MRP (maturity). Treasuries skip D and L.","cram":["Three financial manager decisions: capital budgeting, capital structure, working capital.","Goal: maximize shareholder wealth, meaning current stock price.","Agency problem: managers (agents) versus owners (principals).","C corporation double taxation; S corporation 100 shareholders, one class of stock.","Money market: one year or less. Capital market: long-term.","1933 Act: new issues. 1934 Act: secondary trading, created SEC.","SOX: CEO/CFO certify. Dodd-Frank: CFPB and Volcker Rule.","Fisher: (1+nominal)=(1+real)(1+inflation); bond rate: r*+IP+DRP+LP+MRP."],"ytv":[{"title":"Agency Problem Explained | Agency Costs, Management vs Shareholders","channel":"Farhat Lectures. The # 1 CPA & Accounting Courses","id":"ZrXj_aL04lM","why":"Clear walkthrough of principals, agents, and agency costs."},{"title":"Corporate Governance: Agency Theory","channel":"Galton College","id":"K-veJE85HVE","why":"Shows how governance mechanisms align managers with shareholders."},{"title":"Financial Statements Explained | Income Statement, Balance Sheet & Cash Flow | Part 1","channel":"Amquest Education","id":"NevOs9QNleg","why":"Handy overview that previews the unit 2 statements."}],"questions":[{"q":"Brightline Bakery is deciding whether to buy a new $90,000 oven that will serve the business for ten years. Which financial management decision is this?","o":["Capital budgeting","Capital structure","Working capital management","Dividend policy review"],"a":0,"e":"Choosing which long-term assets to buy is capital budgeting. Capital structure is about how to pay for assets (debt vs. equity), which is the tempting wrong answer.","d":1},{"q":"According to standard corporate finance theory, the primary goal of a firm's financial manager is to","o":["maximize this year's accounting profit","maximize the current market value of shareholders' equity (the stock price)","maximize sales revenue","minimize the firm's tax payments in every year"],"a":1,"e":"Shareholder wealth, measured by stock price, captures timing and risk of cash flows. Profit alone ignores both, which is why 'maximize profit' is wrong.","d":1},{"q":"A woodworker runs her own shop as a sole proprietorship. A customer wins a large judgment against the shop. Which statement is correct?","o":["Only the money she invested in the business is at risk","Her personal assets are protected because the shop is a separate legal entity","Her personal assets can be used to pay the judgment","The business pays double taxes on the judgment"],"a":2,"e":"Sole proprietors have unlimited personal liability because the business and owner are legally the same. Only corporations and LLCs create a liability shield.","d":1},{"q":"Double taxation is most closely associated with which business form?","o":["Sole proprietorship","General partnership","S corporation","C corporation"],"a":3,"e":"A C corporation pays tax on profits and shareholders pay tax again on dividends. An S corporation was created to avoid exactly that.","d":1},{"q":"Zephyr Inc. sells 1 million brand-new shares to the public through an investment bank and receives the proceeds. This transaction happens in the","o":["secondary market","primary market","money market","over-the-counter dealer market only"],"a":1,"e":"New securities sold by the issuer, with cash going to the issuer, are primary market transactions. Resales among investors are secondary.","d":1},{"q":"Treasury bills, commercial paper, and certificates of deposit are traded in the","o":["capital market","primary equity market","money market","derivatives market only"],"a":2,"e":"The money market handles debt maturing in one year or less. Capital markets handle long-term bonds and stocks.","d":1},{"q":"Which law created the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)?","o":["Securities Act of 1933","Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002","Dodd-Frank Act of 2010","Securities Exchange Act of 1934"],"a":3,"e":"The 1934 Act created the SEC. The 1933 Act is the one that requires registration and a prospectus for new issues, often confused with it.","d":1},{"q":"A VP of Medico Corp. learns that her company will be acquired at a big premium, announced next week. She buys stock today. This is","o":["illegal insider trading, because she used material nonpublic information","legal, because she owns the company's stock as an employee","legal, as long as she holds the stock for more than one year","illegal only if she is a member of the board of directors"],"a":0,"e":"Trading on material nonpublic information is illegal for any insider or tippee, not only directors. Holding period does not cure it.","d":1},{"q":"The nominal interest rate on a loan is 9% and expected inflation is 4%. What is the exact real rate of interest (rounded)?","o":["5.00%","4.00%","4.81%","5.36%"],"a":2,"e":"Exact real = 1.09 / 1.04 - 1 = 4.81%. The quick subtraction 9% - 4% = 5% is only an approximation, and 5.36% comes from wrongly multiplying 1.09 x 1.04 - 1.","d":2},{"q":"The real rate of interest is 3% and expected inflation is 2.5%. Using the Fisher effect exactly, what is the nominal rate (rounded)?","o":["5.50%","0.50%","7.50%","5.58%"],"a":3,"e":"(1.03)(1.025) - 1 = 5.575%, about 5.58%. Simply adding gives 5.5%, ignoring the cross-product of real rate and inflation.","d":2},{"q":"Shareholders of Orion Corp. discover that the CEO spent $4 million of company funds on a private jet and a lavish retreat while the stock underperformed. This situation is best described as","o":["an agency problem that creates agency costs","capital budgeting error","a violation of the Securities Act of 1933","pass-through taxation"],"a":0,"e":"Managers (agents) spending in their own interest at owners' expense is the classic agency problem. It is not necessarily illegal, so a securities-law answer is wrong.","d":2},{"q":"In a firm-commitment underwriting of an IPO, the investment bank","o":["merely matches buyers and sellers for a commission without owning shares","buys the entire issue from the company and resells it, bearing the price risk","guarantees the stock price will never fall","transfers the shares to the SEC for approval"],"a":1,"e":"Underwriters purchase the issue and resell it, earning the spread. A broker (not an underwriter) just matches buyers and sellers.","d":2},{"q":"What is the key difference between a dealer and a broker?","o":["A dealer works only in the primary market; a broker works only in the secondary market","A broker holds inventory; a dealer does not","They are the same except that dealers are regulated by the SEC","A dealer trades from its own inventory and earns the bid-ask spread; a broker matches buyers and sellers for a commission"],"a":3,"e":"Dealers take positions and quote bid and ask prices; brokers act as agents. Both work in secondary markets, so the primary/secondary distinction is wrong.","d":2},{"q":"Following accounting scandals at Enron and WorldCom, which law requires the CEO and CFO to personally certify the accuracy of financial statements?","o":["Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002","Securities Act of 1933","Dodd-Frank Act of 2010","Securities Exchange Act of 1934"],"a":0,"e":"SOX added executive certification, auditor oversight (PCAOB) and internal-control requirements. Dodd-Frank came after the 2008 financial crisis, not the accounting scandals.","d":2},{"q":"Which law created the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau and the Volcker Rule?","o":["Sarbanes-Oxley Act","Dodd-Frank Act","Securities Act of 1933","Securities Exchange Act of 1934"],"a":1,"e":"Dodd-Frank (2010) responded to the 2008 crisis with the CFPB, the Volcker Rule, and say-on-pay. SOX was about corporate accounting reliability.","d":2},{"q":"A small consulting firm has 80 shareholders, all U.S. citizens, one class of common stock, and it wants to avoid double taxation while keeping limited liability. Which form fits?","o":["C corporation","General partnership","S corporation","Sole proprietorship"],"a":2,"e":"An S corporation allows up to 100 eligible shareholders with a single class of stock and gives pass-through taxation. A C corporation would be double taxed.","d":2},{"q":"Which feature makes a limited liability company (LLC) attractive to the owners of a small family business?","o":["Limited personal liability combined with pass-through taxation by default","Limited personal liability combined with mandatory double taxation","A cap of 100 owners and a single class of stock","Unlimited liability for all members but lower filing fees"],"a":0,"e":"LLC members get a liability shield like a corporation but are usually taxed like a partnership. The 100-owner cap is an S corporation rule, not an LLC rule.","d":2},{"q":"Project A will earn a profit of $100,000 next year. Project B will earn a profit of $110,000, but not until 8 years from now, and with much higher risk. Why is 'choose the higher profit' a flawed rule?","o":["It overstates the importance of stock price","It ignores the timing and the risk of the cash flows","Profit is never relevant to financial managers","It violates Sarbanes-Oxley"],"a":1,"e":"Money received sooner and with less risk is worth more, which is why finance uses value (stock price), not raw profit.","d":2},{"q":"The following premiums apply to a corporate bond: real risk-free rate 2.0%, inflation premium 2.5%, default risk premium 1.8%, liquidity premium 0.7%, maturity risk premium 1.0%. What is the bond's required rate of return?","o":["6.8%","7.3%","8.0%","9.0%"],"a":2,"e":"r = 2.0 + 2.5 + 1.8 + 0.7 + 1.0 = 8.0%. 7.3% omits the liquidity premium; 6.8% drops the maturity premium and others.","d":2},{"q":"The real risk-free rate is 1.5% and expected inflation is 3.0%. Ignoring other premiums, the approximate yield on a short-term Treasury bill is","o":["1.5%","3.0%","6.0%","4.5%"],"a":3,"e":"A T-bill has essentially no default, liquidity, or maturity risk, so r is about r* + IP = 1.5% + 3.0% = 4.5%.","d":2},{"q":"Alex buys 100 shares of a publicly traded company through his brokerage account from another investor. Who receives Alex's payment?","o":["The company that issued the shares","The investor who sold the shares","The investment bank that underwrote the original IPO","The SEC"],"a":1,"e":"A secondary market trade moves cash between investors. The issuer only receives cash in primary market sales, which is why the issuer is the tempting wrong answer.","d":2},{"q":"Cobalt Inc. (a C corporation) earns $500,000 before tax, pays 21% corporate tax, and distributes all remaining income as dividends to its sole owner, who pays 20% tax on dividends. How much cash does the owner keep?","o":["$395,000","$400,000","$316,000","$350,000"],"a":2,"e":"After corporate tax: 500,000 x 0.79 = 395,000. Dividend tax: 395,000 x 0.80 = 316,000. $395,000 ignores the second layer of tax.","d":3},{"q":"Pine Co. earns $300,000 pre-tax. As a C corporation it would pay 21% corporate tax and distribute the rest as dividends taxed at 15%. As an S corporation the owner pays 24% on pass-through income. By how much does the owner come out ahead as an S corporation?","o":["$21,000","$17,700","$78,000","$26,550"],"a":3,"e":"C corp: 300,000 x 0.79 x 0.85 = 201,450. S corp: 300,000 x 0.76 = 228,000. Difference = 26,550. $21,000 is just the corporate tax.","d":3},{"q":"A bank offers a nominal rate of 12% when inflation is 5%. By what amount does the approximation (nominal minus inflation) overstate the exact real rate?","o":["About 0.33 percentage points","About 0.60 percentage points","About 1.00 percentage point","It is exact, so 0"],"a":0,"e":"Exact real = 1.12 / 1.05 - 1 = 6.67%. Approximation = 7.00%. The overstatement is 0.33 points.","d":3},{"q":"A 10-year Treasury bond yields 5.5%. The real risk-free rate is 1.5% and the maturity risk premium is 0.5%. Assuming no other premiums, what is the expected inflation premium?","o":["4.0%","3.0%","3.5%","7.5%"],"a":2,"e":"Treasury yield = r* + IP + MRP, so IP = 5.5 - 1.5 - 0.5 = 3.5%. 4.0% forgets to subtract the MRP.","d":3},{"q":"After borrowing heavily from bondholders, the managers of a firm (acting for shareholders) switch the firm's cash into a much riskier venture that could pay off hugely but could also wipe out creditors. This is an example of a conflict between","o":["managers and customers","the SEC and the board","pass-through owners and partners","shareholders and creditors"],"a":3,"e":"Shareholders keep the upside of risky bets while creditors bear much of the downside. Covenants in loan contracts are the common remedy.","d":3},{"q":"DataVue plans an IPO of 2,000,000 shares at $25 per share. The underwriter's spread is 7% of the offering price. What are the company's net proceeds from the issue (ignoring other costs)?","o":["$46,500,000","$50,000,000","$3,500,000","$53,500,000"],"a":0,"e":"Gross proceeds = 2,000,000 x 25 = 50,000,000. The bank keeps 7% = 3,500,000, so the company nets 46,500,000.","d":3},{"q":"Elan Corp. wants to sell new shares of stock to the public for the first time. Which requirement applies first under federal securities law?","o":["File a 10-K with the PCAOB under Sarbanes-Oxley","File a registration statement and provide investors a prospectus under the Securities Act of 1933","Obtain Volcker Rule approval","Register under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 only after the shares trade"],"a":1,"e":"The 1933 Act governs new issues through registration and the prospectus. The 1934 Act governs ongoing reporting and secondary trading afterward.","d":3},{"q":"Under Sarbanes-Oxley Section 404, a public company's management must","o":["pay a bonus tied to share price","hire the same audit firm for consulting","submit all stock trades to the PCAOB before executing them","assess and report on the effectiveness of the company's internal controls over financial reporting"],"a":3,"e":"Section 404 focuses on internal control assessment (and auditor attestation). It does not mandate bonuses or allow more consulting from auditors.","d":3},{"q":"A dealer quotes a stock at a bid of $49.80 and an ask of $50.20. An investor buys 500 shares and immediately sells them back to the same dealer. How much does the investor lose because of the spread?","o":["$200","$100","$400","$0"],"a":0,"e":"Investor pays the ask ($50.20) and receives the bid ($49.80): 0.40 x 500 = $200 loss, which is the dealer's gross profit. Half of that ($100) is a common slip.","d":3}]},{"id":"u2","title":"Financial Statements & Cash Flow","day":1,"summary":"Read the income statement, balance sheet and cash flow statement, and compute EBIT, EBITDA, NOPAT, operating cash flow and free cash flow.","lesson":[{"h":"1. The three statements and why accrual is not cash","body":"<p>Every company reports three core statements. Think of them as a camera and two videos of Harbor Hardware, a small store.</p><ul><li><strong>Balance sheet</strong>: a <em>snapshot</em> on one date of what the firm owns (assets), owes (liabilities), and what is left for owners (equity).</li><li><strong>Income statement</strong>: a <em>movie of a period</em> (quarter or year) showing revenue, expenses and profit.</li><li><strong>Statement of cash flows</strong>: another movie, showing where actual cash came from and went.</li></ul><p><strong>Accrual accounting</strong> (GAAP) records revenue when it is <em>earned</em> and expenses when they are <em>incurred</em>, no matter when cash moves. If Harbor ships $50,000 of goods in December and the customer pays in January, December shows $50,000 of revenue but no cash. <strong>Cash accounting</strong> would record it only in January. This is why a profitable firm can still run out of cash, and why finance cares about cash flow more than accounting profit.</p>"},{"h":"2. The income statement: from sales to net income","body":"<p>Read it top to bottom, subtracting as you go. Harbor Hardware's year:</p><ul><li>Sales: $1,000,000</li><li>minus Cost of goods sold (COGS): $600,000 gives <strong>gross profit</strong> $400,000</li><li>minus SG&A (selling, general and administrative): $150,000</li><li>minus Depreciation: $50,000 gives <strong>EBIT</strong> (earnings before interest and taxes, also called operating income) $200,000</li><li>minus Interest: $20,000 gives <strong>EBT</strong> (pretax income) $180,000</li><li>minus Taxes at 21%: $37,800 gives <strong>net income</strong> $142,200</li></ul><p>Three handy variations:</p><ul><li><strong>EBITDA</strong> = EBIT + depreciation + amortization = 200,000 + 50,000 = $250,000. A rough measure of operating cash earnings.</li><li><strong>NOPAT</strong> (net operating profit after tax) = EBIT x (1 - tax rate) = 200,000 x 0.79 = $158,000. It shows operating profit as if the firm had no debt, so you can compare firms regardless of financing.</li><li>Net income is what belongs to shareholders. They can receive it as dividends or the firm can keep it as <strong>retained earnings</strong>.</li></ul><p>Notice that interest comes out <em>before</em> taxes (it is tax deductible) but dividends come out <em>after</em> (not deductible). That is the root of double taxation and why debt gets a tax advantage.</p>"},{"h":"3. The balance sheet: A = L + E","body":"<p>The accounting identity never breaks: <strong>Assets = Liabilities + Owners' equity</strong>. Whatever the firm owns was paid for by lenders (liabilities) or owners (equity).</p><ul><li><strong>Current assets</strong> (cash, accounts receivable, inventory) turn into cash within a year. They are listed in order of liquidity, most liquid first.</li><li><strong>Fixed assets</strong> (property, plant, equipment) are shown at cost minus accumulated depreciation, called <em>net</em> fixed assets.</li><li><strong>Current liabilities</strong> (accounts payable, accrued expenses, short-term notes) are due within a year. <strong>Long-term debt</strong> is due later.</li><li><strong>Equity</strong> = common stock and paid-in capital plus <strong>retained earnings</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Net working capital (NWC)</strong> = current assets - current liabilities. Harbor has $700,000 of current assets and $450,000 of current liabilities, so NWC = $250,000, the cushion for daily operations.</p><p><strong>Retained earnings roll forward:</strong> ending RE = beginning RE + net income - dividends. If beginning RE is $400,000, net income $330,000 and dividends $120,000, ending RE is $610,000.</p><p><strong>Book vs. market value:</strong> the balance sheet shows <em>book values</em> (mostly historical cost under GAAP). A building bought for $200,000 decades ago may be worth $900,000 today, but the balance sheet still shows the old cost less depreciation. <em>Market value</em> is what an asset or the whole firm would sell for now. For valuation, finance cares about market value, because it reflects the future cash flows investors expect.</p>"},{"h":"4. The statement of cash flows","body":"<p>This statement reconciles net income to the change in cash and sorts every cash movement into three buckets.</p><ol><li><strong>Operating activities:</strong> cash from running the business. Start with net income, add back non-cash expenses like depreciation, then adjust for changes in current assets and liabilities (an increase in receivables or inventory uses cash; an increase in payables provides cash).</li><li><strong>Investing activities:</strong> buying or selling long-term assets (capital expenditures, acquisitions, sale of equipment).</li><li><strong>Financing activities:</strong> raising or repaying debt and equity, and paying dividends.</li></ol><p><strong>Why add back depreciation?</strong> Depreciation spreads the cost of an asset over its life. It reduces reported profit, but no cash leaves the company that year (the cash left when the asset was bought). So you add it back. It still helps cash because it lowers taxable income. This is the <strong>depreciation tax shield</strong> = depreciation x tax rate. With $100,000 of depreciation and a 25% tax rate, the firm saves $25,000 of taxes.</p><p>Quick sorting rules: buying a delivery truck is investing; issuing bonds, repaying a loan or paying dividends is financing; collecting from customers and paying suppliers is operating.</p>"},{"h":"5. Operating cash flow and free cash flow","body":"<p><strong>Operating cash flow (OCF)</strong> measures cash generated by the firm's operations. The most common exam formula:</p><p><code>OCF = EBIT + Depreciation - Taxes</code></p><p>Harbor: 200,000 + 50,000 - 37,800 = <strong>$212,200</strong>. Interest is excluded because it is a financing cost; this is the cash flow from the assets, regardless of how they were funded.</p><p><strong>Free cash flow (FCF)</strong> is the cash left after the firm has paid for the investments needed to keep the business going. It is the amount available to pay lenders and owners:</p><p><code>FCF = OCF - Net capital spending - Change in NWC</code></p><ul><li><strong>Net capital spending</strong> = ending net fixed assets - beginning net fixed assets + depreciation (basically capex).</li><li><strong>Change in NWC</strong> = ending NWC - beginning NWC. If NWC rises, the firm tied up more cash, so it subtracts.</li></ul><p>Harbor: capex $80,000 and NWC up by $15,000, so FCF = 212,200 - 80,000 - 15,000 = <strong>$117,200</strong>.</p><p>That cash must go to the people who funded the firm: <code>FCF (cash flow from assets) = Cash flow to creditors + Cash flow to stockholders</code>, where creditors get interest paid minus net new borrowing, and stockholders get dividends minus net new equity raised.</p>"},{"h":"6. Taxes: marginal vs. average","body":"<p>Tax systems are <em>progressive</em>: higher slices of income are taxed at higher rates. Two ideas matter.</p><ul><li><strong>Average tax rate</strong> = total taxes / taxable income. It is the overall bite.</li><li><strong>Marginal tax rate</strong> = the rate on the <em>next dollar</em> earned. It is the one to use for decisions about new projects.</li></ul><p>Illustration with made-up brackets: 10% on the first $50,000, 20% on the next $50,000 (up to $100,000), and 30% above $100,000. For taxable income of $140,000, tax = 5,000 + 10,000 + 12,000 = $27,000. The average rate is 27,000 / 140,000 = 19.3%, but the marginal rate is 30%. A new project that adds $10,000 of income costs $3,000 in tax, not $1,930.</p><p>The U.S. corporate rate is currently a flat 21%, so for most corporate problems the average and marginal rates are the same. Interest on debt is tax-deductible; dividends are not.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["The balance sheet is a snapshot at one date, while the income statement and the statement of cash flows cover a period of time.","The basic accounting identity is assets equal liabilities plus owners' equity.","Under accrual accounting, revenue is recorded when it is earned and expenses when incurred, not when cash changes hands.","EBIT equals sales minus cost of goods sold, minus operating expenses including depreciation, and it is before interest and taxes.","EBITDA adds depreciation and amortization back to EBIT, and NOPAT equals EBIT times one minus the tax rate.","Depreciation is a non-cash expense, but it lowers taxes, so the depreciation tax shield equals depreciation times the tax rate.","Net working capital equals current assets minus current liabilities.","Ending retained earnings equal beginning retained earnings plus net income minus dividends.","The cash flow statement has three sections: operating, investing, and financing activities.","Operating cash flow equals EBIT plus depreciation minus taxes.","Free cash flow equals operating cash flow minus net capital spending minus the change in net working capital.","Use the marginal tax rate for decisions about new income, and remember that balance sheet book values are historical costs that can differ greatly from market values."],"examples":[{"q":"Ridgeline Outdoors had sales of $2,400,000, cost of goods sold of $1,500,000, SG&A of $300,000, depreciation of $100,000 and interest expense of $60,000. The tax rate is 25%. Compute EBIT, EBITDA, net income, and NOPAT.","steps":["EBIT = 2,400,000 - 1,500,000 - 300,000 - 100,000 = 500,000.","EBITDA = EBIT + depreciation = 500,000 + 100,000 = 600,000.","EBT = EBIT - interest = 500,000 - 60,000 = 440,000. Taxes = 440,000 x 0.25 = 110,000. Net income = 440,000 - 110,000 = 330,000.","NOPAT = EBIT x (1 - T) = 500,000 x 0.75 = 375,000 (ignores interest, so it is larger than net income)."],"answer":"EBIT $500,000; EBITDA $600,000; net income $330,000; NOPAT $375,000."},{"q":"Using Ridgeline's figures above (taxes paid $110,000), also assume capital expenditures of $150,000 and net working capital that rose from $220,000 to $260,000. Find operating cash flow and free cash flow.","steps":["OCF = EBIT + depreciation - taxes = 500,000 + 100,000 - 110,000 = 490,000.","Change in NWC = 260,000 - 220,000 = +40,000 (a use of cash).","FCF = OCF - capital spending - change in NWC = 490,000 - 150,000 - 40,000 = 300,000."],"answer":"OCF = $490,000; FCF = $300,000."},{"q":"Ridgeline began the year with retained earnings of $400,000, earned net income of $330,000, and paid dividends of $120,000. Its common stock and paid-in capital total $190,000, and its total liabilities are $1,000,000. What are ending retained earnings, total equity and total assets?","steps":["Ending RE = beginning RE + net income - dividends = 400,000 + 330,000 - 120,000 = 610,000.","Total equity = common stock and paid-in capital + RE = 190,000 + 610,000 = 800,000.","Assets = liabilities + equity = 1,000,000 + 800,000 = 1,800,000."],"answer":"Retained earnings $610,000; equity $800,000; total assets $1,800,000."}],"videos":[{"title":"Income statement, balance sheet and cash flow statement basics","query":"income statement balance sheet cash flow statement explained for beginners"},{"title":"Free cash flow explained (OCF minus capex minus change in NWC)","query":"free cash flow explained operating cash flow capex net working capital"},{"title":"Marginal vs average tax rate explained","query":"marginal tax rate vs average tax rate explained example"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Accounting identity","b":"Assets = Liabilities + Owners' equity."},{"f":"Balance sheet vs income statement","b":"Balance sheet is a snapshot on one date; income statement covers a period."},{"f":"Accrual accounting","b":"Revenue recorded when earned, expenses when incurred, regardless of cash timing."},{"f":"Gross profit","b":"Sales minus cost of goods sold."},{"f":"EBIT","b":"Earnings before interest and taxes (operating income)."},{"f":"EBITDA","b":"EBIT + depreciation + amortization."},{"f":"NOPAT","b":"EBIT x (1 - tax rate): operating profit after tax, ignoring financing."},{"f":"Why is depreciation added back in cash flow?","b":"It reduces profit but is non-cash; the cash was spent when the asset was bought."},{"f":"Depreciation tax shield","b":"Depreciation x tax rate: taxes saved because depreciation is deductible."},{"f":"Net working capital","b":"Current assets minus current liabilities."},{"f":"Retained earnings roll-forward","b":"Beginning RE + net income - dividends = ending RE."},{"f":"Operating activities in cash flow statement","b":"Cash from day-to-day business: net income adjusted for non-cash items and working capital."},{"f":"Investing activities","b":"Purchase or sale of long-term assets (capex, acquisitions)."},{"f":"Financing activities","b":"Borrowing, repaying debt, issuing stock, paying dividends."},{"f":"Operating cash flow formula","b":"OCF = EBIT + depreciation - taxes."},{"f":"Free cash flow formula","b":"FCF = OCF - net capital spending - change in NWC."},{"f":"Net capital spending","b":"Ending net fixed assets - beginning net fixed assets + depreciation."},{"f":"Cash flow identity","b":"Cash flow from assets = cash flow to creditors + cash flow to stockholders."},{"f":"Cash flow to creditors","b":"Interest paid - net new borrowing."},{"f":"Cash flow to stockholders","b":"Dividends paid - net new equity raised."},{"f":"Average vs marginal tax rate","b":"Average = total tax / taxable income; marginal = rate on the next dollar."},{"f":"Book value vs market value","b":"Book is historical cost on the balance sheet; market is current price investors would pay."}],"pairs":[["EBIT","Operating income before interest and taxes"],["EBITDA","EBIT plus depreciation and amortization"],["NOPAT","EBIT times one minus tax rate"],["Net working capital","Current assets minus current liabilities"],["Retained earnings","Cumulative profit not paid as dividends"],["Free cash flow","OCF less capex less change in NWC"],["Accrual accounting","Records revenue when earned, not collected"],["Marginal tax rate","Tax rate on next dollar earned"]],"traps":["Trap: Net income is the cash the firm generated. Truth: Net income is an accrual figure; depreciation and working capital changes make cash flow different.","Trap: Depreciation is a cash outflow. Truth: It is non-cash; it only helps cash by lowering taxes (tax shield).","Trap: Use the average tax rate for evaluating a new project. Truth: Use the marginal rate, because new income is taxed at the top slice.","Trap: An increase in net working capital adds to free cash flow. Truth: Higher NWC ties up cash, so it is subtracted.","Trap: Balance sheet values show what the firm is worth. Truth: They are mostly historical cost; market value can be far higher or lower."],"mnemonic":"FCF recipe: 'Operating cash, minus what you Build (capex), minus what you Bundle up (NWC increase)'. OCF = EBIT + Dep - Taxes, never interest.","cram":["Assets = liabilities + equity; balance sheet is a snapshot.","EBIT = sales - COGS - operating expenses (including depreciation).","NOPAT = EBIT x (1 - tax rate); EBITDA = EBIT + depreciation.","OCF = EBIT + depreciation - taxes.","FCF = OCF - net capital spending - change in NWC.","Ending RE = beginning RE + net income - dividends.","Depreciation is non-cash; tax shield = depreciation x tax rate.","Decisions use the marginal tax rate; the corporate rate is a flat 21%."],"ytv":[{"title":"Balance Sheet Explained: How to Read One (With Example)","channel":"Debit Decoded","id":"mwiLpUu8NCg","why":"Walks through assets, liabilities and equity with a worked example."},{"title":"Financial Statements Explained | Income Statement, Balance Sheet & Cash Flow | Part 1","channel":"Amquest Education","id":"NevOs9QNleg","why":"Shows how all three statements connect."},{"title":"Statements of Financial Position (Balance Sheets) explained in about 10 mins","channel":"Daniel King","id":"K6OsBN3I_po","why":"Quick refresher on balance sheet layout and terms."}],"questions":[{"q":"Which statement reports a firm's assets, liabilities and equity at a single point in time?","o":["The balance sheet","The income statement","The statement of cash flows","The statement of retained earnings only"],"a":0,"e":"The balance sheet is a snapshot on one date. The income statement and cash flow statement cover a period, which is the common mix-up.","d":1},{"q":"A company has total assets of $500,000 and total liabilities of $320,000. What is owners' equity?","o":["$820,000","$180,000","$320,000","$500,000"],"a":1,"e":"Assets = liabilities + equity, so equity = 500,000 - 320,000 = 180,000. $820,000 wrongly adds the two instead of subtracting.","d":1},{"q":"Sales are $800,000, cost of goods sold is $500,000 and operating expenses (including depreciation) are $120,000. What is EBIT?","o":["$300,000","$680,000","$180,000","$120,000"],"a":2,"e":"EBIT = 800,000 - 500,000 - 120,000 = 180,000. $300,000 is gross profit (it stops before operating expenses).","d":1},{"q":"Under accrual accounting, a company records revenue when","o":["cash is collected from the customer","the customer places an order","the company pays its taxes","it is earned, even if the customer has not yet paid"],"a":3,"e":"Accrual accounting matches revenue to when it is earned. Recording on cash receipt describes cash-basis accounting.","d":1},{"q":"Current assets are $90,000 and current liabilities are $60,000. What is net working capital?","o":["$150,000","$30,000","-$30,000","$60,000"],"a":1,"e":"NWC = current assets - current liabilities = 90,000 - 60,000 = 30,000.","d":1},{"q":"Which statement about depreciation is correct?","o":["It is a cash outflow each year equal to the asset's cost","It is an investing activity","It is a non-cash expense that reduces taxable income","It increases net income"],"a":2,"e":"Depreciation spreads out a past purchase; no cash leaves in the depreciation year, but the deduction reduces taxes.","d":1},{"q":"The statement of cash flows groups cash movements into which three categories?","o":["Revenue, expense and profit","Current, fixed and intangible","Assets, liabilities and equity","Operating, investing and financing activities"],"a":3,"e":"The three sections are operating, investing and financing. Assets/liabilities/equity are the balance sheet categories.","d":1},{"q":"Retained earnings on the balance sheet represent","o":["cumulative net income that has not been paid out as dividends","cash the firm is holding in the bank","the market value of the owners' shares","the amount of profit earned this year only"],"a":0,"e":"Retained earnings is an equity account, not cash. A firm can have large retained earnings and very little cash because earnings were reinvested in assets.","d":1},{"q":"A firm reports sales of $1,200,000, COGS of $700,000, SG&A of $150,000, depreciation of $50,000 and interest expense of $40,000. The tax rate is 25%. What is net income?","o":["$260,000","$225,000","$195,000","$300,000"],"a":2,"e":"EBIT = 1,200,000 - 700,000 - 150,000 - 50,000 = 300,000. EBT = 300,000 - 40,000 = 260,000. Net income = 260,000 x 0.75 = 195,000. $260,000 is EBT, before tax.","d":2},{"q":"A company has EBIT of $300,000, depreciation of $50,000 and amortization of $10,000. What is EBITDA?","o":["$240,000","$350,000","$310,000","$360,000"],"a":3,"e":"EBITDA = EBIT + depreciation + amortization = 300,000 + 50,000 + 10,000 = 360,000. $350,000 forgets amortization.","d":2},{"q":"EBIT is $300,000 and the tax rate is 21%. What is NOPAT?","o":["$237,000","$300,000","$63,000","$279,000"],"a":0,"e":"NOPAT = EBIT x (1 - T) = 300,000 x 0.79 = 237,000. $63,000 is the tax, not the after-tax profit.","d":2},{"q":"EBIT is $300,000, depreciation is $50,000 and taxes paid are $52,500. What is operating cash flow?","o":["$247,500","$297,500","$350,000","$402,500"],"a":1,"e":"OCF = EBIT + depreciation - taxes = 300,000 + 50,000 - 52,500 = 297,500. Subtracting depreciation instead of adding it gives $247,500.","d":2},{"q":"Beginning retained earnings are $250,000, net income is $90,000, and dividends are $35,000. What is ending retained earnings?","o":["$375,000","$340,000","$55,000","$305,000"],"a":3,"e":"Ending RE = 250,000 + 90,000 - 35,000 = 305,000. $340,000 forgets dividends.","d":2},{"q":"A tax schedule taxes the first $50,000 at 10%, the next $50,000 at 20%, and everything above $100,000 at 30%. Taxable income is $120,000. What is the average tax rate?","o":["17.5%","30.0%","20.0%","10.0%"],"a":0,"e":"Tax = 5,000 + 10,000 + 6,000 = 21,000. Average = 21,000 / 120,000 = 17.5%. The 30% figure is the marginal rate.","d":2},{"q":"Using the same tax schedule (10% to $50,000; 20% to $100,000; 30% above) and taxable income of $120,000, how much additional tax is owed on an extra $10,000 of income?","o":["$1,750","$3,000","$2,000","$1,000"],"a":1,"e":"New income is taxed at the marginal rate of 30%, so tax = 10,000 x 0.30 = 3,000. $1,750 uses the average rate, a common error.","d":2},{"q":"Years ago a company bought land for $200,000. It is now worth about $900,000. On the company's GAAP balance sheet the land is shown at","o":["$900,000 (market value)","$1,100,000","about $200,000 (historical cost)","$700,000"],"a":2,"e":"GAAP uses historical cost for most assets, so the book value stays near $200,000. Market value is not recorded, which is why book and market values often differ.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is an investing activity on the statement of cash flows?","o":["Purchasing new manufacturing equipment","Paying a cash dividend","Issuing long-term bonds","Collecting accounts receivable"],"a":0,"e":"Buying long-term assets is investing. Dividends and bond issuance are financing; collecting receivables is operating.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is a financing activity?","o":["Buying a delivery van","Repaying principal on a long-term bank loan","Paying suppliers for inventory","Selling goods to customers on credit"],"a":1,"e":"Debt and equity transactions and dividends are financing. Buying a van is investing.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has $100,000 of depreciation and a 25% tax rate. What is its depreciation tax shield?","o":["$100,000","$75,000","$25,000","$125,000"],"a":2,"e":"Tax shield = depreciation x tax rate = 100,000 x 0.25 = 25,000 of taxes saved. $75,000 is the after-tax depreciation, not the savings.","d":2},{"q":"In December, Harbor Hardware ships $50,000 of goods to a customer on credit; the customer pays in January. Under accrual accounting, what is the effect on December's results?","o":["December revenue is unchanged and cash rises by $50,000","December revenue and cash both rise by $50,000","Revenue is recorded in January when cash is collected","December revenue rises by $50,000 and receivables rise, but December cash does not change"],"a":3,"e":"Accrual accounting recognizes revenue when earned (shipment), so the sale appears in December even though no cash arrives until January.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's net working capital rose from $180,000 to $205,000 this year. How does this change affect free cash flow?","o":["It increases FCF by $25,000","It reduces FCF by $25,000","It has no effect on FCF","It reduces FCF by $205,000"],"a":1,"e":"An increase in NWC means more cash tied up in receivables and inventory, so it is subtracted from OCF: change in NWC = +25,000.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has operating cash flow of $420,000 and capital spending of $130,000. Its net working capital grew from $300,000 to $340,000. What is free cash flow?","o":["$290,000","$550,000","$250,000","$330,000"],"a":2,"e":"FCF = 420,000 - 130,000 - 40,000 = 250,000. $290,000 forgets the NWC increase; $550,000 adds capex instead of subtracting it.","d":3},{"q":"Cask & Co. has sales of $900,000, cash costs (excluding depreciation) of $540,000, depreciation of $110,000, and a 30% tax rate. It has no debt. What is operating cash flow?","o":["$250,000","$360,000","$252,000","$285,000"],"a":3,"e":"EBIT = 900,000 - 540,000 - 110,000 = 250,000; taxes = 75,000; OCF = 250,000 + 110,000 - 75,000 = 285,000. $250,000 is EBIT; $252,000 is the after-tax cash margin without the depreciation tax shield.","d":3},{"q":"Net fixed assets rose from $800,000 to $900,000 during the year, and depreciation expense was $120,000. What was net capital spending?","o":["$220,000","$100,000","$20,000","$1,020,000"],"a":0,"e":"Net capital spending = ending NFA - beginning NFA + depreciation = 100,000 + 120,000 = 220,000. $100,000 forgets that depreciation reduced the asset base.","d":3},{"q":"A firm's cash flow from assets (FCF) is $300,000. During the year it paid $40,000 of interest and borrowed a net $25,000 of new debt. What was the cash flow to stockholders?","o":["$315,000","$260,000","$285,000","$15,000"],"a":2,"e":"CF to creditors = interest - net new borrowing = 40,000 - 25,000 = 15,000. CF to stockholders = 300,000 - 15,000 = 285,000.","d":3},{"q":"Net income is $150,000 and depreciation is $40,000. Accounts receivable rose $30,000, inventory fell $10,000, and accounts payable rose $5,000. What is cash flow from operating activities?","o":["$150,000","$195,000","$215,000","$175,000"],"a":3,"e":"CFO = 150 + 40 - 30 (receivables up uses cash) + 10 (inventory down provides cash) + 5 (payables up provides cash) = 175 thousand.","d":3},{"q":"Using the schedule 10% on the first $50,000, 20% on the next $50,000, and 30% above $100,000, what is the average tax rate on taxable income of $150,000?","o":["20.0%","30.0%","15.0%","25.0%"],"a":0,"e":"Tax = 5,000 + 10,000 + 15,000 = 30,000. Average = 30,000 / 150,000 = 20.0%. The marginal rate is 30%.","d":3},{"q":"A firm is in the 30% marginal bracket. A new project would add $40,000 of taxable income. What is the project's after-tax income?","o":["$12,000","$28,000","$40,000","$32,000"],"a":1,"e":"Marginal tax on the new income = 40,000 x 0.30 = 12,000, so after-tax income = 28,000. $12,000 is the tax, not what is kept.","d":3},{"q":"A company has book equity of $500,000 and 100,000 shares that trade at $12. What is its market-to-book ratio?","o":["0.42","1.2","5.0","2.4"],"a":3,"e":"Market value of equity = 100,000 x 12 = 1,200,000. Market-to-book = 1,200,000 / 500,000 = 2.4. 0.42 inverts the ratio.","d":3},{"q":"Firm A and Firm B have identical operations with EBIT of $400,000 and a 25% tax rate. Firm A has no debt. Firm B pays $100,000 of interest. What is Firm B's NOPAT?","o":["$300,000","$225,000","$400,000","$75,000"],"a":0,"e":"NOPAT = EBIT x (1 - T) = 400,000 x 0.75 = 300,000, the same for both, because NOPAT ignores financing. $225,000 is Firm B's net income.","d":3}]},{"id":"u3","title":"Ratio Analysis","day":2,"summary":"Turn financial statements into liquidity, efficiency, leverage, profitability and market ratios, link them with the DuPont identity, and know the limits of ratio analysis.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Why ratios, and the Cedar Outfitters data","body":"<p>A raw number like 'net income of $175,000' means little by itself. Is that good for a company with $2 million of sales? $200 million? <strong>Ratios</strong> divide one number by another so firms of different sizes, or the same firm in different years, can be compared. Analysts do two kinds of comparison: <strong>trend analysis</strong> (this company over time) and <strong>benchmarking</strong> (this company against industry peers).</p><p>Every example below uses Cedar Outfitters, a camping-gear retailer:</p><ul><li>Sales $2,000,000; cost of goods sold $1,200,000; operating expenses (including depreciation) $500,000, so EBIT = $300,000</li><li>Interest $50,000; taxes $75,000; <strong>net income $175,000</strong></li><li>Cash $100,000; receivables $250,000; inventory $300,000; <strong>current assets $650,000</strong>; net fixed assets $850,000; <strong>total assets $1,500,000</strong></li><li><strong>Current liabilities $325,000</strong>; long-term debt $575,000 (total liabilities $900,000); <strong>equity $600,000</strong></li><li>100,000 shares outstanding; stock price $21; dividends paid $70,000</li></ul><p>Ratio families to know: liquidity, efficiency (asset management), leverage (debt), profitability, and market value.</p>"},{"h":"2. Liquidity and efficiency ratios","body":"<p><strong>Liquidity ratios</strong> ask: can the firm pay its short-term bills?</p><ul><li><strong>Current ratio</strong> = current assets / current liabilities = 650,000 / 325,000 = <strong>2.0</strong>. Cedar has $2 of current assets for every $1 due within a year.</li><li><strong>Quick (acid-test) ratio</strong> = (current assets - inventory) / current liabilities = (650,000 - 300,000) / 325,000 = <strong>1.08</strong>. Inventory is removed because it is the hardest current asset to turn into cash quickly.</li></ul><p><strong>Efficiency (asset management) ratios</strong> ask: how hard do the assets work?</p><ul><li><strong>Inventory turnover</strong> = COGS / inventory = 1,200,000 / 300,000 = <strong>4.0 times</strong> per year. Higher is usually better (but too high can mean stockouts).</li><li><strong>Days in inventory (DIO)</strong> = 365 / inventory turnover = 365 / 4 = 91.25 days.</li><li><strong>Days sales outstanding (DSO)</strong> = receivables / (sales / 365) = 250,000 / (2,000,000 / 365) = <strong>45.6 days</strong>. It is the average time to collect from customers; compare to the firm's credit terms (if terms are net 30, 45.6 days signals slow collection).</li><li><strong>Total asset turnover</strong> = sales / total assets = 2,000,000 / 1,500,000 = <strong>1.33</strong>. Each $1 of assets generates $1.33 of sales.</li></ul>"},{"h":"3. Leverage (debt) ratios","body":"<p><strong>Leverage</strong> means using borrowed money. It can boost returns to owners when things go well, but increases risk because interest must be paid no matter what.</p><ul><li><strong>Debt ratio</strong> (total debt ratio) = total liabilities / total assets = 900,000 / 1,500,000 = <strong>60%</strong>. Equivalent: (total assets - total equity) / total assets.</li><li><strong>Debt-to-equity</strong> = total liabilities / equity = 900,000 / 600,000 = <strong>1.5</strong>.</li><li><strong>Equity multiplier</strong> = total assets / equity = 1,500,000 / 600,000 = <strong>2.5</strong>. It also equals 1 + debt-to-equity and 1 / (1 - debt ratio). Cedar holds $2.50 of assets for each $1 of owners' equity.</li><li><strong>Times interest earned (TIE)</strong> = EBIT / interest = 300,000 / 50,000 = <strong>6.0 times</strong>. EBIT could fall by five-sixths before Cedar could not cover interest.</li></ul><p>Higher debt ratio or lower TIE means greater financial risk. Always say which definition of 'debt' a problem uses; on exams, unless told otherwise, 'total debt' means total liabilities.</p>"},{"h":"4. Profitability and market ratios","body":"<p><strong>Profit margins</strong> (as a percent of sales):</p><ul><li><strong>Gross margin</strong> = (sales - COGS) / sales = 800,000 / 2,000,000 = <strong>40%</strong>.</li><li><strong>Operating margin</strong> = EBIT / sales = 300,000 / 2,000,000 = <strong>15%</strong>.</li><li><strong>Net profit margin</strong> = net income / sales = 175,000 / 2,000,000 = <strong>8.75%</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Returns:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ROA</strong> (return on assets) = net income / total assets = 175,000 / 1,500,000 = <strong>11.67%</strong>.</li><li><strong>ROE</strong> (return on equity) = net income / total equity = 175,000 / 600,000 = <strong>29.17%</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Market value ratios</strong> connect the statements to the stock price:</p><ul><li><strong>EPS</strong> (earnings per share) = net income / shares = 175,000 / 100,000 = $1.75.</li><li><strong>P/E ratio</strong> = price / EPS = 21 / 1.75 = <strong>12.0</strong>. Investors pay $12 for each $1 of current earnings.</li><li><strong>Book value per share</strong> = equity / shares = $6.00; <strong>market-to-book</strong> = 21 / 6 = <strong>3.5</strong>.</li><li><strong>Dividend per share</strong> = 70,000 / 100,000 = $0.70; <strong>dividend yield</strong> = DPS / price = 0.70 / 21 = <strong>3.33%</strong>.</li><li><strong>Payout ratio</strong> = dividends / net income = 70,000 / 175,000 = <strong>40%</strong>; the <strong>retention ratio</strong> is the other 60%.</li></ul>"},{"h":"5. The DuPont identity","body":"<p>Why is Cedar's ROE (29.2%) so much higher than its ROA (11.7%)? The <strong>DuPont identity</strong> breaks ROE into three drivers:</p><p><code>ROE = Net profit margin x Total asset turnover x Equity multiplier</code></p><p>Cedar: 8.75% x 1.3333 x 2.5 = <strong>29.17%</strong>. It matches 175,000 / 600,000, which proves the identity.</p><ul><li><strong>Profit margin</strong> = operating efficiency (how much is kept from each sales dollar).</li><li><strong>Asset turnover</strong> = asset use efficiency (sales per dollar of assets).</li><li><strong>Equity multiplier</strong> = financial leverage.</li></ul><p>Use it as a diagnostic. If a competitor has the same ROE but a lower multiplier, it earns that return with less risk. If Cedar's ROE dropped, DuPont shows whether margin, turnover, or leverage caused it. Also note ROA = margin x asset turnover (the first two pieces).</p><p><strong>What-if:</strong> hold margin and turnover the same but cut leverage to an equity multiplier of 1.0 (no debt), and ROE falls to 8.75% x 1.3333 = 11.67%, which is just ROA. Leverage magnifies returns, for better or worse.</p>"},{"h":"6. Trend, common-size analysis and limits of ratios","body":"<p><strong>Common-size statements</strong> express every line as a percent of a base: income statement items as a percent of <em>sales</em>, balance sheet items as a percent of <em>total assets</em>. Cedar's COGS is 60% of sales (1.2M / 2.0M), operating expenses are 25%, so its operating margin is 15%. Common-size analysis lets you compare firms of different size and spot shifts. If COGS rose from 55% to 60% of sales over two years, gross margin slipped from 45% to 40% and you should ask why (supplier prices? discounting?).</p><p><strong>Trend (time-series) analysis</strong> follows a ratio over several years. A DSO that creeps from 35 to 48 days signals deteriorating collections even if each year looks fine on its own.</p><p><strong>Limits of ratio analysis:</strong></p><ul><li>No single 'right' value: a good ratio depends on industry (a grocery chain turns inventory fast; a jeweler does not).</li><li>Different accounting methods (FIFO vs. LIFO, depreciation methods) distort comparisons.</li><li>Balance sheet figures are historical book values and are only a snapshot, so seasonality (a retailer's inventory in January vs. October) can mislead.</li><li>Window dressing, inflation, and conglomerates with several industries make benchmarks fuzzy.</li><li>Ratios point to questions; they do not provide answers.</li></ul>"}],"keyPoints":["Ratios let us compare firms of different sizes and track one firm over time using trend analysis and industry benchmarks.","The current ratio equals current assets divided by current liabilities, and the quick ratio removes inventory from current assets.","Inventory turnover equals cost of goods sold divided by inventory, and days in inventory equals 365 divided by the turnover.","Days sales outstanding equals receivables divided by sales per day, and it measures how fast customers pay.","Total asset turnover equals sales divided by total assets and shows how efficiently assets create sales.","The debt ratio equals total liabilities divided by total assets, and the equity multiplier equals total assets divided by equity.","Times interest earned equals EBIT divided by interest expense and shows how well earnings cover interest payments.","Gross margin, operating margin and net profit margin are each a profit measure divided by sales.","Return on assets equals net income divided by total assets, and return on equity equals net income divided by equity.","Earnings per share is net income divided by shares, the price-to-earnings ratio is price divided by earnings per share, and market-to-book is price divided by book value per share.","The payout ratio is dividends divided by net income, and the dividend yield is dividends per share divided by the stock price.","The DuPont identity says return on equity equals net profit margin times total asset turnover times the equity multiplier."],"examples":[{"q":"Cedar Outfitters has current assets of $650,000 (including $300,000 of inventory and $250,000 of receivables), current liabilities of $325,000, sales of $2,000,000 and COGS of $1,200,000. Compute the current ratio, quick ratio, inventory turnover, days in inventory, and DSO.","steps":["Current ratio = 650,000 / 325,000 = 2.0.","Quick ratio = (650,000 - 300,000) / 325,000 = 350,000 / 325,000 = 1.08.","Inventory turnover = COGS / inventory = 1,200,000 / 300,000 = 4.0 times.","Days in inventory = 365 / 4 = 91.25 days.","DSO = receivables / (sales / 365) = 250,000 / (2,000,000 / 365) = 250,000 / 5,479.45 = 45.6 days."],"answer":"Current 2.0; quick 1.08; inventory turnover 4.0 (91.25 days); DSO 45.6 days."},{"q":"Cedar's total assets are $1,500,000, total liabilities $900,000, equity $600,000, EBIT $300,000, interest $50,000, net income $175,000 and sales $2,000,000. Find the debt ratio, debt-to-equity, equity multiplier, times interest earned, net profit margin, ROA and ROE.","steps":["Debt ratio = 900,000 / 1,500,000 = 60%.","Debt-to-equity = 900,000 / 600,000 = 1.5.","Equity multiplier = 1,500,000 / 600,000 = 2.5 (also 1 + 1.5).","TIE = 300,000 / 50,000 = 6.0 times.","Net margin = 175,000 / 2,000,000 = 8.75%.","ROA = 175,000 / 1,500,000 = 11.67%; ROE = 175,000 / 600,000 = 29.17%."],"answer":"Debt ratio 60%; D/E 1.5; EM 2.5; TIE 6.0; margin 8.75%; ROA 11.67%; ROE 29.17%."},{"q":"Use Cedar's numbers (net income $175,000; 100,000 shares; price $21; equity $600,000; dividends $70,000; total asset turnover 1.3333). Verify ROE with the DuPont identity and compute EPS, P/E, market-to-book, dividend yield and payout ratio.","steps":["DuPont: ROE = 8.75% x 1.3333 x 2.5 = 29.17%, matching 175,000 / 600,000.","EPS = 175,000 / 100,000 = $1.75; P/E = 21 / 1.75 = 12.0.","Book value per share = 600,000 / 100,000 = $6.00; market-to-book = 21 / 6 = 3.5.","DPS = 70,000 / 100,000 = $0.70; dividend yield = 0.70 / 21 = 3.33%.","Payout ratio = 70,000 / 175,000 = 40%; retention ratio = 60%."],"answer":"ROE 29.17%; EPS $1.75; P/E 12.0; M/B 3.5; dividend yield 3.33%; payout 40%."}],"videos":[{"title":"DuPont analysis explained (3-step ROE)","query":"DuPont analysis ROE net profit margin asset turnover equity multiplier explained"},{"title":"Liquidity, leverage and efficiency ratios for beginners","query":"financial ratio analysis current ratio quick ratio inventory turnover debt ratio explained"},{"title":"P/E, market-to-book and dividend payout ratios","query":"P/E ratio market to book ratio dividend payout ratio explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Current ratio","b":"Current assets / current liabilities."},{"f":"Quick (acid-test) ratio","b":"(Current assets - inventory) / current liabilities."},{"f":"Inventory turnover","b":"COGS / inventory."},{"f":"Days in inventory (DIO)","b":"365 / inventory turnover."},{"f":"Days sales outstanding (DSO)","b":"Receivables / (sales / 365); average collection period."},{"f":"Total asset turnover","b":"Sales / total assets."},{"f":"Debt ratio","b":"Total liabilities / total assets."},{"f":"Debt-to-equity","b":"Total liabilities / total equity."},{"f":"Equity multiplier","b":"Total assets / equity = 1 + D/E = 1 / (1 - debt ratio)."},{"f":"Times interest earned","b":"EBIT / interest expense."},{"f":"Gross profit margin","b":"(Sales - COGS) / sales."},{"f":"Operating margin","b":"EBIT / sales."},{"f":"Net profit margin","b":"Net income / sales."},{"f":"ROA","b":"Net income / total assets."},{"f":"ROE","b":"Net income / total equity."},{"f":"Earnings per share (EPS)","b":"Net income / shares outstanding."},{"f":"P/E ratio","b":"Stock price / EPS."},{"f":"Market-to-book ratio","b":"Price per share / book value per share."},{"f":"Dividend yield","b":"Dividend per share / stock price."},{"f":"Payout ratio","b":"Dividends / net income; retention ratio = 1 - payout."},{"f":"DuPont identity","b":"ROE = net margin x total asset turnover x equity multiplier."},{"f":"Common-size statements","b":"Income items as % of sales; balance sheet items as % of total assets."}],"pairs":[["Current ratio","Current assets divided by current liabilities"],["Quick ratio","Liquidity measure excluding inventory"],["DSO","Average days to collect receivables"],["Equity multiplier","Total assets divided by equity"],["Times interest earned","EBIT divided by interest expense"],["ROE","Net income divided by equity"],["Payout ratio","Share of earnings paid as dividends"],["DuPont identity","Margin times turnover times multiplier"]],"traps":["Trap: A higher current ratio is always better. Truth: Too much idle cash, inventory or receivables hurts profitability; ratios are judged against industry norms.","Trap: Inventory turnover is sales divided by inventory. Truth: The textbook version uses COGS over inventory; DSO is the one that uses sales (per day).","Trap: Debt ratio and debt-to-equity are the same number. Truth: Debt ratio is debt / assets (60%); debt-to-equity is debt / equity (1.5). Equity multiplier = 1 + D/E.","Trap: Paying a current liability with cash always lowers the current ratio. Truth: If the ratio is above 1, paying down current liabilities with cash raises it.","Trap: A high ROE always means a strong firm. Truth: DuPont may show it is just heavy leverage (a high equity multiplier), which means high risk."],"mnemonic":"DuPont: 'Make it, Move it, Multiply it'. Margin (profit per sale) x Turnover (sales per asset) x Multiplier (assets per equity) = ROE.","cram":["Current ratio=CA/CL; quick ratio removes inventory first.","Inventory turnover=COGS/inventory; DSO=receivables/(sales/365).","Debt ratio=liabilities/assets; equity multiplier=assets/equity.","TIE=EBIT/interest; ROA=NI/assets; ROE=NI/equity.","P/E=price/EPS; market-to-book=price/book value per share.","Payout=dividends/NI; dividend yield=DPS/price.","DuPont: ROE=net margin x asset turnover x equity multiplier.","Ratio limits: accounting methods, seasonality, industry differences."],"ytv":[{"title":"DuPont Analysis Explained: The 3-Factor ROE Formula","channel":"Ryan O'Connell, CFA, FRM","id":"0-9fGLgl20k","why":"Breaks ROE into margin, turnover and leverage with a worked example."},{"title":"DuPont analysis explained","channel":"The Finance Storyteller","id":"bhbDDSohJ84","why":"A short, intuitive second take on the DuPont identity."},{"title":"ROE DuPont Analysis - How to Use the DuPont Equation to Analyze a Stock","channel":"Learn to Invest - Investors Grow","id":"qRBHfpyTDU8","why":"Applies DuPont to compare real companies."}],"questions":[{"q":"A firm has current assets of $480,000 and current liabilities of $240,000. What is its current ratio?","o":["2.0","0.5","1.0","240,000"],"a":0,"e":"Current ratio = 480,000 / 240,000 = 2.0. 0.5 inverts the ratio.","d":1},{"q":"The quick ratio differs from the current ratio because it excludes","o":["accounts receivable","inventory","cash","accounts payable"],"a":1,"e":"Inventory is the least liquid current asset, so the quick ratio uses (current assets - inventory) / current liabilities.","d":1},{"q":"Net income is $45,000 and sales are $600,000. What is the net profit margin?","o":["13.3%","75%","7.5%","0.75%"],"a":2,"e":"Net margin = 45,000 / 600,000 = 7.5%. 13.3% flips the fraction (sales / net income).","d":1},{"q":"Net income is $90,000 and total assets are $1,000,000. What is ROA?","o":["11.1%","90%","0.9%","9.0%"],"a":3,"e":"ROA = net income / total assets = 90,000 / 1,000,000 = 9.0%. 11.1% inverts the ratio.","d":1},{"q":"A company earned $500,000 of net income and has 250,000 shares outstanding. What is EPS?","o":["$0.50","$2.00","$125.00","$250,000"],"a":1,"e":"EPS = net income / shares = 500,000 / 250,000 = $2.00.","d":1},{"q":"A stock trades at $30 and EPS is $2.00. What is the P/E ratio?","o":["0.067","60.0","15.0","28.0"],"a":2,"e":"P/E = price / EPS = 30 / 2 = 15. 60 multiplies instead of divides.","d":1},{"q":"Which ratio is a measure of financial leverage?","o":["Inventory turnover","Gross margin","Dividend yield","Debt ratio"],"a":3,"e":"The debt ratio (total debt / total assets) shows how much of the firm is financed with borrowed money. Turnover and margin are efficiency and profitability ratios.","d":1},{"q":"A firm's inventory turnover rises from 4 to 6 while sales stay constant. What does this generally indicate?","o":["Inventory is being sold more quickly relative to its level","The firm is more highly leveraged","Receivables are collected more slowly","Gross margin must have increased"],"a":0,"e":"Higher turnover means inventory moves faster. It says nothing about debt, receivables, or margins.","d":1},{"q":"COGS is $900,000 and average inventory is $150,000. Using 365 days, approximately how many days does inventory sit before being sold?","o":["6 days","150 days","61 days","24 days"],"a":2,"e":"Turnover = 900,000 / 150,000 = 6.0. Days in inventory = 365 / 6 = 60.8, about 61. '6' is the turnover itself, not days.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has receivables of $120,000 and annual credit sales of $1,095,000. Using a 365-day year, what is DSO?","o":["9.1 days","91 days","120 days","40 days"],"a":3,"e":"Sales per day = 1,095,000 / 365 = 3,000. DSO = 120,000 / 3,000 = 40 days.","d":2},{"q":"Sales are $3,000,000 and total assets are $1,500,000. What is total asset turnover?","o":["2.0","0.5","4,500,000","1.5"],"a":0,"e":"TAT = sales / total assets = 3,000,000 / 1,500,000 = 2.0. 0.5 inverts the fraction.","d":2},{"q":"Total assets are $2,000,000 and owners' equity is $800,000. What is the debt ratio?","o":["40%","60%","150%","2.5"],"a":1,"e":"Debt = 2,000,000 - 800,000 = 1,200,000; debt ratio = 1,200,000 / 2,000,000 = 60%. 40% is the equity ratio.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has a debt ratio of 60%. What is its equity multiplier?","o":["1.5","0.6","1.67","2.5"],"a":3,"e":"EM = 1 / (1 - debt ratio) = 1 / 0.40 = 2.5. 1.67 is 1 / 0.6, using the debt ratio instead of the equity share.","d":2},{"q":"EBIT is $360,000 and interest expense is $90,000. What is the times interest earned ratio?","o":["4.0","0.25","3.0","270,000"],"a":0,"e":"TIE = EBIT / interest = 360,000 / 90,000 = 4.0. Putting EBT (EBIT minus interest) in the numerator wrongly gives 3.0.","d":2},{"q":"Net income is $120,000 and total equity is $800,000. What is ROE?","o":["6.7%","15.0%","10.0%","150%"],"a":1,"e":"ROE = 120,000 / 800,000 = 15%. 6.7% inverts the ratio.","d":2},{"q":"Sales are $500,000 and COGS is $320,000. What is the gross profit margin?","o":["64%","56%","36%","180%"],"a":2,"e":"Gross margin = (500,000 - 320,000) / 500,000 = 180,000 / 500,000 = 36%. 64% is COGS as a percent of sales.","d":2},{"q":"A firm earned net income of $160,000 and paid dividends of $40,000. What are its payout ratio and retention ratio?","o":["25% payout; 75% retention","75% payout; 25% retention","40% payout; 60% retention","25% payout; 25% retention"],"a":0,"e":"Payout = 40,000 / 160,000 = 25%, retention = 1 - 0.25 = 75%.","d":2},{"q":"A stock sells for $40 and pays an annual dividend of $1.20 per share. What is the dividend yield?","o":["33.3%","3.0%","4.8%","0.03%"],"a":1,"e":"Yield = DPS / price = 1.20 / 40 = 3.0%. 33.3% inverts the fraction.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has equity of $3,000,000 and 200,000 shares outstanding, and its stock trades at $45. What is the market-to-book ratio?","o":["0.33","15.0","3.0","2.0"],"a":2,"e":"Book value per share = 3,000,000 / 200,000 = $15. Market-to-book = 45 / 15 = 3.0.","d":2},{"q":"In a common-size income statement, cost of goods sold rose from 55% of sales to 60% of sales over two years. What does this imply?","o":["Gross margin rose from 45% to 40%","Operating expenses decreased","Sales decreased by 5%","Gross margin fell from 45% to 40%"],"a":3,"e":"Gross margin = 100% minus COGS%, so it dropped by 5 points. Common-size percentages do not show whether dollar sales changed.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is a limitation of ratio analysis?","o":["Ratios can only be calculated for companies with no debt","Different accounting methods and seasonality can make comparisons misleading","Ratios cannot be used to compare companies of different size","Ratios eliminate the effect of industry differences"],"a":1,"e":"Accounting differences, seasonality and industry mix distort ratios. Comparing different-size firms is actually a strength of ratios.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has a net profit margin of 6%, total asset turnover of 1.8, and an equity multiplier of 2.5. What is its ROE?","o":["10.8%","15.0%","27.0%","4.3%"],"a":2,"e":"DuPont: ROE = 0.06 x 1.8 x 2.5 = 0.27 = 27%. 10.8% omits leverage (that is ROA).","d":3},{"q":"A firm has ROE of 18%, net profit margin of 4.5%, and total asset turnover of 2.0. What is its equity multiplier?","o":["1.6","4.0","9.0","2.0"],"a":3,"e":"EM = ROE / (margin x turnover) = 0.18 / (0.045 x 2.0) = 0.18 / 0.09 = 2.0.","d":3},{"q":"Davis Co. has net income of $100,000, sales of $1,000,000, total assets of $800,000, and equity of $400,000. If it replaced all debt with equity (equity multiplier 1.0) while margin and turnover stayed the same, its ROE would be","o":["12.5%","25.0%","10.0%","8.0%"],"a":0,"e":"Current ROE = 25% (margin 10% x TAT 1.25 x EM 2.0). With EM = 1.0, ROE = ROA = 10% x 1.25 = 12.5%.","d":3},{"q":"A firm has current assets of $800,000 and current liabilities of $400,000 (current ratio 2.0). It uses $100,000 of cash to pay off accounts payable. What is the new current ratio?","o":["2.0","1.75","2.33","1.5"],"a":2,"e":"New CA = 700,000; new CL = 300,000; ratio = 2.33. When the ratio is above 1, using cash to cut current liabilities raises it.","d":3},{"q":"A firm has cash of $200,000, receivables of $150,000, inventory of $250,000, and current liabilities of $300,000. It spends $100,000 of cash to buy more inventory. What is the new quick ratio?","o":["1.17","1.00","1.50","0.83"],"a":3,"e":"Original quick = 350,000 / 300,000 = 1.17. After the purchase, cash falls to 100,000 and inventory is excluded, so quick = (100,000 + 150,000) / 300,000 = 0.83.","d":3},{"q":"A stock trades at $21. The firm has net income of $175,000, equity of $600,000, and 100,000 shares. What is its market-to-book ratio, and what is its P/E?","o":["Market-to-book 3.5; P/E 12.0","Market-to-book 12.0; P/E 3.5","Market-to-book 3.5; P/E 3.5","Market-to-book 0.29; P/E 12.0"],"a":0,"e":"BVPS = 600,000 / 100,000 = $6, so M/B = 21 / 6 = 3.5. EPS = 1.75, so P/E = 21 / 1.75 = 12.0.","d":3},{"q":"A firm has sales of $5,000,000, net income of $250,000, total assets of $2,500,000, and a debt ratio of 0.60. What is its ROE?","o":["10.0%","25.0%","15.0%","6.25%"],"a":1,"e":"Equity = 2,500,000 x 0.40 = 1,000,000. ROE = 250,000 / 1,000,000 = 25%. DuPont check: 5% x 2.0 x 2.5 = 25%. 10% is ROA.","d":3},{"q":"Alder Co.'s DSO is 52 days, while the industry average is 38 days. Its average daily sales are $20,000. If Alder collected as quickly as the industry, how much less cash would be tied up in receivables?","o":["$760,000","$1,040,000","$14,000","$280,000"],"a":3,"e":"Excess days = 52 - 38 = 14. Cash tied up = 14 x 20,000 = 280,000. $1,040,000 is its entire receivables balance (52 x 20,000).","d":3},{"q":"A firm's EBIT is $450,000 and interest expense is $150,000, so TIE is 3.0. If EBIT falls by 40% and interest stays the same, the new times interest earned ratio is","o":["1.8","1.2","2.0","0.6"],"a":0,"e":"New EBIT = 450,000 x 0.60 = 270,000. TIE = 270,000 / 150,000 = 1.8. A 40% fall in EBIT cuts TIE by 40% from 3.0 to 1.8.","d":3}]},{"id":"u4","title":"Financial Planning & Working Capital","day":2,"summary":"Forecast funding needs with budgets and the percent-of-sales method, measure growth capacity, and manage the cash conversion cycle, credit terms and short-term financing.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Budgets and pro forma statements","body":"<p>Financial planning answers: <em>where do we want to be, and how much money will it take?</em> A plan turns sales goals into numbers through <strong>budgets</strong> and <strong>pro forma</strong> (projected) financial statements.</p><ul><li>The <strong>sales forecast</strong> is the starting point; everything else flows from it.</li><li>The <strong>operating budget</strong> turns sales into production, purchases and expenses.</li><li>The <strong>cash budget</strong> shows month-by-month cash in and cash out, so the firm knows when it will be short (and must borrow) or have a surplus (and can invest).</li><li><strong>Pro forma income statement and balance sheet</strong> show what the financials would look like at the end of the plan.</li></ul><p><strong>Cash budget mini-example:</strong> Bluebird Toys starts the month with $20,000 of cash, expects $150,000 of collections, and plans $160,000 of payments. Ending cash before financing = 20,000 + 150,000 - 160,000 = $10,000. Its policy is a minimum balance of $25,000, so it must borrow $15,000 on its line of credit.</p><p>Planning also tests scenarios (best case, worst case) so management can see how sensitive funding needs are to sales.</p>"},{"h":"2. The percent-of-sales method and AFN","body":"<p>The <strong>percent-of-sales</strong> method assumes many balance sheet and income statement items rise in direct proportion to sales. Items that do are <em>spontaneous</em>: assets such as cash, receivables, inventory (and fixed assets if running at full capacity), and spontaneous liabilities such as accounts payable and accruals. Items that do not change automatically are notes payable, long-term debt, and common stock; they only change when management decides to raise or repay money.</p><p>The gap between assets needed and financing available is the <strong>Additional Funds Needed (AFN)</strong>:</p><p><code>AFN = (A*/S0) x dS - (L*/S0) x dS - M x S1 x (1 - payout)</code></p><ul><li>A*/S0 = spontaneous assets as a fraction of current sales (capital intensity); L*/S0 = spontaneous liabilities as a fraction of current sales</li><li>dS = increase in sales, S1 = new sales, M = profit margin, (1 - payout) = retention ratio</li></ul><p><strong>Maple Lane Furniture:</strong> sales grow from $1,000,000 to $1,200,000. Assets of $800,000 and spontaneous liabilities of $150,000 vary with sales; notes payable ($100,000), long-term debt ($250,000) and equity ($300,000) do not. Margin is 5% and payout is 40%.</p><ul><li>New required assets grow by 0.80 x 200,000 = $160,000</li><li>Spontaneous liabilities supply 0.15 x 200,000 = $30,000</li><li>New net income = 0.05 x 1,200,000 = $60,000; retained = 60,000 x 0.60 = $36,000</li><li>AFN = 160,000 - 30,000 - 36,000 = <strong>$94,000</strong></li></ul><p>Check with the balance sheet: required assets $960,000; financing available = liabilities 180,000 + 100,000 + 250,000 = 530,000 plus equity 336,000 = $866,000. The shortfall is 960,000 - 866,000 = $94,000. If AFN is <em>negative</em>, the firm has a surplus. Faster growth, higher capital intensity, lower margin, and higher payout all increase AFN.</p>"},{"h":"3. Internal and sustainable growth","body":"<p>How fast can a firm grow without outside money? Two benchmark rates answer this. Let <strong>b</strong> = the retention ratio (1 - payout).</p><ul><li><strong>Internal growth rate (IGR)</strong>: the maximum growth with <em>no external financing at all</em> (no new debt or equity). <code>IGR = (ROA x b) / (1 - ROA x b)</code></li><li><strong>Sustainable growth rate (SGR)</strong>: the maximum growth with <em>no new equity</em> and a <em>constant debt-to-equity ratio</em> (it borrows as equity grows). <code>SGR = (ROE x b) / (1 - ROE x b)</code></li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> Maple Lane has net income $60,000, total assets $800,000, equity $300,000, and retains 60%. ROA = 7.5%, so ROA x b = 4.5% and IGR = 0.045 / 0.955 = <strong>4.71%</strong>. ROE = 20%, so ROE x b = 12% and SGR = 0.12 / 0.88 = <strong>13.64%</strong>. The SGR is higher because it allows borrowing alongside retained earnings.</p><p>To grow faster than SGR a firm must raise margins, turn assets faster, retain more earnings, take on more debt, or sell new stock. SGR ties back to DuPont, since ROE = margin x turnover x multiplier.</p>"},{"h":"4. The cash conversion cycle","body":"<p>Working capital is a cycle: buy inventory, sell it on credit, collect cash, pay suppliers. The <strong>cash conversion cycle (CCC)</strong> measures how many days cash is tied up:</p><p><code>CCC = DIO + DSO - DPO</code></p><ul><li><strong>DIO</strong> (days inventory outstanding) = inventory / (COGS / 365)</li><li><strong>DSO</strong> (days sales outstanding) = receivables / (sales / 365)</li><li><strong>DPO</strong> (days payables outstanding) = accounts payable / (COGS / 365)</li><li>DIO + DSO is the <strong>operating cycle</strong>: purchase of inventory to collection of cash.</li></ul><p><strong>Worked example (Bluebird Toys):</strong> COGS $3,650,000 per year ($10,000 per day), sales $5,475,000 ($15,000 per day), inventory $500,000, receivables $600,000, payables $300,000.</p><ul><li>DIO = 500,000 / 10,000 = 50 days</li><li>DSO = 600,000 / 15,000 = 40 days</li><li>DPO = 300,000 / 10,000 = 30 days</li><li>Operating cycle = 50 + 40 = 90 days; <strong>CCC = 90 - 30 = 60 days</strong></li></ul><p>Bluebird pays suppliers 30 days after getting goods, but waits 90 days to be paid by customers, so it must finance 60 days of operations. Shortening the CCC (faster inventory turns, quicker collections, slower but not abusive payments) frees cash. Cutting DSO by 5 days frees 5 x 15,000 = $75,000.</p>"},{"h":"5. Managing receivables, inventory and payables; credit terms","body":"<p><strong>Receivables:</strong> credit terms attract customers but tie up cash and risk bad debts. Tools: credit screening, sensible terms, early-payment discounts, aging schedules, collection follow-up.</p><p><strong>Inventory:</strong> too little causes stockouts; too much costs money in storage, insurance, obsolescence and tied-up cash. Tools: economic order quantity (EOQ = square root of 2 x annual demand x order cost / carrying cost per unit), just-in-time, safety stock.</p><p><strong>Payables (trade credit):</strong> free financing from suppliers, but be careful about <strong>credit terms</strong>. The notation <strong>2/10 net 30</strong> means 2% off if paid within 10 days; otherwise the full amount is due at day 30.</p><p>Skipping the discount is a loan from the supplier, and it is expensive:</p><p><code>Cost of forgoing = [d / (1 - d)] x [365 / (N - p)]</code>, d = discount, N = net due day, p = discount period.</p><p>For 2/10 net 30: 0.02 / 0.98 = 2.0408%. You borrow for 20 days (30 - 10), so there are 365 / 20 = 18.25 periods: simple cost = 2.0408% x 18.25 = <strong>37.2%</strong>. Compounded, the effective annual rate is (1.020408)^18.25 - 1 = <strong>44.6%</strong>. If a bank loan costs less than that, borrow and take the discount. Paying late (stretching payables to day 40) shrinks the period to 30 days and lowers the cost to 24.8%, but harms supplier relations and credit rating. Longer net periods (2/10 net 60) cut the cost sharply, to 14.9%.</p>"},{"h":"6. Short-term financing and the liquidity-profitability trade-off","body":"<p><strong>Sources of short-term financing:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trade credit and accruals</strong> (spontaneous, often free if you pay on time).</li><li><strong>Bank loans and lines of credit</strong>: revolving credit up to a limit; interest on the amount used, often a commitment fee on the unused part.</li><li><strong>Commercial paper</strong>: unsecured short-term notes sold by large, strong firms.</li><li><strong>Secured loans and factoring</strong>: borrowing against receivables or inventory, or selling receivables at a discount to get cash now.</li></ul><p><strong>Liquidity vs. profitability:</strong> cash and inventory earn little or nothing, so more current assets mean safety but lower returns. Fewer current assets mean higher expected profit but more risk of a cash crunch. Likewise, short-term debt is usually cheaper than long-term debt, but it must be rolled over and rates can jump.</p><ul><li><strong>Conservative (relaxed) policy:</strong> high current assets and more long-term financing. Safer, lower return.</li><li><strong>Aggressive (restricted) policy:</strong> low current assets and heavy short-term borrowing. Riskier, higher return.</li><li><strong>Matching (maturity-matching) principle:</strong> finance permanent assets (fixed assets and the permanent part of current assets) with long-term funds, and temporary seasonal needs with short-term funds.</li></ul>"}],"keyPoints":["A financial plan starts with a sales forecast and turns it into budgets, a cash budget, and pro forma financial statements.","A cash budget shows the timing of cash inflows and outflows so the firm can plan borrowing or investing.","The percent-of-sales method assumes spontaneous assets and spontaneous liabilities rise in proportion to sales.","Additional funds needed equals the increase in required assets, minus the increase in spontaneous liabilities, minus the addition to retained earnings.","Faster growth, higher capital intensity, a lower profit margin, and a higher dividend payout all raise additional funds needed.","The internal growth rate is return on assets times the retention ratio, divided by one minus that product, and it assumes no external financing.","The sustainable growth rate uses return on equity in the same formula and assumes no new equity and a constant debt-to-equity ratio.","The cash conversion cycle equals days inventory outstanding plus days sales outstanding minus days payables outstanding.","Shortening the cash conversion cycle frees up cash, for example by collecting receivables faster or reducing inventory.","Credit terms of two slash ten net thirty mean a two percent discount if paid in ten days, otherwise the full amount is due in thirty days.","The cost of forgoing the discount is the discount divided by one minus the discount, times three hundred sixty five divided by the days of extra credit, which for two ten net thirty is about thirty seven percent.","An aggressive working capital policy holds fewer current assets and uses more short-term debt, which raises both expected return and risk, while a conservative policy does the opposite."],"examples":[{"q":"Cottonwood Co. has sales of $2,000,000 and expects them to grow 15% next year. Its assets of $1,500,000 all vary with sales, and its spontaneous liabilities are $300,000. Net profit margin is 6% and the payout ratio is 50%. What is AFN?","steps":["New sales S1 = 2,000,000 x 1.15 = 2,300,000; increase dS = 300,000.","Assets needed: (1,500,000 / 2,000,000) x 300,000 = 0.75 x 300,000 = 225,000.","Spontaneous liabilities: (300,000 / 2,000,000) x 300,000 = 0.15 x 300,000 = 45,000.","New net income = 0.06 x 2,300,000 = 138,000; retained earnings addition = 138,000 x (1 - 0.50) = 69,000.","AFN = 225,000 - 45,000 - 69,000 = 111,000."],"answer":"AFN = $111,000 of outside financing needed."},{"q":"Falcon Supply has annual COGS of $7,300,000, sales of $10,950,000, inventory of $1,200,000, receivables of $900,000 and accounts payable of $800,000 (use 365 days). Find DIO, DSO, DPO, the operating cycle, and the cash conversion cycle. How much cash would be freed by cutting DSO by 5 days?","steps":["Daily COGS = 7,300,000 / 365 = 20,000; daily sales = 10,950,000 / 365 = 30,000.","DIO = 1,200,000 / 20,000 = 60 days. DSO = 900,000 / 30,000 = 30 days. DPO = 800,000 / 20,000 = 40 days.","Operating cycle = DIO + DSO = 90 days.","CCC = 60 + 30 - 40 = 50 days.","Cutting DSO by 5 days frees 5 x 30,000 = 150,000 of cash."],"answer":"DIO 60, DSO 30, DPO 40; operating cycle 90 days; CCC 50 days; $150,000 freed."},{"q":"A supplier offers 3/15 net 45 on a $10,000 invoice. What is the annualized cost of not taking the discount (simple and compounded, 365 days)? If the firm can borrow from a bank at 12%, should it take the discount?","steps":["d = 3%, N = 45, p = 15, so extra credit = 45 - 15 = 30 days.","Periodic cost = 0.03 / 0.97 = 3.0928%; periods per year = 365 / 30 = 12.1667.","Simple annual cost = 3.0928% x 12.1667 = 37.6%.","Effective annual rate = (1.030928)^12.1667 - 1 = 44.9%.","Taking the discount: pay $9,700 on day 15, saving $300. Borrowing $9,700 from the bank for 30 days costs about 9,700 x 0.12 x 30 / 365 = $95.67, far less than $300, so take the discount."],"answer":"Cost of forgoing about 37.6% simple (44.9% effective); borrow at 12% and take the discount."}],"videos":[{"title":"Cash conversion cycle explained (DIO, DSO, DPO)","query":"cash conversion cycle DIO DSO DPO explained example"},{"title":"Additional funds needed (AFN) percent of sales method","query":"additional funds needed AFN percent of sales method pro forma"},{"title":"Cost of trade credit 2/10 net 30","query":"cost of trade credit 2/10 net 30 forgoing cash discount explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Pro forma financial statements","b":"Projected statements showing expected results of a plan."},{"f":"Cash budget","b":"Schedule of expected cash receipts and payments, showing needed borrowing or surplus."},{"f":"Percent-of-sales method","b":"Forecasts statement items as a constant percent of sales."},{"f":"Spontaneous liabilities","b":"Liabilities such as accounts payable and accruals that grow automatically with sales."},{"f":"Additional funds needed (AFN)","b":"Required asset growth minus spontaneous liabilities growth minus addition to retained earnings."},{"f":"AFN formula","b":"(A*/S0)dS - (L*/S0)dS - M x S1 x (1 - payout)."},{"f":"Capital intensity ratio","b":"A*/S0: assets needed per dollar of sales."},{"f":"What raises AFN?","b":"Faster growth, higher capital intensity, lower margin, higher payout."},{"f":"Internal growth rate","b":"ROA x b / (1 - ROA x b): growth with no external financing."},{"f":"Sustainable growth rate","b":"ROE x b / (1 - ROE x b): growth with no new equity, constant debt-to-equity."},{"f":"Retention ratio (b)","b":"1 - payout ratio."},{"f":"Days inventory outstanding","b":"Inventory / (COGS / 365)."},{"f":"Days payables outstanding","b":"Accounts payable / (COGS / 365)."},{"f":"Operating cycle","b":"DIO + DSO: days from buying inventory to collecting cash."},{"f":"Cash conversion cycle","b":"DIO + DSO - DPO."},{"f":"2/10 net 30","b":"2% discount if paid within 10 days; full amount due in 30 days."},{"f":"Cost of forgoing discount (simple)","b":"[d/(1-d)] x [365/(N-p)]; 2/10 net 30 is about 37.2%."},{"f":"Trade credit","b":"Supplier financing from buying now and paying later."},{"f":"Line of credit","b":"Revolving bank loan up to a set limit."},{"f":"Commercial paper","b":"Unsecured short-term notes issued by large, creditworthy firms."},{"f":"Aggressive working capital policy","b":"Low current assets, heavy short-term debt: higher return, higher risk."},{"f":"Matching principle","b":"Finance permanent assets with long-term funds, temporary needs with short-term funds."}],"pairs":[["Pro forma statement","Projected financial statement for a plan"],["Spontaneous liability","Liability that grows automatically with sales"],["AFN","External financing needed to support growth"],["Internal growth rate","Growth using retained earnings only"],["Sustainable growth rate","Growth without new equity, constant leverage"],["Operating cycle","Days from inventory purchase to cash collection"],["Cash conversion cycle","DIO plus DSO minus DPO"],["Trade credit","Financing from suppliers' delayed payment terms"]],"traps":["Trap: The cash conversion cycle adds DPO. Truth: CCC = DIO + DSO - DPO; paying suppliers later shortens it.","Trap: Skipping a 2/10 net 30 discount costs only 2%. Truth: Annualized, it is about 37% (44.6% compounded), usually costlier than a bank loan.","Trap: Longer net terms make the discount more costly to forgo. Truth: A longer net period shrinks the cost (2/10 net 60 is about 14.9%).","Trap: All balance sheet items grow with sales in AFN. Truth: Only spontaneous items do; notes payable, long-term debt and stock do not change automatically.","Trap: Internal and sustainable growth rates are the same. Truth: Internal uses ROA with zero external financing; sustainable uses ROE and allows debt to grow with equity."],"mnemonic":"CCC = 'Inventory plus Owed-to-us minus We-owe': DIO + DSO - DPO. Days you wait for cash, minus days the supplier lets you wait.","cram":["AFN=(A*/S0)dS-(L*/S0)dS-M*S1*(1-payout).","IGR=ROA*b/(1-ROA*b); SGR=ROE*b/(1-ROE*b).","CCC=DIO+DSO-DPO; operating cycle=DIO+DSO.","DIO=inventory/(COGS/365); DSO=receivables/(sales/365); DPO=payables/(COGS/365).","2/10 net 30: forgoing the discount costs about 37.2% annualized.","Cost of forgoing=[d/(1-d)]x[365/(N-p)].","Aggressive policy: less current assets, more short-term debt, more risk.","Negative AFN means a surplus of funds."],"ytv":[{"title":"How To Calculate The Cash Conversion Cycle | And What It Means","channel":"The Financial Controller","id":"C7BFvju8uIs","why":"Step-by-step CCC calculation with the DIO, DSO and DPO inputs."},{"title":"Operating Cycle and Cash (Conversion) Cycle Explained","channel":"Professor Ikram","id":"R9zZ-Z-GI0I","why":"Separates the operating cycle from the cash conversion cycle."},{"title":"What is the Cash Conversion Cycle?","channel":"Invoice Finance News","id":"Ooq0PnAEf5s","why":"Short plain-English overview of why CCC matters for cash."}],"questions":[{"q":"Which formula correctly gives the cash conversion cycle?","o":["DIO + DSO - DPO","DIO - DSO + DPO","DIO + DSO + DPO","DSO + DPO - DIO"],"a":0,"e":"CCC = days inventory + days receivables - days payables. Payables are a source of financing, so DPO is subtracted.","d":1},{"q":"Pro forma financial statements are","o":["audited statements filed with the SEC","projected statements showing the expected effects of a financial plan","statements prepared on a cash basis only","restated prior-year statements"],"a":1,"e":"Pro forma means 'as if' or projected, and they are central to planning. They are not audited historical reports.","d":1},{"q":"The percent-of-sales method of forecasting assumes that","o":["all liabilities and equity items stay constant","sales are unrelated to assets","certain balance sheet and income statement items change in proportion to sales","profit margin falls as sales rise"],"a":2,"e":"Spontaneous items like receivables, inventory and payables are tied to sales; financing items like long-term debt are not.","d":1},{"q":"Credit terms of 2/10 net 30 mean","o":["2% interest is charged if paid after 10 days and the balance is due in 30","a 10% discount if paid within 2 days","a 2% discount if paid after 30 days","a 2% discount if paid within 10 days, otherwise the full amount is due in 30 days"],"a":3,"e":"The first number is the discount percent, the second the discount period in days, and 'net 30' is the final due date.","d":1},{"q":"Which of the following is a spontaneous source of financing that increases automatically with sales?","o":["Long-term bonds","Accounts payable","Common stock","Notes payable to a bank"],"a":1,"e":"Payables and accruals grow with purchases and operations. Bonds, stock and bank notes require a deliberate financing decision.","d":1},{"q":"A company that chooses an aggressive working capital policy generally","o":["holds large cash balances and uses only long-term financing","has lower expected return and lower risk","holds fewer current assets and uses more short-term financing, accepting more risk","avoids all short-term debt"],"a":2,"e":"Aggressive means lean current assets and short-term debt. Higher expected return comes with higher liquidity risk.","d":1},{"q":"A firm has days inventory outstanding of 45, days sales outstanding of 35, and days payables outstanding of 25. What is the cash conversion cycle?","o":["105 days","15 days","80 days","55 days"],"a":3,"e":"CCC = 45 + 35 - 25 = 55 days. 105 adds DPO instead of subtracting it.","d":1},{"q":"The internal growth rate is the maximum growth rate a firm can achieve","o":["without any external financing","without any retained earnings","with unlimited new equity","with a constant payout of zero and unlimited debt"],"a":0,"e":"IGR assumes the firm funds growth only from retained earnings: no new debt, no new equity.","d":1},{"q":"Annual COGS is $2,190,000 and inventory is $300,000. Using 365 days, what is days inventory outstanding?","o":["7.3 days","1,095 days","50 days","6 days"],"a":2,"e":"Daily COGS = 2,190,000 / 365 = 6,000. DIO = 300,000 / 6,000 = 50 days.","d":2},{"q":"Annual sales are $3,650,000 (all on credit) and receivables are $400,000. Using 365 days, what is DSO?","o":["9 days","91 days","10 days","40 days"],"a":3,"e":"Daily sales = 3,650,000 / 365 = 10,000. DSO = 400,000 / 10,000 = 40 days.","d":2},{"q":"Annual COGS is $2,190,000 and accounts payable are $180,000. Using 365 days, what is days payables outstanding?","o":["30 days","12 days","50 days","8.2 days"],"a":0,"e":"Daily COGS = 6,000. DPO = 180,000 / 6,000 = 30 days.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's days inventory outstanding is 50 and its days sales outstanding is 40. What is its operating cycle?","o":["10 days","90 days","40 days","50 days"],"a":1,"e":"Operating cycle = DIO + DSO = 90 days. The cash conversion cycle would additionally subtract DPO.","d":2},{"q":"A firm begins the month with $30,000 in cash, expects collections of $210,000, and expects payments of $245,000. Its minimum cash balance is $25,000. How much must it borrow?","o":["$5,000","$25,000","$0","$30,000"],"a":3,"e":"Cash before borrowing = 30,000 + 210,000 - 245,000 = -5,000. To end at 25,000 it must borrow 30,000. $5,000 only covers the deficit, not the minimum balance.","d":2},{"q":"What is the approximate annualized cost (365-day simple method) of forgoing the discount on terms of 2/10 net 30?","o":["37.2%","2.0%","18.3%","74.5%"],"a":0,"e":"[0.02 / 0.98] x [365 / 20] = 2.0408% x 18.25 = 37.2%. 2.0% is only the per-period rate.","d":2},{"q":"A supplier offers 1/10 net 30. The firm can borrow from a bank at 10%. What should it do?","o":["Skip the discount, because the discount is only 1%","Take the discount, because forgoing it costs about 18.4% annualized","Take the discount only if the bank rate is above 18.4%","Pay on day 30 since the cost is 1%"],"a":1,"e":"Cost of forgoing = 0.01 / 0.99 x 365 / 20 = 18.4%. Borrowing at 10% to capture the discount is cheaper.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has ROE of 15% and retains 60% of earnings. What is its sustainable growth rate?","o":["9.00%","15.00%","9.89%","6.00%"],"a":2,"e":"SGR = (0.15 x 0.60) / (1 - 0.15 x 0.60) = 0.09 / 0.91 = 9.89%. 9.00% omits the denominator adjustment.","d":2},{"q":"In the percent-of-sales method, which item would typically NOT increase automatically when sales grow?","o":["Long-term debt","Accounts receivable","Inventory","Accounts payable"],"a":0,"e":"Long-term debt changes only when management borrows or repays. Receivables, inventory and payables tend to scale with sales.","d":2},{"q":"Projected sales are $5,000,000, net profit margin is 4%, and the payout ratio is 25%. How much will be added to retained earnings?","o":["$200,000","$150,000","$50,000","$1,250,000"],"a":1,"e":"Net income = 5,000,000 x 0.04 = 200,000. Retained = 200,000 x (1 - 0.25) = 150,000.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's assets of $600,000 all vary with sales of $1,000,000. If sales grow by $100,000 and spontaneous liabilities and retained earnings are ignored, how much additional asset financing is required?","o":["$100,000","$600,000","$60,000","$6,000"],"a":2,"e":"Capital intensity = 600,000 / 1,000,000 = 0.60. Added assets = 0.60 x 100,000 = 60,000.","d":2},{"q":"Which action would shorten a firm's cash conversion cycle?","o":["Building a larger safety stock of inventory","Paying suppliers earlier than required","Extending the credit period granted to customers","Offering customers a discount for early payment, reducing DSO"],"a":3,"e":"Faster collections cut DSO and the CCC. More inventory, earlier payment, and looser credit all lengthen it.","d":2},{"q":"Holding larger cash and inventory balances most directly illustrates which trade-off?","o":["Higher profitability and higher liquidity","Higher liquidity but lower profitability","Lower liquidity and lower risk","Higher leverage and lower interest cost"],"a":1,"e":"Cash and inventory are safe but earn little. The classic working capital trade-off is safety (liquidity) versus return.","d":2},{"q":"Sales are projected to rise from $4,000,000 to $4,600,000. Assets of $2,400,000 and spontaneous liabilities of $400,000 vary with sales. The profit margin is 5% and the payout ratio is 40%. What is AFN?","o":["$222,000","$300,000","$162,000","$360,000"],"a":2,"e":"dS = 600,000. Assets: 0.60 x 600,000 = 360,000. Liabilities: 0.10 x 600,000 = 60,000. Retained = 0.05 x 4,600,000 x 0.60 = 138,000. AFN = 360,000 - 60,000 - 138,000 = 162,000.","d":3},{"q":"A firm has net income of $90,000, total assets of $1,000,000, and a payout ratio of 30%. What is its internal growth rate?","o":["6.30%","9.00%","2.70%","6.72%"],"a":3,"e":"ROA = 9%, b = 0.70, ROA x b = 6.3%. IGR = 0.063 / 0.937 = 6.72%. 6.30% forgets to divide by (1 - ROA x b).","d":3},{"q":"Annual COGS is $5,475,000 and sales are $7,300,000. Inventory is $900,000, receivables are $600,000, and payables are $375,000. Using 365 days, what is the cash conversion cycle?","o":["65 days","115 days","90 days","40 days"],"a":0,"e":"Daily COGS 15,000: DIO = 60, DPO = 25. Daily sales 20,000: DSO = 30. CCC = 60 + 30 - 25 = 65.","d":3},{"q":"Annual sales are $7,300,000 (365-day year). A new collections program cuts DSO from 45 days to 36 days. How much cash is released from receivables?","o":["$900,000","$720,000","$180,000","$20,000"],"a":2,"e":"Daily sales = 20,000. Reduction = 9 days x 20,000 = 180,000. $900,000 is 45 days of receivables, the whole balance before the change.","d":3},{"q":"What is the approximate annualized cost (simple, 365 days) of forgoing the discount on terms of 2/10 net 60?","o":["37.2%","2.0%","7.3%","14.9%"],"a":3,"e":"[0.02 / 0.98] x [365 / 50] = 2.0408% x 7.3 = 14.9%. 37.2% is the cost for net 30; the longer net period spreads the same 2% over more days.","d":3},{"q":"A firm that has 2/10 net 30 terms decides to pay on day 40 instead of day 30, still giving up the discount. What is its approximate annualized cost of the trade credit (simple, 365 days)?","o":["24.8%","37.2%","18.3%","2.0%"],"a":0,"e":"Credit period = 40 - 10 = 30 days. Cost = 0.02 / 0.98 x 365 / 30 = 2.0408% x 12.167 = 24.8%. Stretching payables lowers the quoted cost but harms supplier relationships.","d":3},{"q":"What is the effective annual rate (compounded, 365 days) of forgoing the discount on terms of 2/10 net 30?","o":["37.2%","44.6%","2.04%","40.8%"],"a":1,"e":"EAR = (1 + 0.02 / 0.98)^(365 / 20) - 1 = (1.020408)^18.25 - 1 = 44.6%. The 37.2% figure ignores compounding.","d":3},{"q":"Sales will rise from $1,000,000 to $1,050,000. Assets of $500,000 and spontaneous liabilities of $100,000 vary with sales. Profit margin is 8% and payout is 25%. What does the calculation show?","o":["Additional funds needed of $43,000","Additional funds needed of $25,000","A surplus of $63,000","A surplus of $43,000 (AFN is -$43,000)"],"a":3,"e":"dS = 50,000. Assets +25,000; liabilities +5,000; retained = 0.08 x 1,050,000 x 0.75 = 63,000. AFN = 25,000 - 5,000 - 63,000 = -43,000, a surplus.","d":3},{"q":"A firm earns a 5% net margin, has total asset turnover of 1.6, and an equity multiplier of 2.0. It retains 50% of earnings. What is its sustainable growth rate?","o":["8.70%","8.00%","16.00%","4.35%"],"a":0,"e":"ROE (DuPont) = 5% x 1.6 x 2.0 = 16%. ROE x b = 8%. SGR = 0.08 / 0.92 = 8.70%. 8.00% skips the denominator adjustment.","d":3}]},{"id":"u5","title":"Time Value of Money: Lump Sums","day":3,"summary":"Learn why money has a time value and how to compound and discount single cash flows, solve for rates and periods, compare APR with EAR, and use a financial calculator or Excel.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Why a dollar today beats a dollar tomorrow","body":"<p>The <strong>time value of money (TVM)</strong> is the idea that a dollar in your hand today is worth more than a dollar promised in the future. There are three reasons.</p><ul><li><strong>Opportunity cost:</strong> money today can be invested and earn a return, so waiting costs you that return.</li><li><strong>Inflation:</strong> prices rise, so a future dollar buys less.</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> a promised future payment might not arrive.</li></ul><p><strong>Business example:</strong> a supplier offers your company $90,000 today or $120,000 in four years. The bigger number looks better, but you cannot compare dollars at different dates directly. You must move both amounts to the same date. Everything in this unit is just a way of moving dollars across time.</p><p>Two directions: <strong>compounding</strong> moves money forward in time (future value); <strong>discounting</strong> moves it backward (present value). The interest rate is the exchange rate between dates. When you discount, the rate is often called the <strong>discount rate</strong> or <strong>required return</strong>.</p>"},{"h":"2. Future value of a lump sum","body":"<p>A <strong>lump sum</strong> is a single amount paid or received once. If you invest <code>PV</code> dollars at rate <code>r</code> per period for <code>n</code> periods with compound interest:</p><p><code>FV = PV x (1 + r)^n</code></p><p>The term <code>(1 + r)^n</code> is the <strong>future value interest factor</strong>. <strong>Compounding</strong> means you earn interest on your interest. Simple interest would be only <code>PV x (1 + r x n)</code>.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> your firm sets aside $40,000 earning 5% for 6 years. FV = 40,000 x 1.05^6 = $53,603.83. Simple interest would give only $52,000.00; the difference of $1,603.83 is interest earned on interest.</p><p>Key habit: <em>rate and period must match.</em> If n is in months, r must be a monthly rate.</p>"},{"h":"3. Present value and discounting","body":"<p>To move money backward, solve the same formula for PV:</p><p><code>PV = FV / (1 + r)^n</code></p><p>This is called <strong>discounting</strong>. A higher rate or a longer time gives a smaller PV.</p><p><strong>Back to the supplier:</strong> if your opportunity cost is 8%, the $120,000 in four years has PV = 120,000 / 1.08^4 = $88,203.58. That is less than $90,000 today, so you should take the $90,000. The decision rule is always: put every cash flow at the same date, then compare.</p><p>For several lump sums at different dates, discount each one separately and add: <code>PV = sum of CFt / (1 + r)^t</code>. You cannot discount a total at the average date.</p><p><strong>Sanity check:</strong> PV is always less than FV when r is positive and n is positive. If your PV is bigger than your FV, you compounded in the wrong direction.</p>"},{"h":"4. Solving for the rate (r) and the number of periods (n)","body":"<p>The same equation has four variables: PV, FV, r, and n. Know any three and you can find the fourth.</p><ul><li><strong>Solve for r:</strong> <code>r = (FV / PV)^(1/n) - 1</code>. Example: $10,000 grows to $16,000 in 6 years: r = 1.6^(1/6) - 1 = 8.15%.</li><li><strong>Solve for n:</strong> <code>n = ln(FV / PV) / ln(1 + r)</code>. Example: $8,000 to $20,000 at 7%: n = ln(2.5)/ln(1.07) = 13.54 years.</li></ul><p>On a financial calculator you enter the three knowns and press compute on the unknown. In Excel use <code>=RATE</code> and <code>=NPER</code>.</p><p><strong>Common errors:</strong> dividing the total gain by n (that ignores compounding), and forgetting that if n counts months, the answer is in months.</p>"},{"h":"5. Compounding frequency, APR and EAR, continuous compounding","body":"<p>Banks often quote an <strong>APR (annual percentage rate)</strong>, also called the <strong>nominal rate</strong>. It is the periodic rate times the number of periods per year. It does <em>not</em> include compounding. If compounding happens <code>m</code> times a year:</p><ul><li>Periodic rate = <code>APR / m</code></li><li>Number of periods = <code>years x m</code></li><li><strong>EAR</strong> (effective annual rate) = <code>(1 + APR/m)^m - 1</code></li></ul><p>EAR is the true annual growth rate, so use it to compare offers. Example: 12% APR compounded monthly has a periodic rate of 1% and EAR = 1.01^12 - 1 = 12.68%. The same 12% compounded semiannually has EAR 12.36%.</p><p><strong>Continuous compounding</strong> is the limit as m grows without bound: <code>FV = PV x e^(r x t)</code> and <code>EAR = e^r - 1</code>. At 6% continuous, EAR = 6.184%. More frequent compounding always gives a slightly higher EAR, but the gain shrinks quickly.</p><p>Business tip: a credit card at 18% APR compounded monthly really costs 19.56% a year.</p>"},{"h":"6. The Rule of 72 and quick estimates","body":"<p>The <strong>Rule of 72</strong> estimates doubling time: <code>years to double = 72 / rate in percent</code>. At 9%, money doubles in about 72/9 = 8 years (exact: 8.04). It is most accurate for rates between 6% and 10%.</p><p>You can run it backward: to double in 6 years you need about 72/6 = 12%. Use it to check your calculator answers. Tripling or quadrupling means roughly two or three doubling periods when it is close to a power of two.</p><p><strong>Business use:</strong> if tuition or a supplier price rises 4% a year, it doubles in about 18 years.</p>"},{"h":"7. Calculator and Excel mechanics","body":"<p>The five TVM keys are <strong>N</strong> (number of periods), <strong>I/Y</strong> (interest rate per period in percent, not decimal), <strong>PV</strong>, <strong>PMT</strong>, and <strong>FV</strong>. Set PMT = 0 for lump-sum problems.</p><p><strong>Sign convention:</strong> cash you pay out is negative; cash you receive is positive. If you enter PV = -1,000 (you deposit), the computed FV is positive (you get money back). If both have the same sign the answer shows the opposite sign, which is a hint you set it up wrong.</p><ul><li><code>=FV(rate, nper, pmt, pv)</code></li><li><code>=PV(rate, nper, pmt, fv)</code></li><li><code>=RATE(nper, pmt, pv, fv)</code></li><li><code>=NPER(rate, pmt, pv, fv)</code></li></ul><p>Example: <code>=FV(0.05, 10, 0, -1000)</code> returns $1,628.89. Always clear previous entries and match the rate to the period (monthly compounding: rate/12 and years x 12).</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Time value of money means a dollar today is worth more than a dollar in the future because money can earn a return.","Future value equals present value times one plus r, all raised to the power n.","Present value equals future value divided by one plus r, raised to the power n. This is called discounting.","A higher discount rate or a longer time period always lowers the present value of a future payment.","To solve for the rate, use r equals the quantity FV divided by PV, raised to the power one over n, minus one.","To solve for the number of periods, use n equals the natural log of FV over PV, divided by the natural log of one plus r.","The periodic rate equals the APR divided by the number of compounding periods per year, and the number of periods equals years times m.","The effective annual rate, or EAR, equals one plus APR over m, raised to the m, minus one. Always compare offers using EAR.","More frequent compounding gives a higher EAR. Continuous compounding uses e to the r times t, and its EAR is e to the r minus one.","The Rule of 72 says years to double is about seventy-two divided by the interest rate in percent.","On a financial calculator and in Excel, outflows are negative and inflows are positive. Match the rate to the period, and use PMT equals zero for lump sums."],"examples":[{"q":"Your firm invests $40,000 today at 5% compounded annually. (a) What is it worth in 6 years? (b) How much would you need to invest today at 5% to have $100,000 in 6 years?","steps":["Part (a): FV = PV x (1 + r)^n = 40,000 x 1.05^6.","1.05^6 = 1.340096, so FV = $53,603.83.","Part (b): PV = FV / (1 + r)^n = 100,000 / 1.05^6.","PV = $74,621.54.","Calculator check for (b): N = 6, I/Y = 5, FV = 100,000, PMT = 0, compute PV; Excel: =PV(0.05,6,0,100000) returns a negative number (you pay it out)."],"answer":"(a) $53,603.83; (b) $74,621.54"},{"q":"A land parcel bought for $200,000 is sold 9 years later for $350,000. (a) What annual rate of return did the owner earn? (b) At that same rate, how many years would it take for $350,000 to double?","steps":["Part (a): r = (FV/PV)^(1/n) - 1 = (350,000/200,000)^(1/9) - 1.","(1.75)^(1/9) = 1.064153, so r = 6.42%.","Part (b): n = ln(2)/ln(1 + r).","n = 11.15 years.","Rule-of-72 check: 72 / 6.4 = about 11.3 years. Close to the exact answer."],"answer":"(a) about 6.42% per year; (b) about 11.1 years"},{"q":"Bank X offers 6.00% APR compounded monthly. Bank Y offers 6.10% APR compounded semiannually. Bank Z offers 6.15% compounded annually. (a) Find each EAR. (b) Which is best for a saver, and what is the value of $10,000 after 3 years at the best bank?","steps":["EAR = (1 + APR/m)^m - 1.","X: (1 + .06/12)^12 - 1 = 6.168%.","Y: (1 + .061/2)^2 - 1 = 6.193%.","Z: compounded annually, so EAR = 6.150%.","Best for a saver: Y (highest EAR, even though Z has the highest stated rate). FV = 10,000 x (1.0305)^6 = $11,975.34.","Lesson: do not rank by stated rate; rank by EAR."],"answer":"EARs: X 6.168%, Y 6.193%, Z 6.150%. Y is best; $10,000 grows to $11,975.34."}],"videos":[{"title":"Time value of money present value future value explained","query":"time value of money present value future value explained for beginners"},{"title":"APR vs EAR effective annual rate","query":"APR vs EAR effective annual rate compounding explained finance"},{"title":"Financial calculator N I/Y PV PMT FV tutorial","query":"BA II Plus time value of money N I/Y PV PMT FV tutorial"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Time value of money","b":"A dollar today is worth more than a dollar later because it can earn a return."},{"f":"Future value formula","b":"FV = PV x (1 + r)^n"},{"f":"Present value formula","b":"PV = FV / (1 + r)^n"},{"f":"Compounding","b":"Earning interest on previously earned interest."},{"f":"Discounting","b":"Moving a future cash flow back to today's value."},{"f":"Discount rate","b":"The required return used to compute present value; opportunity cost."},{"f":"Solve for r","b":"r = (FV/PV)^(1/n) - 1"},{"f":"Solve for n","b":"n = ln(FV/PV) / ln(1 + r)"},{"f":"APR (nominal rate)","b":"Periodic rate times periods per year; ignores compounding."},{"f":"Periodic rate","b":"APR divided by number of compounding periods per year, m."},{"f":"EAR formula","b":"EAR = (1 + APR/m)^m - 1"},{"f":"Which rate to compare loans/investments?","b":"The EAR, not the APR."},{"f":"Continuous compounding FV","b":"FV = PV x e^(r x t)"},{"f":"EAR with continuous compounding","b":"EAR = e^r - 1"},{"f":"Rule of 72","b":"Years to double is about 72 divided by the rate in percent."},{"f":"Effect of more frequent compounding","b":"Higher EAR and higher future value (diminishing gains)."},{"f":"Calculator keys","b":"N, I/Y, PV, PMT, FV; set PMT = 0 for lump sums."},{"f":"Sign convention","b":"Cash out negative, cash in positive; PV and FV need opposite signs."},{"f":"Excel FV / PV","b":"=FV(rate,nper,pmt,pv) and =PV(rate,nper,pmt,fv)"},{"f":"Excel RATE / NPER","b":"=RATE(nper,pmt,pv,fv) and =NPER(rate,pmt,pv,fv)"},{"f":"Simple vs compound interest","b":"Simple earns only on principal: PV(1 + r n). Compound earns interest on interest."}],"questions":[{"q":"A manager says, 'Receiving $10,000 today is better than receiving $10,000 three years from now, even if there is no inflation.' Which idea best supports this statement?","o":["Money received today can be invested to earn a return, so it grows to more than $10,000 by year three","Cash loses its physical value if it is held for three years","Future payments are always legally uncertain, so they have no value","A dollar is worth the same at any date, so the order does not matter"],"a":0,"e":"This is the time value of money: money in hand can earn interest or a return (the opportunity cost of waiting). The 'same value at any date' option ignores that opportunity cost.","d":1},{"q":"You deposit $5,000 in an account that pays 6% interest compounded annually. What is the balance after 4 years?","o":["$6,200.00","$6,691.13","$6,312.38","$3,960.47"],"a":2,"e":"FV = 5,000 x (1.06)^4 = $6,312.38. The $6,200.00 answer is simple interest (no interest on interest); $3,960.47 wrongly discounts instead of compounding.","d":1},{"q":"A supplier will pay your firm $20,000 in 5 years. If your required return is 8% compounded annually, what is the present value of that payment?","o":["$29,386.56","$13,611.66","$14,285.71","$14,700.60"],"a":1,"e":"PV = 20,000 / (1.08)^5 = $13,611.66. Multiplying by 1.08^5 gives a future value, not a present value; dividing by 1 + 0.08 x 5 uses simple interest and overstates PV; using 4 periods is the wrong n.","d":1},{"q":"On a financial calculator you enter N = 10, I/Y = 5, PV = -1,000, PMT = 0, and compute FV. Why does the calculator show FV as a positive number?","o":["Positive always means the answer is correct and negative means an error","The calculator is subtracting the interest from the deposit","The calculator uses a sign convention: money you pay out (PV) and money you receive back (FV) must have opposite signs","FV is always displayed positive no matter what sign PV has"],"a":2,"e":"Cash-flow sign convention: outflows are negative and inflows positive. Entering PV as -1,000 (you deposit) makes FV come out positive (you withdraw 1,628.89). If both were positive the calculator would show FV negative.","d":1},{"q":"Using the Rule of 72, about how many years will it take an investment earning 9% per year to double?","o":["About 9 years","About 12 years","About 8 years","About 6 years"],"a":2,"e":"Rule of 72: years to double is about 72 divided by the rate in percent. 72 / 9 = 8. Twelve years would be the answer at 6 percent.","d":1},{"q":"A project promises a single $500,000 payment in 6 years. Holding everything else constant, what happens to the present value of that payment if the discount rate increases?","o":["The present value increases","The present value decreases","The present value stays the same because the payment is fixed","The future value decreases but the present value is unchanged"],"a":1,"e":"PV = FV / (1 + r)^n. A larger r makes the denominator larger, so PV falls. Discount rate and PV move in opposite directions.","d":1},{"q":"Which Excel function returns the number of periods needed for an investment to grow from a present value to a target future value at a given rate?","o":["=RATE","=NPER","=PMT","=PV"],"a":1,"e":"NPER solves for the number of periods. RATE solves for the interest rate, PMT for the payment, and PV for the present value.","d":1},{"q":"A bank quotes a 12% APR compounded monthly. What is the periodic (monthly) interest rate?","o":["1.00%","12.00%","0.10%","12.68%"],"a":0,"e":"Periodic rate = APR / m = 12% / 12 = 1% per month. 12.68% is the effective annual rate (EAR), not the monthly rate.","d":1},{"q":"You invest $10,000 and it grows to $16,000 in exactly 6 years with annual compounding and no other deposits. What annual return did you earn?","o":["8.15%","10.00%","9.86%","6.94%"],"a":0,"e":"(FV/PV)^(1/n) - 1 = (1.6)^(1/6) - 1 = 8.15%. Dividing the 60% total gain by 6 gives 10% but ignores compounding; using n = 5 or 7 is a wrong exponent.","d":2},{"q":"How many years will it take $8,000 to grow to $20,000 at 7% compounded annually?","o":["21.43 years","13.54 years","10.29 years","13.09 years"],"a":1,"e":"n = ln(20,000/8,000) / ln(1.07) = ln(2.5)/ln(1.07) = 13.54 years. 21.43 is simple interest; 10.29 is the time to DOUBLE; dividing by 0.07 instead of ln(1.07) is an approximation error.","d":2},{"q":"A bank advertises a 12% APR compounded monthly. What is the effective annual rate (EAR)?","o":["12.68%","12.00%","12.36%","12.55%"],"a":0,"e":"EAR = (1 + .12/12)^12 - 1 = 12.68%. Twelve percent is the APR, and the 12.36% option is semiannual compounding.","d":2},{"q":"Which investment offers the highest effective annual rate? (A) 8.00% APR compounded quarterly, (B) 7.90% APR compounded monthly, (C) 8.10% APR compounded annually.","o":["B, with an EAR of 8.19%","C, because it has the highest stated rate of 8.10%","A, with an EAR of 8.24%","All three are equal because they are all about 8%"],"a":2,"e":"EARs: A = 8.24%, B = 8.19%, C = 8.10%. Compare effective rates, not stated rates; compounding frequency can beat a higher quoted rate.","d":2},{"q":"You invest $15,000 at an 8% APR compounded semiannually for 3 years. What is the ending balance?","o":["$18,895.68","$16,872.96","$19,023.63","$18,979.79"],"a":3,"e":"Semiannual: rate per period = 4%, periods = 6. FV = 15,000 x 1.04^6 = $18,979.79. Using annual compounding gives $18,895.68; using only 3 periods at 4% understates it.","d":2},{"q":"You want $50,000 in 10 years. An account pays 6% APR compounded monthly. How much must you deposit today?","o":["$27,919.74","$47,567.40","$27,481.64","$90,969.84"],"a":2,"e":"PV = 50,000 / (1.005)^120 = $27,481.64. Using annual compounding gives $27,919.74, a larger deposit than needed. Multiplying instead of dividing is a future-value error.","d":2},{"q":"Tuition at a private university is $20,000 today and is expected to rise 4% per year. According to the Rule of 72, about when will it reach $40,000?","o":["In about 14 years","In about 18 years","In about 36 years","In about 25 years"],"a":1,"e":"72 / 4 = 18 years to double. Doubling $20,000 gives $40,000. The 36-year answer would be a quadrupling.","d":2},{"q":"You deposit $10,000 in an account paying 5% compounded continuously. What is the balance after 4 years?","o":["$12,155.06","$12,000.00","$10,512.71","$12,214.03"],"a":3,"e":"FV = PV x e^(rt) = 10,000 x e^(0.05 x 4) = 10,000 x e^0.20 = $12,214.03. Annual compounding gives $12,155.06, slightly less. Continuous compounding is the limit of more and more frequent compounding.","d":2},{"q":"A buyer offers you $90,000 today or $120,000 in 4 years. Your opportunity cost is 8% compounded annually. Which is the better choice?","o":["Take the $120,000 because it is the larger nominal amount","Take the $90,000 today; the $120,000 has a present value of only $88,203.58","Take the $120,000; its present value is $163,258.68","They are exactly equal in value"],"a":1,"e":"PV of $120,000 = 120,000 / 1.08^4 = $88,203.58, which is less than $90,000. The $163,258.68 figure comes from compounding in the wrong direction.","d":2},{"q":"In Excel you type =FV(0.05, 10, 0, 1000). What does it return?","o":["+$1,628.89, always positive","-$1,628.89, a negative number, because the 1,000 present value was entered as a positive inflow","$1,500.00 using simple interest","A #NUM! error because PMT is zero"],"a":1,"e":"Excel uses the same sign convention as a calculator. With pv entered positive, FV returns -$1,628.89. To get a positive answer, enter the present value as -1000. A PMT of zero is perfectly valid.","d":2},{"q":"A bank pays 6% APR compounded daily (365 days) on a $20,000 deposit. What is the balance after exactly one year?","o":["$21,200.00","$21,233.56","$21,227.27","$21,236.63"],"a":3,"e":"FV = 20,000 x (1 + .06/365)^365 = $21,236.63. Annual compounding gives $21,200.00; monthly gives $21,233.56; quarterly $21,227.27. More frequent compounding means a higher ending balance.","d":2},{"q":"What is the present value of $1,000,000 to be received in 30 years if the discount rate is 5% compounded annually?","o":["$400,000.00","$4,321,942.38","$231,377.45","$952,380.95"],"a":2,"e":"PV = 1,000,000 / 1.05^30 = $231,377.45. The $400,000.00 figure uses simple interest. Discounting is powerful over long horizons.","d":2},{"q":"$2,000 grows to $3,000 in 5 years with semiannual compounding. What is the APR?","o":["8.28%","4.14%","8.45%","10.00%"],"a":0,"e":"Periodic rate = (3,000/2,000)^(1/10) - 1 = 4.14% per half-year. APR = 2 x 4.14% = 8.28%. 4.14% is only the periodic rate; 8.45% is the effective annual rate.","d":2},{"q":"You deposit $5,000. It earns 6% per year for the first 3 years and then 8% per year for the next 2 years (all compounded annually). What is the balance after 5 years?","o":["$6,946.01","$7,012.76","$7,346.64","$6,691.13"],"a":0,"e":"Do it in two stages: 5,000 x 1.06^3 = $5,955.08; then x 1.08^2 = $6,946.01. Averaging the rates (7%) gives $7,012.76, which is wrong because compounding is multiplicative.","d":3},{"q":"You want $50,000 in 8 years. An account pays 6% APR compounded quarterly. How much must you deposit today?","o":["$31,049.65","$31,370.62","$44,385.56","$7,747.87"],"a":0,"e":"Quarterly: i = 1.5%, n = 32. PV = 50,000 / 1.015^32 = $31,049.65. Annual discounting gives $31,370.62; mixing 6% with 32 periods or 1.5% with 8 periods are mismatches of rate and period.","d":3},{"q":"Option A: $1,000 invested for 2 years at 12% APR compounded monthly. Option B: $1,000 invested for 2 years at a 12.5% effective annual rate. Which ends with more money?","o":["Option B, because 12.5% is higher than 12%","They are equal at $1,269.73","Option A by about $20","Option A: $1,269.73 versus $1,265.62 for Option B"],"a":3,"e":"A: 1,000 x 1.01^24 = $1,269.73 (EAR 12.68%). B: 1,000 x 1.125^2 = $1,265.62. A's 12% was a nominal APR; its effective rate 12.68% beats 12.5%. The difference is only about $4.11.","d":3},{"q":"An account pays 6% compounded continuously. What is its effective annual rate?","o":["6.000%","6.168%","6.184%","6.090%"],"a":2,"e":"EAR = e^0.06 - 1 = 6.184%. Monthly compounding gives 6.168% and semiannual 6.090%; continuous is the highest possible for a 6% nominal rate.","d":3},{"q":"A buyer promises $10,000 in 2 years and another $15,000 in 5 years. At a 7% discount rate, what are these promises worth today?","o":["$17,824.65","$19,429.18","$20,231.44","$25,000.00"],"a":1,"e":"Discount each cash flow separately: 10,000/1.07^2 = $8,734.39; 15,000/1.07^5 = $10,694.79; total $19,429.18. Discounting the $25,000 total for 5 years, or swapping the times, is wrong.","d":3},{"q":"At 8% compounded annually, about how many years does it take money to triple?","o":["9.00 years","13.73 years","37.50 years","14.27 years"],"a":3,"e":"n = ln(3)/ln(1.08) = 14.27 years. Nine years is the Rule-of-72 DOUBLING time; 37.5 is 3/0.08, a simple-interest answer.","d":3},{"q":"A credit card charges an 18% APR compounded monthly. What is the effective annual rate you actually pay if you carry a balance for a full year?","o":["18.00%","19.25%","1.50%","19.56%"],"a":3,"e":"EAR = (1 + .18/12)^12 - 1 = 19.56%. The 1.50% is the monthly rate and the 19.25% figure assumes quarterly compounding.","d":3},{"q":"You have $25,000 today and want $250,000 in 30 years with no further deposits. Roughly what annual return (compounded annually) is required?","o":["7.98%","30.00%","7.00%","10.00%"],"a":0,"e":"(250,000/25,000)^(1/30) - 1 = 10^(1/30) - 1 = 7.98%. At 7% the money would reach only $190,306.38. Ten times your money is not a 10% annual return; it is spread over 30 compounding years.","d":3},{"q":"$5,000 grows to $9,000 in an account paying 6% APR compounded monthly. How many years does it take?","o":["About 117.85 years","About 10.09 years","About 12.00 years","About 9.82 years"],"a":3,"e":"Solve in months: n = ln(1.8)/ln(1.005) = 117.85 months, which is 9.82 years. Reporting 117.85 forgets that n counts months; using annual 6% ignores monthly compounding.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Compounding","Earning interest on prior interest"],["Discounting","Bringing future dollars to today"],["APR","Nominal annual rate without compounding"],["EAR","True annual rate including compounding"],["Periodic rate","APR divided by compounding periods"],["Rule of 72","72 over rate gives doubling years"],["Continuous compounding","Growth factor e to the rt"],["Discount rate","Required return used to find PV"]],"traps":["Trap: Higher stated (nominal) rate means the better deal. Truth: Compare EARs; compounding frequency can reverse the ranking.","Trap: Total gain divided by years gives the annual return. Truth: Returns compound, so use (FV/PV)^(1/n) - 1.","Trap: With monthly compounding, plug the annual rate and years into the formula. Truth: Use APR/12 and years x 12 so rate and period match.","Trap: Solving for n with months gives years. Truth: n is counted in compounding periods; divide months by 12 for years.","Trap: Entering PV and FV with the same sign. Truth: One is cash out and the other cash in, so signs must be opposite."],"mnemonic":"Compound goes UP, Discount goes DOWN: FV multiplies by (1+r)^n, PV divides by (1+r)^n. 'Multiply to move forward, divide to go back.'","cram":["FV = PV(1+r)^n; PV = FV/(1+r)^n.","r = (FV/PV)^(1/n) - 1; n = ln(FV/PV)/ln(1+r).","Periodic rate = APR/m; periods = years x m.","EAR = (1 + APR/m)^m - 1; compare offers on EAR.","Continuous: FV = PV e^(rt); EAR = e^r - 1.","Rule of 72: doubling years = 72 / rate percent.","Calculator signs: outflow negative, inflow positive; PMT = 0 for lump sums.","Higher r or longer n means lower PV."],"ytv":[{"title":"Time Value of Money - Present Value vs Future Value","channel":"The Organic Chemistry Tutor","id":"cy4PiY5ERTI","why":"Clear worked examples of FV and PV formulas."},{"title":"How to Calculate Present Value and Future Value","channel":"Prof John K","id":"dtfnWxez13w","why":"Step-by-step calculations with calculator and formulas."},{"title":"Time Value of Money - Explained (Step by Step Beginner's Guide)","channel":"WallStreetMojo","id":"_Kn5Q3DyZmY","why":"Beginner-friendly overview of why TVM matters."}]},{"id":"u6","title":"Annuities, Perpetuities & Loans","day":3,"summary":"Value level payment streams, build loan payments and amortization schedules, handle perpetuities and uneven cash flows, and solve retirement-savings problems.","lesson":[{"h":"1. What is an annuity? Ordinary vs annuity due","body":"<p>An <strong>annuity</strong> is a series of <em>equal</em> cash payments at <em>regular</em> intervals for a <em>fixed</em> number of periods. Car loans, mortgages, lease payments, and bond coupons are annuities.</p><ul><li><strong>Ordinary annuity:</strong> payments occur at the <strong>end</strong> of each period (first payment at t = 1). Loans and most corporate finance problems default to this.</li><li><strong>Annuity due:</strong> payments occur at the <strong>beginning</strong> of each period (first payment at t = 0). Rent and leases are usually paid in advance.</li></ul><p>A <strong>perpetuity</strong> is an annuity that never ends. An <strong>uneven (mixed) cash flow stream</strong> has unequal payments and is handled one cash flow at a time.</p><p><strong>Key link:</strong> annuity due value = ordinary annuity value x (1 + r), for both PV and FV, because every payment comes one period sooner.</p>"},{"h":"2. Present value and future value of an annuity","body":"<p>Instead of discounting each payment, use the shortcut formulas.</p><p><code>PV = PMT x [1 - (1 + r)^-n] / r</code></p><p><code>FV = PMT x [(1 + r)^n - 1] / r</code></p><p><strong>Example (PV):</strong> a supplier contract pays $1,000 at the end of each year for 5 years. At 8%: PV = 1,000 x (1 - 1.08^-5)/0.08 = $3,992.71. <strong>Example (FV):</strong> depositing $2,000 a year for 10 years at 6% builds $26,361.59.</p><p>For an annuity due, multiply by (1 + r): the same 5-year $1,000 stream paid at the beginning of each year is worth $4,312.13 today.</p><p><strong>Matching rule:</strong> if payments are monthly, use the monthly rate (APR/12) and the number of months. Mixing annual rates with monthly payments is the most common mistake. On a calculator set PMT, N, I/Y, and compute PV or FV; to switch to annuity due use the BGN mode.</p>"},{"h":"3. Loan payments and mortgages","body":"<p>A <strong>fully amortizing loan</strong> is paid off with equal payments. The loan amount is the <em>present value</em> of those payments, so we solve the PV-of-annuity formula for the payment:</p><p><code>PMT = PV x r / [1 - (1 + r)^-n]</code></p><p><strong>Car loan example:</strong> $25,000 at a 6% APR for 5 years, paid monthly. i = 0.06/12 = 0.5%, n = 60. PMT = $483.32 per month. In Excel: <code>=PMT(0.06/12, 60, -25000)</code>. If you enter the loan as positive, Excel returns a negative payment (cash-flow sign convention).</p><p><strong>Mortgage example:</strong> $300,000, 30 years, 6.5% APR: PMT = $1,896.20. Total paid over 360 months is $682,633, so interest is $382,633 - more than the house loan itself. Shorter loans have higher payments but far less total interest.</p><p>You can also solve for the number of payments (<code>=NPER</code>) or the rate (<code>=RATE</code>) when the other items are known.</p>"},{"h":"4. Amortization schedules: interest vs principal","body":"<p>An <strong>amortization schedule</strong> splits each payment into interest and principal.</p><ol><li><strong>Interest</strong> = periodic rate x beginning balance.</li><li><strong>Principal</strong> = payment - interest.</li><li><strong>Ending balance</strong> = beginning balance - principal.</li></ol><p><strong>Example:</strong> $25,000 car loan at 0.5% per month with a payment of $483.32.</p><ul><li>Month 1: interest = 25,000 x 0.005 = $125.00; principal = $358.32; balance = $24,641.68.</li><li>Month 2: interest = $123.21; principal = $360.11.</li></ul><p>Because interest is charged on a shrinking balance, the interest portion falls each month and the principal portion rises, while the total payment stays the same. At the end the balance is zero. The <strong>remaining balance</strong> at any time equals the PV of the remaining payments. On a 30-year mortgage the first payments are mostly interest ($1,000.00 of a $1,199.10 payment on $200,000 at 6%).</p>"},{"h":"5. Perpetuities and growing perpetuities","body":"<p>A <strong>perpetuity</strong> pays the same amount forever. Its value is <code>PV = C / r</code>. A scholarship endowment that pays $30,000 a year forever at 6% needs $500,000 today: the fund earns $30,000 each year and the principal never shrinks.</p><p>A <strong>growing perpetuity</strong> has cash flows that grow at rate g forever: <code>PV = C1 / (r - g)</code>, valid only if r is greater than g. <strong>C1 is next period's cash flow</strong>, not today's. If you are given today's cash flow C0, first compute C1 = C0 x (1 + g).</p><p><strong>Timing rule:</strong> both formulas value the stream <em>one period before the first cash flow</em>. If the first payment is at t = 3, the formula gives the value at t = 2; discount it two more years to get today's value. For a perpetuity whose first payment is today, add the payment to C/r.</p><p>Where it shows up: preferred stock (perpetuity), common stock with constant dividend growth (growing perpetuity, next unit).</p>"},{"h":"6. Uneven cash flow streams","body":"<p>Many real projects have different cash flows each year. The method is always the same: <strong>discount each cash flow separately, then add</strong>.</p><p><code>PV = CF1/(1+r) + CF2/(1+r)^2 + ... + CFn/(1+r)^n</code></p><p><strong>Example:</strong> a project returns $1,000, $2,000, and $3,000 at the end of years 1, 2, and 3 at a 10% rate: PV = 909.09 + 1,652.89 + 2,253.94 = $4,815.93.</p><p><strong>Mixed streams:</strong> if part of the stream is level, you can split it into an annuity piece plus individual lump sums. For a stream that has a deferred start (first payment later than year 1), value the annuity at one period before its first payment, then discount that value back to today.</p><p>On a calculator use the cash-flow (CF) worksheet and NPV function; in Excel, <code>=NPV(rate, range)</code> discounts the first value one period, so it assumes end-of-period flows.</p>"},{"h":"7. Retirement-savings style problems and solving for PMT, n, r","body":"<p>Retirement problems chain two annuities: an <strong>accumulation phase</strong> (FV of deposits) and a <strong>distribution phase</strong> (PV of withdrawals equals the balance at retirement).</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> save $500 a month for 25 years at a 6% APR: FV = $346,496.98. Then withdraw monthly for 20 years at the same rate: PMT = $2,482.41 per month.</p><p>To solve for an unknown:</p><ul><li><strong>Payment needed to reach a goal:</strong> <code>PMT = FV x r / [(1 + r)^n - 1]</code>. To have $100,000 in 8 years at 5%: $10,472.18 per year.</li><li><strong>Number of payments:</strong> <code>n = -ln(1 - PV x r / PMT) / ln(1 + r)</code>, or <code>=NPER</code>.</li><li><strong>Rate:</strong> usually requires a calculator or <code>=RATE</code>. It returns the periodic rate; multiply by the periods per year for APR.</li></ul><p>Checklist: (1) draw a timeline, (2) mark ordinary vs due, (3) match rate and period, (4) check the sign convention, (5) sanity-check the result.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["An annuity is a stream of equal payments at regular intervals. An ordinary annuity pays at the end of each period; an annuity due pays at the beginning.","The present value of an ordinary annuity equals payment times one minus one plus r to the negative n, all divided by r.","The future value of an ordinary annuity equals payment times one plus r to the n, minus one, divided by r.","To convert an ordinary annuity to an annuity due, multiply the present value or the future value by one plus r.","Match the rate and the period. Monthly payments need the monthly rate, which is the A P R divided by twelve, and the number of months.","A loan payment is found by treating the loan amount as the present value of the payments. Payment equals loan times r divided by one minus one plus r to the negative n.","In an amortization schedule, interest equals the periodic rate times the beginning balance. The principal portion is the payment minus interest, so interest falls and principal rises over time.","The remaining balance on a loan equals the present value of the remaining payments.","The present value of a perpetuity equals the cash flow divided by r. The present value of a growing perpetuity equals next year's cash flow divided by r minus g.","These formulas value the stream one period before the first cash flow. If the first payment is later, discount the result back to today.","For uneven cash flows, discount each cash flow separately and add them up. In Excel, PMT returns a negative number when the loan is entered as a positive number."],"examples":[{"q":"A company borrows $25,000 for equipment at a 6% APR with 60 monthly payments. (a) Find the monthly payment. (b) Split the first two payments into interest and principal. (c) What is the total interest over the life of the loan?","steps":["Monthly rate i = 6%/12 = 0.5%; n = 60.","(a) PMT = 25,000 x 0.005 / (1 - 1.005^-60) = $483.32. Excel: =PMT(0.005,60,-25000).","(b) Month 1 interest = 25,000 x 0.005 = $125.00; principal = $483.32 - 125.00 = $358.32; new balance = $24,641.68.","Month 2 interest = $24,641.68 x 0.005 = $123.21; principal = $360.11.","(c) Total paid = $483.32 x 60 = $28,999.20; interest = $3,999.20."],"answer":"(a) $483.32; (b) month 1: $125.00 interest, $358.32 principal; month 2: $123.21 interest, $360.11 principal; (c) total interest $3,999.20"},{"q":"An employee saves $600 at the end of each month for 30 years at a 6% APR compounded monthly. In retirement she wants equal monthly withdrawals for 25 years from that balance, still earning 6% APR. (a) What is the balance at retirement? (b) What is the monthly withdrawal?","steps":["Monthly rate i = 0.005.","(a) FV = 600 x [(1.005^360 - 1)/0.005] = $602,709.03.","(b) The balance is the PV of 300 withdrawals: PMT = FV x i / (1 - (1 + i)^-300) = $602,709.03 x 0.005 / (1 - 1.005^-300).","PMT = $3,883.26 per month.","Check: total deposited = $216,000; total withdrawn = $1,164,979; interest does the heavy lifting."],"answer":"(a) $602,709.03; (b) about $3,883.26 per month"},{"q":"A foundation will pay a scholarship of $40,000 at the end of each year, with a 5% required return. (a) How large must the endowment be if the scholarship lasts forever? (b) If the first scholarship is $40,000 next year and it grows 2% annually forever, how large must the endowment be? (c) What is it worth if the scholarship lasts only 10 years (level $40,000)?","steps":["(a) Perpetuity: PV = C / r = 40,000 / 0.05 = $800,000.","(b) Growing perpetuity: PV = C1 / (r - g) = 40,000 / (0.05 - 0.02) = $1,333,333.","(c) 10-year annuity: PV = 40,000 x (1 - 1.05^-10)/0.05 = $308,869.","Notice a 10-year annuity is worth much less than the perpetuity, because payments after year 10 are missing."],"answer":"(a) $800,000; (b) $1,333,333; (c) $308,869"}],"videos":[{"title":"Present value and future value of annuities explained","query":"present value future value of annuity explained ordinary annuity annuity due"},{"title":"Loan amortization schedule interest vs principal","query":"loan amortization schedule explained interest principal PMT Excel"},{"title":"Perpetuity and growing perpetuity valuation","query":"perpetuity growing perpetuity present value formula explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Annuity","b":"Equal payments at regular intervals for a fixed number of periods."},{"f":"Ordinary annuity","b":"Payments at the end of each period (first at t = 1)."},{"f":"Annuity due","b":"Payments at the beginning of each period (first at t = 0)."},{"f":"PV of ordinary annuity","b":"PMT x [1 - (1 + r)^-n] / r"},{"f":"FV of ordinary annuity","b":"PMT x [(1 + r)^n - 1] / r"},{"f":"Convert ordinary to due","b":"Multiply PV or FV by (1 + r)."},{"f":"Loan payment formula","b":"PMT = PV x r / [1 - (1 + r)^-n]"},{"f":"Amortization: interest each period","b":"Periodic rate x beginning balance."},{"f":"Amortization: principal each period","b":"Payment minus interest for that period."},{"f":"Pattern in amortization","b":"Interest portion falls, principal portion rises, payment stays equal."},{"f":"Remaining loan balance","b":"PV of the remaining payments."},{"f":"Perpetuity PV","b":"C / r"},{"f":"Growing perpetuity PV","b":"C1 / (r - g), with r > g."},{"f":"C1 vs C0","b":"C1 = C0 x (1 + g); use next year's cash flow in the formula."},{"f":"Perpetuity timing rule","b":"Formula values the stream one period before the first cash flow."},{"f":"Uneven cash flow stream","b":"Discount each cash flow separately, then add."},{"f":"Excel =PMT","b":"=PMT(rate, nper, pv); returns negative if pv is positive."},{"f":"Excel =NPER and =RATE","b":"Solve for number of payments or periodic rate."},{"f":"Mortgage inputs (monthly)","b":"rate = APR/12, nper = years x 12."},{"f":"Deferred annuity","b":"Value at one period before first payment, then discount back."},{"f":"Payment needed for savings goal","b":"PMT = FV x r / [(1 + r)^n - 1]"}],"questions":[{"q":"A company pays a vendor $5,000 at the END of each month for 12 months. What type of cash flow is this?","o":["An ordinary annuity","An annuity due","A perpetuity","An uneven cash flow stream"],"a":0,"e":"Equal payments at regular intervals are an annuity; because payments occur at the end of each period it is an ordinary annuity. An annuity due would pay at the beginning of each period.","d":1},{"q":"Compared with an otherwise identical ordinary annuity, an annuity due has which effect on present value and future value?","o":["Both are lower, by a factor of (1 + r)","Present value is higher but future value is lower","Both are unchanged because the payments are the same size","Both are higher, by a factor of (1 + r)"],"a":3,"e":"Each annuity-due payment arrives one period earlier, so it has one more period to earn interest (FV) and is discounted one fewer period (PV). Multiply the ordinary-annuity value by (1 + r).","d":1},{"q":"A charitable trust will pay $500 per year forever, and the appropriate discount rate is 5%. What is the present value of this perpetuity?","o":["$25","$10,000","$476","$9,524"],"a":1,"e":"Perpetuity PV = C / r = 500 / 0.05 = $10,000. Multiplying by r gives an annual interest figure, not a value.","d":1},{"q":"What is the present value of a 5-year ordinary annuity of $1,000 per year if the discount rate is 8%?","o":["$5,866.60","$4,312.13","$5,000.00","$3,992.71"],"a":3,"e":"PV = 1,000 x [1 - 1.08^-5] / 0.08 = $3,992.71. $5,866.60 is the FUTURE value; $4,312.13 is the annuity-due value.","d":1},{"q":"You deposit $2,000 at the end of each year for 10 years into an account earning 6% compounded annually. What is the balance right after the last deposit?","o":["$14,720.17","$26,361.59","$20,000.00","$27,943.29"],"a":1,"e":"FV = 2,000 x [(1.06^10 - 1) / 0.06] = $26,361.59. $14,720.17 is the present value; $27,943.29 treats the deposits as beginning-of-year.","d":1},{"q":"In Excel you enter =PMT(0.005, 60, 20000) to compute a car-loan payment. Why does the result appear as a negative number?","o":["The loan balance is shrinking, so the payment is -$386.66 in error","A negative sign means the interest rate was entered incorrectly","Excel treats the loan proceeds as cash received (positive) and the payments as cash paid out (negative); the result is -$386.66","Excel always returns negative values for any function that has a rate argument"],"a":2,"e":"PMT = 20,000 x 0.005 / (1 - 1.005^-60) = $386.66, shown as -$386.66 because of the cash-flow sign convention. Enter the loan as -20000 for a positive payment.","d":1},{"q":"As a fully amortizing loan is paid down, what happens to the interest and principal portions of each equal payment?","o":["Both portions stay constant","The interest portion falls and the principal portion rises","The interest portion rises and the principal portion falls","Both portions fall until the loan is repaid"],"a":1,"e":"Interest each period = rate x beginning balance. The balance shrinks, so interest shrinks. With a constant total payment, the principal part must grow.","d":1},{"q":"Which of the following is the best example of a perpetuity?","o":["A 30-year fixed-rate mortgage payment","A 5-year car lease payment","A fixed dividend on preferred stock that is expected to continue indefinitely","A 10-year bond's face-value repayment"],"a":2,"e":"A perpetuity is a level cash flow that continues forever, such as a fixed preferred dividend or a perpetual endowment payout. The other choices all end after a finite number of periods.","d":1},{"q":"You borrow $25,000 for a car at a 6% APR with monthly payments for 5 years. What is the monthly payment?","o":["$416.67","$483.32","$541.67","$125.00"],"a":1,"e":"i = 0.5% per month, n = 60. PMT = 25,000 x 0.005 / (1 - 1.005^-60) = $483.32. $416.67 ignores interest; $125.00 is only the first month's interest.","d":2},{"q":"A buyer finances $300,000 with a 30-year mortgage at a 6.5% APR, payments monthly. What is the monthly payment (principal and interest)?","o":["$833.33","$1,625.00","$2,613.32","$1,896.20"],"a":3,"e":"i = 0.065/12 = 0.54167% per month, n = 360. PMT = $1,896.20. $833.33 has no interest; $1,625.00 is interest only; $2,613.32 would be the 15-year payment.","d":2},{"q":"A $200,000 30-year mortgage at a 6% APR has a monthly payment of $1,199.10. How much of the FIRST payment reduces principal?","o":["$1,199.10","$1,000.00","$199.10","$599.55"],"a":2,"e":"First-month interest = 0.005 x 200,000 = $1,000.00. Principal = $1,199.10 - 1,000.00 = $199.10. Early payments are mostly interest.","d":2},{"q":"You save $400 at the end of each month for 30 years in an account earning a 7% APR compounded monthly. What is the balance at the end of 30 years?","o":["$144,000.00","$453,411.77","$490,835.00","$487,988.40"],"a":3,"e":"i = 0.07/12, n = 360. FV = 400 x [((1 + i)^360 - 1)/i] = $487,988.40. $144,000.00 ignores interest; $453,411.77 uses annual deposits and compounding; the last choice treats deposits as beginning-of-month.","d":2},{"q":"A lease requires 5 annual payments of $1,000 made at the BEGINNING of each year. If the discount rate is 8%, what is the present value of the lease payments?","o":["$4,312.13","$3,992.71","$5,000.00","$3,696.95"],"a":0,"e":"Annuity due = ordinary annuity x (1 + r) = $3,992.71 x 1.08 = $4,312.13. Dividing by 1.08 moves the value the wrong way.","d":2},{"q":"A lottery winner can take $600,000 today or $50,000 at the end of each year for 20 years. The winner's discount rate is 5%. Which is worth more in present value terms?","o":["The lump sum, because the payments are worth only $376,889 in PV","They are equal because time value of money does not apply to annuities","The lump sum, because $600,000 is more than $50,000","The 20 payments, with a PV of about $623,111"],"a":3,"e":"PV of the payments = 50,000 x [1 - 1.05^-20]/0.05 = $623,111 > $600,000. The $376,889 option discounts the $1,000,000 total as one lump, which is wrong.","d":2},{"q":"A donor wants to fund a scholarship that pays $30,000 at the end of every year forever. If the endowment earns 6%, how much must be donated today?","o":["$500,000","$1,800","$28,302","$900,000"],"a":0,"e":"PV of a perpetuity = C / r = 30,000 / 0.06 = $500,000. The endowment's earnings (6% of 500,000 = 30,000) pay the scholarship each year while the principal stays intact.","d":2},{"q":"A firm expects a cash flow of $100,000 one year from now, growing by 3% each year forever. If the discount rate is 8%, what is the value of the stream today?","o":["$2,060,000","$2,000,000","$1,250,000","$909,091"],"a":1,"e":"Growing perpetuity = C1 / (r - g) = 100,000 / 0.05 = $2,000,000. The $100,000 is already next year's cash flow (C1), so do not multiply by 1.03. Dividing by r alone ignores growth.","d":2},{"q":"An investment pays $1,000 at the end of year 1, $2,000 at the end of year 2, and $3,000 at the end of year 3. At a 10% discount rate, what is its present value?","o":["$4,507.89","$5,297.52","$4,815.93","$6,000.00"],"a":2,"e":"Discount each flow separately: 909.09 + 1,652.89 + 2,253.94 = $4,815.93. An uneven stream is not an annuity, and discounting the total at the last date understates value ($4,507.89).","d":2},{"q":"You want $100,000 in 8 years. You will make equal deposits at the end of each year into an account earning 5% compounded annually. How large must each deposit be?","o":["$12,500.00","$9,973.51","$15,472.18","$10,472.18"],"a":3,"e":"Solve FV annuity for PMT: PMT = FV x r / [(1 + r)^n - 1] = 100,000 x 0.05 / (1.05^8 - 1) = $10,472.18. $15,472.18 uses the loan (PV) formula by mistake, and $9,973.51 is the beginning-of-year deposit.","d":2},{"q":"You borrow $10,000 at a 12% APR compounded monthly and pay $200 per month. About how many monthly payments are needed to pay off the loan?","o":["50.0 payments","About 69.7 payments","56.0 payments","83.3 payments"],"a":1,"e":"n = -ln(1 - PV x i / PMT) / ln(1 + i) = -ln(1 - 10,000 x 0.01 / 200)/ln(1.01) = -ln(0.5)/ln(1.01) = 69.7. Dividing 10,000 by 200 gives 50 but ignores interest.","d":2},{"q":"When using Excel's PMT function for a 30-year mortgage with a 6% APR and monthly payments, which rate and nper arguments are correct?","o":["rate = 0.06/12 (0.005) and nper = 360","rate = 0.06 and nper = 30","rate = 0.06 and nper = 360","rate = 0.06/12 and nper = 30"],"a":0,"e":"Rate and period must match. Monthly payments need a monthly rate (APR/12 = 0.005) and a monthly count of periods (30 x 12 = 360). Mixing annual and monthly units is the most common error.","d":2},{"q":"You invest $1,000 at the beginning of each year for 5 years at 8%. What is the balance at the end of year 5?","o":["$6,335.93","$5,866.60","$5,000.00","$5,432.04"],"a":0,"e":"FV of annuity due = $5,866.60 x 1.08 = $6,335.93. Each deposit earns one extra year of interest relative to an ordinary annuity.","d":2},{"q":"A worker saves $500 at the end of each month for 25 years at a 6% APR (monthly compounding). She then withdraws equal monthly amounts for 20 years at the same rate, ending with a zero balance. What is the approximate monthly withdrawal?","o":["$2,482.41","$1,443.74","$625.00","$2,232.48"],"a":0,"e":"Phase 1: FV = 500 x [(1.005^300 - 1)/0.005] = $346,496.98. Phase 2: PMT = $346,496.98 x 0.005 / (1 - 1.005^-240) = $2,482.41. Dividing by 240 ignores interest earned during retirement.","d":3},{"q":"An investor will receive $10,000 at the end of each year for 5 years, with the first payment arriving at the end of year 4. At an 8% discount rate, what is the value today?","o":["$39,927.10","$31,695.42","$29,347.61","$50,296.65"],"a":1,"e":"The ordinary-annuity formula values the stream one period before the first payment, i.e., at t = 3: $39,927.10. Discount that back 3 more years: $39,927.10 / 1.08^3 = $31,695.42. Using 4 years of discounting double-counts a period.","d":3},{"q":"A $200,000 30-year mortgage at a 6% APR has a payment of $1,199.10. What is the remaining loan balance immediately after the 60th payment?","o":["$186,108.71","$128,053.94","$188,053.94","$166,666.67"],"a":0,"e":"Remaining balance = PV of the remaining 300 payments = $1,199.10 x [1 - 1.005^-300]/0.005 = $186,108.71. Subtracting total payments ($128,053.94) ignores that early payments are mostly interest.","d":3},{"q":"A project will generate cash flows that begin at the end of year 3 at $2,000,000, then grow 3% per year forever. If the discount rate is 8%, what is the present value today?","o":["$34,293,553","$40,000,000","$31,753,290","$21,433,471"],"a":0,"e":"Growing perpetuity formula C / (r - g) values the stream one period before the first cash flow, at t = 2: 2,000,000/0.05 = $40,000,000. Discount back 2 years: $34,293,553. Using 3 years would discount one period too many.","d":3},{"q":"A $250,000 30-year mortgage at a 6% APR has monthly payments of $1,498.88. What is the total interest paid over the life of the loan?","o":["$450,000","$539,595","$250,000","$289,595"],"a":3,"e":"Total paid = $1,498.88 x 360 = $539,595. Interest = total paid - principal = $289,595. Simple interest on the original balance ($450,000) overstates it because the balance falls over time.","d":3},{"q":"A buyer borrows $200,000 and can choose a 15-year loan at 5.5% APR or a 30-year loan at 6% APR (both monthly payments). Which statement is correct about total interest paid?","o":["The 30-year loan costs about $137,526 less in total interest","Both loans cost the same total interest because the principal is the same","The 15-year loan costs about $137,526 less in total interest ($94,150 versus $231,676)","The 15-year loan costs more total interest because its monthly payment is higher"],"a":2,"e":"15-year: payment $1,634.17, interest $94,150. 30-year: payment $1,199.10, interest $231,676. A higher payment pays principal off faster and sharply cuts interest.","d":3},{"q":"You borrow $18,000 and repay it with 48 end-of-month payments of $450. Which is the approximate APR (nominal annual rate, monthly compounding)?","o":["0.77%","5.00%","9.24%","9.64%"],"a":2,"e":"Solve the annuity equation for the monthly rate (Excel =RATE(48,-450,18000)): i = 0.770%. APR = 12 x i = 9.24%. 0.77% is the monthly rate, the add-on rate 5.00% ignores amortization, and 9.64% is the EAR.","d":3},{"q":"A 36-month equipment lease requires payments of $1,500 at the BEGINNING of each month. The lessee's discount rate is a 9% APR compounded monthly. What is the present value of the lease payments?","o":["$47,170.21","$54,000","$47,523.98","$46,819.06"],"a":2,"e":"Monthly rate 0.75%, n = 36. Ordinary-annuity PV = $47,170.21; annuity due multiplies by 1.0075 = $47,523.98.","d":3},{"q":"A trust will pay $1,000 per year forever, with the FIRST payment made today (time 0). The discount rate is 5%. What is the present value of the stream?","o":["$20,000","$21,000","$19,047.62","$22,050"],"a":1,"e":"A perpetuity formula C/r values payments starting one year out: 1,000/0.05 = 20,000. Add the payment received today: 21,000 (equivalent to 20,000 x 1.05). Using 1,000/0.05/1.05 moves the value in the wrong direction.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Ordinary annuity","Payments at end of each period"],["Annuity due","Payments at start of each period"],["Perpetuity","Level payments continuing forever"],["Amortization","Paying a loan down with equal installments"],["Growing perpetuity","Payments growing at g forever"],["Remaining balance","PV of the remaining payments"],["PMT function","Excel loan or annuity payment calculator"],["Uneven cash flows","Discount each payment separately"]],"traps":["Trap: Use C0 in the growing-perpetuity formula. Truth: The formula needs C1 = C0 x (1 + g), next period's cash flow.","Trap: Use the annual rate with monthly payments. Truth: Use APR/12 and the number of months so rate and period match.","Trap: Annuity due is worth less because payments start sooner. Truth: Earlier payments make an annuity due worth MORE; multiply by (1 + r).","Trap: Remaining balance equals loan minus payments made. Truth: Early payments are mostly interest; balance is the PV of the remaining payments.","Trap: A deferred annuity is discounted by its starting year. Truth: Value it one period before the first payment, then discount back from there."],"mnemonic":"Perpetuity timing: 'The formula looks ONE period BACK from the first cash flow.' First payment at year 3 means the answer sits at year 2, so discount two more years.","cram":["Ordinary annuity: end payments; annuity due: beginning, multiply by (1 + r).","PV annuity = PMT[1 - (1+r)^-n]/r; FV annuity = PMT[(1+r)^n - 1]/r.","Loan payment = PV r / [1 - (1+r)^-n]; monthly means APR/12 and months.","Interest = rate x beginning balance; interest falls, principal rises.","Remaining balance = PV of remaining payments.","Perpetuity PV = C/r; growing = C1/(r - g), with C1 = C0(1+g).","Formulas value the stream one period before the first cash flow.","Uneven flows: discount each separately; Excel PMT returns a negative sign."],"ytv":[{"title":"Present Value of Ordinary Annuity and Annuity Due. Simplified","channel":"Farhat Lectures. The # 1 CPA & Accounting Courses","id":"hown_g7W9vI","why":"Shows the ordinary vs annuity due formulas side by side."},{"title":"Ordinary Annuity vs Annuity Due","channel":"Edspira","id":"rViT_ODg5Yk","why":"Short, clear explanation of timing differences and the (1+r) link."},{"title":"Present Value of an Annuity DUE (**IMPORTANT**)","channel":"Professor Ikram","id":"b1SCkPkR8dA","why":"Worked annuity-due example with calculator steps."}]},{"id":"u7","title":"Interest Rates & Bond Valuation","day":4,"summary":"Price bonds as the present value of coupons plus face value, understand yields, premium and discount pricing, interest rate and reinvestment risk, credit ratings, the yield curve, and the Fisher equation.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Bond basics and vocabulary","body":"<p>A <strong>bond</strong> is a loan: the investor lends money to a corporation or government, which promises fixed interest payments and repayment of principal. Key terms:</p><ul><li><strong>Par (face) value:</strong> the amount repaid at maturity, usually $1,000.</li><li><strong>Coupon rate:</strong> the stated annual interest rate, applied to par value.</li><li><strong>Coupon payment:</strong> coupon rate x par. A 6% bond pays $60 a year (or $30 every six months).</li><li><strong>Maturity:</strong> the date the principal is repaid.</li><li><strong>Yield to maturity (YTM):</strong> the annual return if you buy at today's price and hold to maturity; it is the discount rate that makes PV of all cash flows equal the price.</li><li><strong>Current yield:</strong> annual coupon / current price (ignores capital gain or loss).</li></ul><p>The coupon rate is fixed when the bond is issued. The market price and YTM change every day as interest rates move.</p>"},{"h":"2. Pricing a bond: PV of coupons plus PV of face","body":"<p>Bond price = <strong>present value of the coupons (an annuity)</strong> + <strong>present value of the face value (a lump sum)</strong>, discounted at the YTM:</p><p><code>Price = C x [1 - (1 + y)^-n]/y + Face / (1 + y)^n</code></p><p><strong>Example:</strong> a 5-year bond with a 6% annual coupon ($60) and face $1,000, when the market yield is 8%.</p><ul><li>PV of coupons = 60 x annuity(5, 8%) = $239.56</li><li>PV of face = 1,000 / 1.08^5 = $680.58</li><li>Price = <strong>$920.15</strong> (a discount, because the coupon is below the market yield)</li></ul><p>Calculator: N = 5, I/Y = 8, PMT = 60, FV = 1000, compute PV (shows negative, the amount you pay). Excel: <code>=PV(0.08,5,60,1000)</code>.</p>"},{"h":"3. Semiannual bonds","body":"<p>Most U.S. corporate bonds pay coupons <strong>twice a year</strong>. Adjust three inputs: the coupon is half the annual coupon, the yield per period is YTM/2, and the number of periods is years x 2.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> 10-year, 8% coupon, face $1,000, YTM 6% (APR, semiannual): PMT = $40, i = 3%, n = 20. Price = $1,148.77.</p><p>The quoted YTM on a semiannual bond is a nominal rate (periodic yield x 2), so the effective annual yield is a little higher. If you solve for the YTM from a price, you find the semiannual rate first and then double it (the \"bond equivalent yield\").</p><p>Common error: using 10 periods and 6% for a bond that pays semiannually. Always match payments, rate, and periods.</p>"},{"h":"4. Premium, par, discount, and the price-yield relationship","body":"<p>Compare the coupon rate with the market yield:</p><ul><li>Coupon rate &gt; YTM: price &gt; par (<strong>premium</strong>).</li><li>Coupon rate = YTM: price = par.</li><li>Coupon rate &lt; YTM: price &lt; par (<strong>discount</strong>).</li></ul><p>Bond prices and yields move in <strong>opposite directions</strong>. When market rates rise, existing lower-coupon bonds become less attractive and their prices fall until their YTM matches the market. The relationship is convex (curved): price gains from a yield fall are bigger than losses from an equal yield rise.</p><p><strong>Pull to par:</strong> if yields don't change, a premium or discount bond's price moves gradually toward $1,000 at maturity. A 3-year, 7% bond at a 5% yield is worth $1,054.46 today and $1,037.19 one year later.</p><p>Relationship for a discount bond: coupon rate &lt; current yield &lt; YTM. For a premium bond the order reverses.</p>"},{"h":"5. Interest rate risk and reinvestment risk","body":"<p><strong>Interest rate (price) risk</strong> is the risk that bond prices fall when rates rise. It is larger for bonds with:</p><ul><li><strong>Longer maturity</strong> (more distant cash flows are discounted more strongly), and</li><li><strong>Lower coupon rates</strong> (more of the value is in the far-off face value).</li></ul><p>A zero-coupon bond has the most interest rate risk for its maturity. Example: if yields rise from 6% to 7%, a 20-year 4% bond drops 11.5% while a 5-year 8% bond drops only 4.0%.</p><p><strong>Reinvestment risk</strong> is the risk that coupons and maturing principal must be reinvested at lower rates. It is larger for short-term bonds and high-coupon bonds, and zero-coupon bonds held to maturity have none. The two risks offset: a long-term bond has high price risk and low reinvestment risk, a short-term bond the reverse.</p>"},{"h":"6. Zero-coupon, callable, and convertible bonds","body":"<ul><li><strong>Zero-coupon bonds</strong> pay no coupons and sell at a discount: Price = Face / (1 + y)^n. A 10-year zero at 5% is $613.91. The accrued discount may be taxable annually (OID) even though no cash is received.</li><li><strong>Callable bonds</strong> let the issuer redeem early at a call price. Issuers call when rates fall. Investors demand a higher yield, and use <strong>yield to call (YTC)</strong> as a conservative measure for premium bonds. Price appreciation is capped (price compression).</li><li><strong>Convertible bonds</strong> can be exchanged for a fixed number of shares (conversion ratio). Conversion value = ratio x stock price. They offer a lower coupon because of the equity option.</li><li><strong>Putable bonds</strong> let the investor sell back to the issuer.</li></ul><p>Features that favor the issuer (call) raise yield; features that favor the investor (put, convertible) lower yield.</p>"},{"h":"7. What drives rates: Fisher, risk premiums, ratings, and the yield curve","body":"<p><strong>Nominal vs real (Fisher):</strong> (1 + nominal) = (1 + real) x (1 + inflation). Approximately, nominal = real + inflation. A 9% nominal rate with 4% inflation gives an exact real rate of 4.81%.</p><p><strong>Components of a nominal rate:</strong> r = r* (real risk-free) + IP (inflation premium) + DRP (default risk premium) + LP (liquidity premium) + MRP (maturity risk premium). Treasuries have essentially zero DRP and LP.</p><p><strong>Credit ratings:</strong> agencies like S&amp;P and Moody's grade default risk. S&amp;P: AAA, AA, A, BBB are <em>investment grade</em>; BB and below are speculative ('junk'). Lower ratings mean a higher default risk premium and a higher yield.</p><p><strong>Term structure / yield curve</strong> plots yield against maturity for similar bonds. Normal = upward sloping, inverted = downward sloping, flat. Theories: <em>expectations</em> (long rates are averages of expected short rates), <em>liquidity/maturity risk premium</em> (adds a premium for longer bonds), and <em>market segmentation</em> (supply and demand in each maturity range).</p>"}],"keyPoints":["A bond pays a fixed coupon equal to the coupon rate times face value, and repays face value at maturity.","Bond price equals the present value of the coupons, which is an annuity, plus the present value of the face value, discounted at the yield to maturity.","For a semiannual bond, divide the annual coupon and the yield by two, and multiply the years by two.","If the coupon rate is above the yield to maturity, the bond sells at a premium. If it is below, the bond sells at a discount. If equal, it sells at par.","Bond prices and yields move in opposite directions. When market rates rise, bond prices fall.","Interest rate risk is greater for bonds with longer maturity and lower coupons. Zero-coupon bonds have the most interest rate risk for their maturity.","Reinvestment risk is the risk that coupons and principal must be reinvested at lower rates. Short-term bonds have more reinvestment risk.","Current yield equals the annual coupon divided by the current price. Yield to maturity also includes the gain or loss as the price moves to par.","A zero-coupon bond pays no coupons and is priced as face value divided by one plus y, raised to the n.","A callable bond lets the issuer redeem early, so investors demand a higher yield and price appreciation is capped. A convertible bond can be exchanged for shares.","The Fisher equation says one plus the nominal rate equals one plus the real rate times one plus inflation.","The nominal rate equals the real risk-free rate plus premiums for inflation, default risk, liquidity, and maturity risk. Lower credit ratings carry higher yields. An inverted yield curve means short rates exceed long rates."],"examples":[{"q":"A 10-year bond has a $1,000 face value and a 7% annual coupon. The market yield is 9%. (a) What is the price? (b) Is it a premium or discount bond? (c) If the yield stays at 9%, what is the price one year later?","steps":["Coupon = 0.07 x 1,000 = $70; n = 10; y = 9%.","(a) PV of coupons = 70 x annuity(10, 9%) = $449.24; PV of face = 1,000/1.09^10 = $422.41. Price = $871.65.","(b) Coupon (7%) < yield (9%), so the bond trades at a discount.","(c) Nine years remain: price = $880.10. The discount shrinks as maturity approaches (pull to par)."],"answer":"(a) $871.65; (b) discount; (c) $880.10"},{"q":"A 15-year bond with a $1,000 face value pays an 8% coupon semiannually. The market yield is 10% (APR, semiannual). (a) Find the price. (b) Compute the current yield. (c) Is YTM greater or less than current yield?","steps":["Semiannual: PMT = $40, i = 5%, n = 30.","(a) Price = 40 x annuity(30, 5%) + 1,000/1.05^30 = $846.28. Calculator: N=30, I/Y=5, PMT=40, FV=1000, CPT PV.","(b) Current yield = annual coupon / price = 80 / 846.28 = 9.45%.","(c) This is a discount bond, so coupon rate (8%) < current yield < YTM (10%)."],"answer":"(a) $846.28; (b) 9.45%; (c) YTM (10%) is greater than current yield"},{"q":"A 5-year bond with an 8% annual coupon and $1,000 face value is selling for $1,050. (a) Find the YTM. (b) If expected inflation is 3%, find the real yield. (c) Will the YTM be above or below 8%, and why?","steps":["Solve 1,050 = 80 x annuity(5, y) + 1,000/(1+y)^5. Calculator: N=5, PV=-1050, PMT=80, FV=1000, CPT I/Y; Excel =RATE(5,80,-1050,1000).","(a) y = 6.79%.","(b) Real = (1 + 0.0679)/(1.03) - 1 = 3.68%. The approximation (nominal - inflation) gives 3.79%.","(c) The price is above par (premium), so the YTM is below the 8% coupon rate."],"answer":"(a) 6.79%; (b) about 3.68%; (c) below 8% because it is a premium bond"}],"videos":[{"title":"Bond valuation and yield to maturity explained","query":"bond valuation price yield to maturity explained step by step"},{"title":"Interest rate risk and bond duration basics","query":"bond interest rate risk maturity coupon price sensitivity explained"},{"title":"Yield curve and term structure of interest rates","query":"yield curve term structure expectations liquidity preference explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Par (face) value","b":"Amount repaid at maturity, usually $1,000."},{"f":"Coupon payment","b":"Coupon rate x par value."},{"f":"Yield to maturity (YTM)","b":"Discount rate that equates PV of cash flows to price."},{"f":"Current yield","b":"Annual coupon divided by current price."},{"f":"Bond price formula","b":"PV of coupons (annuity) + PV of face (lump sum)."},{"f":"Semiannual bond adjustments","b":"Coupon/2, yield/2, years x 2."},{"f":"Premium bond","b":"Coupon rate > YTM; price above par."},{"f":"Discount bond","b":"Coupon rate < YTM; price below par."},{"f":"Price-yield relationship","b":"Inverse: yields up, prices down."},{"f":"Discount bond yield order","b":"Coupon rate < current yield < YTM."},{"f":"Pull to par","b":"Premium or discount prices move toward par at maturity."},{"f":"Interest rate risk drivers","b":"Longer maturity and lower coupon mean larger price swings."},{"f":"Reinvestment risk","b":"Risk of reinvesting coupons/principal at lower rates."},{"f":"Zero-coupon price","b":"Face / (1 + y)^n"},{"f":"Callable bond","b":"Issuer can redeem early; investors want higher yield; YTC."},{"f":"Convertible bond","b":"Exchangeable for shares; conversion value = ratio x stock price."},{"f":"Fisher equation","b":"(1 + nominal) = (1 + real)(1 + inflation)."},{"f":"Components of nominal rate","b":"r* + IP + DRP + LP + MRP"},{"f":"Investment grade (S&P)","b":"AAA to BBB-; BB+ and below is junk."},{"f":"Inverted yield curve","b":"Short-term yields exceed long-term yields."},{"f":"Expectations theory","b":"Long rates equal the average of expected short rates."}],"questions":[{"q":"A bond has a $1,000 face value and a 6% annual coupon rate. How much does the bondholder receive in coupon interest each year?","o":["$600","$60","$6","$1,060"],"a":1,"e":"Coupon payment = coupon rate x face value = 0.06 x 1,000 = $60 per year. The coupon is based on face (par) value, not market price.","d":1},{"q":"A bond with an 8% coupon rate is trading in a market where similar bonds yield 6%. At which price will the bond trade?","o":["At a discount (below face value)","At a premium (above face value)","At exactly face value","At face value minus the coupon"],"a":1,"e":"The bond pays more than the market requires (8% vs 6%), so investors bid its price above par until the yield to maturity falls to 6%.","d":1},{"q":"If market interest rates rise, what happens to the price of an existing fixed-rate bond?","o":["The price rises","The price stays the same because the coupon is fixed","The coupon rate rises automatically","The price falls"],"a":3,"e":"Bond prices and yields move inversely. The existing coupon is fixed, so the price must fall until the bond's yield matches the new higher market rate.","d":1},{"q":"A bond pays an $80 annual coupon and currently trades at $950. What is its current yield?","o":["8.00%","8.42%","11.88%","9.00%"],"a":1,"e":"Current yield = annual coupon / current price = 80 / 950 = 8.42%. It ignores the capital gain at maturity. The 8% is the coupon rate (coupon over face).","d":1},{"q":"Using S&P-style ratings, which is the lowest rating that is still considered investment grade?","o":["BB+","A-","B","BBB-"],"a":3,"e":"Investment grade runs from AAA down to BBB-. BB+ and below is speculative ('junk') grade, with higher default risk and higher yields.","d":1},{"q":"A bond has a nominal yield of 7% and expected inflation is 3%. Using the approximate Fisher relationship, what is the real rate of return?","o":["About 4%","About 10%","About 3%","About 7%"],"a":0,"e":"Approximate Fisher effect: real rate is about nominal minus inflation = 7% - 3% = 4%. The exact formula is (1.07/1.03) - 1 = 3.88%.","d":1},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes a zero-coupon bond?","o":["It pays a coupon each year and returns face value at maturity","It pays no periodic interest and sells at a discount to face value; the investor's return comes from price appreciation to par","It always sells at a premium to face value","It has no maturity date"],"a":1,"e":"A zero-coupon bond is priced as the PV of its face value only. It sells at a deep discount and accrues value until it matures at par.","d":1},{"q":"What does an inverted yield curve show?","o":["Short-term yields are higher than long-term yields","Long-term yields are higher than short-term yields","All maturities have the same yield","Yields are negative at every maturity"],"a":0,"e":"An inverted curve slopes downward: short-maturity yields exceed long-maturity yields. It has often preceded economic slowdowns.","d":1},{"q":"A 5-year bond has a $1,000 face value and a 6% annual coupon. If the yield to maturity is 8%, what is the price?","o":["$239.56","$920.15","$680.58","$1,089.04"],"a":1,"e":"Price = 60 x [1 - 1.08^-5]/0.08 + 1,000/1.08^5 = $239.56 + $680.58 = $920.15. The first two options each leave out part of the cash flows; the last uses a yield that is too low.","d":2},{"q":"A 10-year bond with a $1,000 face value pays an 8% coupon semiannually. The yield to maturity is 6% (APR, semiannual). What is the price?","o":["$1,148.77","$1,147.20","$1,000.00","$875.38"],"a":0,"e":"Semiannual: PMT = $40, i = 3%, n = 20. Price = $1,148.77. Treating the bond as annual gives $1,147.20; $875.38 is what you would get at a 10% YTM.","d":2},{"q":"A zero-coupon bond has a $1,000 face value and matures in 10 years. If the yield to maturity is 5% (annual compounding), what is the price?","o":["$500.00","$783.53","$1,628.89","$613.91"],"a":3,"e":"Price = 1,000 / 1.05^10 = $613.91. There are no coupons, so only the face value is discounted.","d":2},{"q":"A company's bond has a 7% coupon rate (paid annually), and the market yield for bonds of equal risk is also 7%. What is the price of the bond?","o":["Above face value (premium)","Below face value (discount)","Exactly its face value (par)","It cannot be determined without knowing the maturity"],"a":2,"e":"When coupon rate equals yield to maturity, the PV of coupons plus face equals face value at any maturity. The bond sells at par.","d":2},{"q":"Bond A is a 20-year bond with a 4% annual coupon. Bond B is a 5-year bond with an 8% annual coupon. Both have $1,000 face value and currently yield 6%. If yields rise to 7%, which bond's price falls by the larger percentage?","o":["Bond B falls about 11.5% versus about 4.0% for Bond A","Both fall by the same percentage because both yield 6%","Bond A falls about 11.5% versus about 4.0% for Bond B","Neither falls, because coupon payments are fixed"],"a":2,"e":"Longer maturity and lower coupon mean more interest rate risk. Bond A: $770.60 to $682.18 (-11.5%). Bond B: $1,084.25 to $1,041.00 (-4.0%).","d":2},{"q":"A bond's nominal yield is 9% and expected inflation is 4%. What is the exact real rate of return using the Fisher equation?","o":["5.00%","13.36%","4.81%","4.00%"],"a":2,"e":"(1 + nominal) = (1 + real)(1 + inflation), so real = 1.09/1.04 - 1 = 4.81%. The 5% answer is the approximation; 13.36% multiplies instead of divides.","d":2},{"q":"According to the pure expectations theory of the term structure, what does an upward-sloping yield curve imply?","o":["Investors expect short-term interest rates to fall","Investors expect short-term rates to stay constant","Long-term bonds have no risk","Investors expect short-term interest rates to rise in the future"],"a":3,"e":"Under the expectations theory, long-term yields are averages of expected future short-term rates. A rising curve means short rates are expected to rise. (The liquidity/maturity risk premium theory adds a premium on top.)","d":2},{"q":"An investor buys a 10-year bond for $1,080 when its coupon rate exceeds the market yield. If the yield to maturity does not change, what will the price do over time?","o":["It will gradually decline toward $1,000 as maturity approaches","It will gradually increase above $1,080","It will stay at $1,080 until maturity","It will drop to $0 at maturity"],"a":0,"e":"A premium bond's price is pulled to par by maturity (pull-to-par). The capital loss offsets the above-market coupon, leaving a return equal to the YTM.","d":2},{"q":"A 10-year corporate bond's nominal yield is built from: real risk-free rate 2.0%, inflation premium 3.0%, default risk premium 1.0%, liquidity premium 0.5%, and maturity risk premium 1.5%. What is the nominal yield?","o":["5.0%","6.5%","9.5%","8.0%"],"a":3,"e":"r = r* + IP + DRP + LP + MRP = 2.0 + 3.0 + 1.0 + 0.5 + 1.5 = 8.0%. Missing the premiums gives 5.0% (Treasury-like) or 6.5% (no maturity premium).","d":2},{"q":"Which investor is MOST exposed to reinvestment risk when market interest rates fall?","o":["An investor holding a 30-year zero-coupon bond to maturity","An investor who has already sold the bond at par","A retiree holding a bond that is about to mature and must reinvest the proceeds in lower-yielding bonds","The bond issuer who locked in a fixed coupon"],"a":2,"e":"Reinvestment risk is the chance that coupons or maturing principal must be reinvested at lower rates. A zero-coupon bond held to maturity has no coupons to reinvest, so it has essentially none.","d":2},{"q":"A bond is selling at a discount. Which relationship among coupon rate, current yield, and yield to maturity is correct?","o":["Coupon rate < current yield < yield to maturity","Coupon rate > current yield > yield to maturity","Current yield < coupon rate < yield to maturity","Coupon rate = current yield = yield to maturity"],"a":0,"e":"For a discount bond, price < par so current yield (coupon/price) exceeds coupon rate (coupon/par); YTM is higher still because it also includes the gain to par. The order reverses for a premium bond.","d":2},{"q":"A convertible bond can be exchanged for 20 shares of common stock. The stock currently trades at $55, and the bond trades at $1,150. What is the bond's conversion value?","o":["$1,150","$1,100","$2.75","$1,000"],"a":1,"e":"Conversion value = conversion ratio x stock price = 20 x 55 = $1,100. The bond trades above conversion value because of its bond floor and option value. $2.75 divides instead of multiplies.","d":2},{"q":"A 15-year zero-coupon bond has a $1,000 face value and a 7% yield to maturity (APR, semiannual compounding). What is the price?","o":["$362.45","$596.89","$356.28","$487.80"],"a":2,"e":"Semiannual: i = 3.5%, n = 30. Price = 1,000 / 1.035^30 = $356.28. Annual compounding gives $362.45; $596.89 uses only 15 periods.","d":2},{"q":"A 3-year bond with a 7% annual coupon and $1,000 face value is priced at $950. What is its approximate yield to maturity?","o":["7.37%","7.00%","8.89%","8.97%"],"a":3,"e":"Solve 950 = 70 x annuity(3, y) + 1,000/(1+y)^3 for y; this gives y = 8.97% (Excel =RATE(3,70,-950,1000)). Current yield (7.37%) ignores the $50 gain to par; the coupon rate is 7%; 8.89% is the rough approximation formula.","d":3},{"q":"A 20-year bond with a $1,000 face value pays a 7% coupon semiannually. The yield to maturity is 8% (APR, semiannual). What is the price?","o":["$901.82","$1,000.00","$1,115.57","$901.04"],"a":3,"e":"Semiannual: PMT = $35, i = 4%, n = 40. Price = $901.04. The annual-pay version gives $901.82 - close but not the same; $1,115.57 is the price at a 6% YTM.","d":3},{"q":"A 10-year bond with a $1,000 face value pays an 8% coupon semiannually and trades at $950. What is the quoted yield to maturity (APR)?","o":["4.38%","8.76%","8.95%","8.42%"],"a":1,"e":"Solve for the semiannual rate (=RATE(20,40,-950,1000)): 4.380%. Quoted YTM = 2 x 4.380% = 8.76%. The 8.95% figure is the effective annual yield and 4.38% is just the six-month rate.","d":3},{"q":"A 10-year, 6% annual-coupon bond is priced at par ($1,000) with a yield of 6%. If its yield falls to 5%, what is the percentage change in price?","o":["+1.00%","-7.72%","+7.72%","+5.00%"],"a":2,"e":"New price = 60 x annuity(10, 5%) + 1,000/1.05^10 = $1,077.22, a gain of 7.72%. The price rises when the yield falls, and by more than 1% because of the 10-year duration.","d":3},{"q":"A 10-year, 8% annual-coupon bond with $1,000 face value trades at $1,100. It is callable in 5 years at $1,050. What is the yield to call (YTC)?","o":["8.00%","6.47%","6.60%","7.27%"],"a":1,"e":"Solve 1,100 = 80 x annuity(5, y) + 1,050/(1+y)^5 for y: YTC = 6.47% (=RATE(5,80,-1100,1050)). YTM to the 10-year maturity is 6.60%. For a premium bond the YTC is lower, so investors treat it as the conservative yield; 7.27% is the current yield.","d":3},{"q":"A 10-year zero-coupon bond and a 10-year 8% annual-coupon bond both have a $1,000 face value and a yield of 8%. If yields rise to 9%, which falls more in percentage terms?","o":["The zero-coupon bond falls about 8.8% versus about 6.4% for the coupon bond","The coupon bond falls about 8.8% versus about 6.4% for the zero","They fall by the same amount because maturity is the same","Neither falls, since both are held to maturity"],"a":0,"e":"Zero: $463.19 to $422.41 (-8.8%). Coupon bond: $1,000.00 to $935.82 (-6.4%). All of the zero's cash flow comes at the end, so it has the longest effective duration and the most interest rate risk.","d":3},{"q":"Why does a callable bond typically offer a higher yield than an otherwise identical non-callable bond, and what happens to its price when market rates fall sharply?","o":["Investors demand compensation for call risk; the price rises less than a non-callable bond because the issuer is likely to call it (price compression)","The call feature favors investors, so the yield is higher and the price rises more than a non-callable bond","The call feature has no effect on yield but lowers the credit rating","The yield is higher because callable bonds have longer maturity and prices stay at par"],"a":0,"e":"The call option benefits the issuer: when rates drop the issuer can refinance cheaply, capping the price near the call price. Investors require a higher yield (or lower price) to accept that.","d":3},{"q":"A bond yields a nominal 6.5% while expected inflation is 2.5%. What is the exact real rate?","o":["3.90%","4.00%","9.16%","3.50%"],"a":0,"e":"Real = (1 + nominal)/(1 + inflation) - 1 = 1.065/1.025 - 1 = 3.90%. The 4.00% figure is the approximation (nominal minus inflation).","d":3},{"q":"A 3-year bond with a 7% annual coupon and $1,000 face value has a 5% yield to maturity. If the yield stays at 5%, what will its price be one year from now (right after the next coupon is paid)?","o":["$1,054.46","$1,000.00","$1,037.19","$1,056.58"],"a":2,"e":"Today's price is $1,054.46. One year later it has 2 years left: 70 x annuity(2, 5%) + 1,000/1.05^2 = $1,037.19. A premium bond's price declines toward par if yields don't change.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Coupon rate","Stated annual interest as percent of par"],["YTM","Return if bought and held to maturity"],["Current yield","Annual coupon divided by market price"],["Premium bond","Priced above par value"],["Zero-coupon bond","Pays only face value at maturity"],["Reinvestment risk","Reinvesting cash flows at lower rates"],["Callable bond","Issuer may redeem before maturity"],["Fisher equation","Links nominal, real, and inflation rates"]],"traps":["Trap: A bond's price is its face value, since it repays $1,000. Truth: Price is the PV of coupons plus face at the current YTM, so it can be above or below par.","Trap: Coupon rate and yield to maturity are the same thing. Truth: Coupon is fixed on par; YTM is the market's required return and changes daily.","Trap: Higher market rates raise bond prices because new coupons are bigger. Truth: Existing bond prices fall when market rates rise.","Trap: Semiannual bond: use the annual coupon, annual yield, and years. Truth: Halve coupon and yield, and double the periods.","Trap: Exact real rate equals nominal minus inflation. Truth: That is only the approximation; exact is (1 + nominal)/(1 + inflation) - 1."],"mnemonic":"'Yield up, price down, longer and lower fall farther.' Rising rates cut prices, and long-maturity, low-coupon bonds are hit hardest.","cram":["Price = PV of coupons + PV of face at the YTM.","Semiannual: coupon/2, yield/2, periods x 2.","Coupon > YTM is premium; coupon < YTM is discount; equal is par.","Yields up means prices down; longer maturity and lower coupon mean more rate risk.","Current yield = coupon / price; discount bond: coupon rate < CY < YTM.","Zero-coupon price = Face / (1+y)^n; highest price risk.","Fisher: (1+nominal) = (1+real)(1+inflation).","Nominal rate = r*+IP+DRP+LP+MRP; lowest investment grade is BBB-."],"ytv":[{"title":"Bond Prices And How They Are Related To Yield to Maturity (YTM)","channel":"Professor Ikram","id":"BqFc3iosyqQ","why":"Explains the inverse price-yield relationship with examples."},{"title":"How to Calculate the Price of a Bond using a Financial Calculator","channel":"Professor Capko","id":"E72Ju52Eu5E","why":"Calculator keystrokes for bond pricing."},{"title":"Calculate Yield to Maturity of a Coupon Bond in 2 Minutes","channel":"Ryan O'Connell, CFA, FRM","id":"7TbvfoR2Uwg","why":"Fast walkthrough of solving for YTM."}]},{"id":"u8","title":"Stock Valuation","day":4,"summary":"Value preferred and common stock with the dividend discount and Gordon growth models, solve for required return, handle nonconstant growth, and compare relative, free-cash-flow, and market-efficiency ideas.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Common stock vs preferred stock","body":"<p><strong>Common stock</strong> represents ownership of the corporation.</p><ul><li><strong>Residual claim:</strong> paid after creditors and preferred holders, in dividends and in liquidation.</li><li><strong>Voting rights:</strong> elect the board and vote on major issues (one vote per share, usually).</li><li><strong>Dividends:</strong> discretionary; the board decides.</li><li><strong>Preemptive right:</strong> in some charters, the right to buy new shares first so ownership is not diluted.</li><li><strong>Limited liability:</strong> you can lose at most your investment.</li></ul><p><strong>Preferred stock</strong> is a hybrid between debt and equity.</p><ul><li>Fixed dividend, stated as a percent of par or a dollar amount.</li><li><strong>Priority</strong> over common for dividends and in liquidation.</li><li>Usually <strong>no vote</strong> unless dividends are missed.</li><li><strong>Cumulative:</strong> missed dividends (arrears) must be paid before common dividends.</li><li>May be callable or convertible. Dividends are paid from after-tax income (not deductible), and skipping them does not trigger bankruptcy.</li></ul><p>Businesses issue common stock to raise ownership capital with no required payment; preferred stock to raise funds without giving up control or taking on required interest.</p>"},{"h":"2. Valuing preferred stock","body":"<p>Preferred stock pays a fixed dividend forever, so it is a <strong>perpetuity</strong>:</p><p><code>P = D / r</code></p><p><strong>Example:</strong> preferred stock with $100 par and a 6% dividend pays $6.00 per year. If investors require 7.5%, price = 6 / 0.075 = $80.00. It trades below par because the required return exceeds the stated rate. Required return can be found from a price: r = D / P.</p><p>If there is a call price, a more advanced treatment values it like a bond, but for the exam the perpetuity formula is the default.</p>"},{"h":"3. The dividend discount model (DDM)","body":"<p>A stock is worth the <strong>present value of all the cash it will pay its owner</strong>. For a stock held forever, that is all future dividends:</p><p><code>P0 = D1/(1+r) + D2/(1+r)^2 + D3/(1+r)^3 + ...</code></p><p>If you sell after some years, the cash flows are the dividends plus the sale price, but the sale price is itself the PV of later dividends, so the value is the same. Special cases:</p><ul><li><strong>Zero growth:</strong> D is constant, so P0 = D / r (same as preferred). A stock paying $4 forever at 10% is worth $40.00.</li><li><strong>Constant growth:</strong> dividends grow at g forever - next section.</li><li><strong>Nonconstant growth:</strong> growth changes over time - section 6.</li></ul><p>Remember: r is the investor's <em>required return</em>, which depends on risk (see the risk and return unit).</p>"},{"h":"4. Constant growth (Gordon) model and the D0 vs D1 trap","body":"<p>If dividends grow at a constant rate g forever and r &gt; g:</p><p><code>P0 = D1 / (r - g)</code>, where <code>D1 = D0 x (1 + g)</code></p><p><strong>The trap:</strong> the formula needs <strong>D1, next year's dividend</strong>. If the problem says 'the company <em>just paid</em> $2.00' or gives 'D0', you must grow it first. If it says 'the next dividend will be $2.00', that already <em>is</em> D1.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> D0 = $1.80, g = 4%, r = 9%. D1 = 1.80 x 1.04 = $1.872. P0 = 1.872 / 0.05 = $37.44. (Using 1.80 would give $36.00 - wrong.)</p><p>Because everything grows at g, the price also grows at g: P1 = P0 x (1 + g) = $38.94. Also, P_t = D_(t+1) / (r - g) at any date t.</p><p><strong>Sensitivity:</strong> as r gets close to g the price explodes; if g &ge; r the model breaks. A sustainable growth rate can be estimated as <code>g = retention ratio x ROE</code>.</p>"},{"h":"5. Required return = dividend yield + capital gains yield","body":"<p>Rearranging the Gordon model gives the investor's expected return:</p><p><code>r = D1/P0 + g</code></p><ul><li><strong>Dividend yield</strong> = D1 / P0 (cash income)</li><li><strong>Capital gains yield</strong> = g (the expected rate of price growth, because P grows at g)</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> the stock trades at $48 and just paid D0 = $2.40, with g = 5%. D1 = 2.40 x 1.05 = $2.52. Dividend yield = 2.52 / 48 = 5.25%; capital gains yield = 5%. Expected return = <strong>10.25%</strong>.</p><p>You can also solve for g if you know r, P0, and D1: g = r - D1/P0. And you can use the equation to back out the market's required return from the observed price - the basis of one cost of equity estimate (the dividend growth approach in the WACC unit).</p><p><strong>Holding-period return</strong> for one year: (D1 + P1 - P0) / P0.</p>"},{"h":"6. Nonconstant (supernormal) growth","body":"<p>Young, fast-growing firms may grow at 20% or more for a few years, then slow to a normal rate. Steps:</p><ol><li>Forecast each dividend during the high-growth years: D1, D2, ..., DN.</li><li>At the end of the high-growth period (year N), the dividends settle at constant growth g. Compute the <strong>terminal value</strong>: <code>P_N = D_(N+1) / (r - g)</code>, where D_(N+1) = D_N x (1 + g).</li><li>Discount every dividend and the terminal value back to today and add.</li></ol><p><strong>Example:</strong> D0 = $2.00, growth 20% for 2 years then 5% forever, r = 12%. D1 = $2.40, D2 = $2.88, D3 = $3.02. P2 = $3.02 / 0.07 = $43.20. P0 = $2.40/1.12 + $2.88/1.12^2 + $43.20/1.12^2 = <strong>$38.88</strong>.</p><p>On a financial calculator, use the cash-flow (CF) worksheet and NPV. The most common mistakes are leaving out the terminal value and using D_N instead of D_(N+1).</p>"},{"h":"7. Relative and corporate valuation, and market efficiency","body":"<p><strong>P/E multiples.</strong> P/E = price / earnings per share. To value a firm, multiply its expected EPS by the peer or industry P/E: $3.50 x 14 = $49.00. Related multiples include price/book and price/sales. The constant-growth model implies P0/E1 = payout / (r - g).</p><p><strong>Corporate (free cash flow) valuation.</strong> Useful for firms that pay no dividends. Value of operations = PV of expected free cash flows (FCF) at WACC; with constant FCF growth, FCF1/(WACC - g). Then <strong>equity value = value of operations - debt - preferred stock</strong> (plus non-operating assets), divided by shares outstanding for price per share. Example: FCF1 = $12M, WACC 10%, g 3% gives $171.43M; less $40M debt and $10M preferred leaves $121.43M for 5M shares = $24.29 per share.</p><p><strong>Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH).</strong> Prices reflect available information, so you cannot consistently earn abnormal returns:</p><ul><li><strong>Weak form:</strong> past prices and volume are reflected - technical analysis fails.</li><li><strong>Semi-strong form:</strong> all public information is reflected - fundamental analysis fails.</li><li><strong>Strong form:</strong> all information, including insider information, is reflected.</li></ul><p>Each form includes the one before it. Empirical evidence supports weak and (mostly) semi-strong, but not strong form.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Common stockholders own the firm, vote for the board, and have a residual claim. Dividends on common stock are at the discretion of the board.","Preferred stock pays a fixed dividend, has priority over common stock in dividends and liquidation, and usually has no voting rights. Cumulative preferred dividends build up as arrears.","Preferred stock is a perpetuity, so its value equals the dividend divided by the required return.","A stock's value is the present value of its expected future dividends, discounted at the required return.","In the constant growth or Gordon model, price today equals D1 divided by r minus g. This requires r to be greater than g.","D1 equals D0 times one plus g. If the problem says the company just paid a dividend, grow it first. If it says the next dividend, use it as is.","Under constant growth, the stock price also grows at the rate g, so the capital gains yield equals g.","The required return equals the dividend yield, D1 over P0, plus the growth rate g, which is the capital gains yield.","For nonconstant growth, forecast each dividend during the high-growth period, compute a terminal value with the Gordon formula using the first dividend after growth settles, and discount everything to today.","Price equals EPS times a P/E multiple. In free cash flow valuation, equity value equals the value of operations minus debt and preferred stock.","The weak form of market efficiency says past prices are reflected in current prices, the semi-strong form says all public information is reflected, and the strong form says all information, including insider information, is reflected."],"examples":[{"q":"A company just paid an annual dividend of $1.80 per share. Dividends are expected to grow 4% per year forever. The required return is 9%. (a) What is the stock worth today? (b) What price do you expect in one year? (c) What are the expected dividend yield and capital gains yield?","steps":["The dividend 'just paid' is D0 = $1.80, so first compute D1 = 1.80 x 1.04 = $1.872.","(a) P0 = D1 / (r - g) = 1.872 / (0.09 - 0.04) = $37.44.","(b) P1 = P0 x 1.04 = $38.94. Check with D2/(r - g) = 1.872 x 1.04 / 0.05 = $38.94.","(c) Dividend yield = D1/P0 = 1.872 / 37.44 = 5.00%; capital gains yield = g = 4.00%. Total = 9.00% = r.","Trap check: using D0 = 1.80 would give $36.00, which is too low."],"answer":"(a) $37.44; (b) $38.94; (c) 5.00% dividend yield + 4.00% capital gains yield"},{"q":"A stock sells for $48 and the company just paid a $2.40 dividend. Dividends are expected to grow 5% per year. (a) What is the required return? (b) If instead the required return were 12%, what would the stock be worth?","steps":["D1 = 2.40 x 1.05 = $2.52.","(a) r = D1/P0 + g = 2.52/48 + 0.05 = 5.25% + 5.00% = 10.25%.","(b) P0 = 2.52 / (0.12 - 0.05) = $36.00.","The market price ($48) is above the $36 value at a 12% return, so the market is requiring less than 12%."],"answer":"(a) 10.25%; (b) $36.00"},{"q":"A growing company just paid D0 = $1.00. Dividends will grow 15% per year for 3 years, then 5% per year forever. The required return is 11%. Find the stock price today.","steps":["D1 = 1.00 x 1.15 = $1.15; D2 = $1.32; D3 = $1.52.","D4 = D3 x 1.05 = $1.60.","Terminal value P3 = D4 / (0.11 - 0.05) = $26.62.","PV of dividends: $1.04 + $1.07 + $1.11 = $3.22.","PV of terminal value = $26.62 / 1.11^3 = $19.46.","P0 = $22.68."],"answer":"$22.68"}],"videos":[{"title":"Gordon growth model constant dividend growth stock valuation","query":"Gordon growth model constant growth dividend discount model stock valuation example"},{"title":"Nonconstant growth stock valuation terminal value","query":"nonconstant supernormal growth stock valuation terminal value two stage dividend discount"},{"title":"Efficient market hypothesis weak semi-strong strong form","query":"efficient market hypothesis weak semi-strong strong form explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Common stock","b":"Residual ownership claim with voting rights and discretionary dividends."},{"f":"Preferred stock","b":"Fixed-dividend hybrid with priority over common, usually no vote."},{"f":"Cumulative preferred","b":"Missed dividends accumulate and must be paid before common."},{"f":"Preemptive right","b":"Right to buy new shares to avoid dilution."},{"f":"Preferred stock value","b":"P = D / r"},{"f":"Dividend discount model","b":"Stock value = PV of all expected future dividends."},{"f":"Zero-growth stock","b":"P0 = D / r"},{"f":"Constant growth (Gordon) model","b":"P0 = D1 / (r - g), with r > g"},{"f":"D1 formula","b":"D1 = D0 x (1 + g)"},{"f":"D0 vs D1 trap","b":"'Just paid' means D0; grow it once to get D1."},{"f":"Required return, constant growth","b":"r = D1/P0 + g"},{"f":"Dividend yield","b":"D1 / P0"},{"f":"Capital gains yield","b":"Equals g under constant growth."},{"f":"Price next year, constant growth","b":"P1 = P0 x (1 + g)"},{"f":"Terminal value (nonconstant growth)","b":"P_N = D_(N+1) / (r - g)"},{"f":"P/E valuation","b":"Price = expected EPS x peer P/E."},{"f":"Equity value from corporate model","b":"Operations value - debt - preferred stock."},{"f":"Value of operations (constant growth FCF)","b":"FCF1 / (WACC - g)"},{"f":"Weak-form EMH","b":"Past prices reflected; technical analysis fails."},{"f":"Semi-strong EMH","b":"All public information reflected; fundamental analysis fails."},{"f":"Strong-form EMH","b":"All information, even private, is reflected in prices."}],"questions":[{"q":"A preferred stock pays a fixed annual dividend of $5.00 per share. If the required return on the stock is 8%, what is the value per share?","o":["$0.40","$62.50","$5.40","$67.50"],"a":1,"e":"Preferred stock is a perpetuity: value = D / r = 5.00 / 0.08 = $62.50. Multiplying D by r gives a dollar figure that has no meaning as a price.","d":1},{"q":"A stock is expected to pay a dividend of $3.00 next year (D1). Dividends are expected to grow 4% per year forever, and the required return is 10%. What is the current stock price?","o":["$30.00","$50.00","$52.00","$21.43"],"a":1,"e":"P0 = D1 / (r - g) = 3.00 / (0.10 - 0.04) = $50.00. Because the $3.00 is already D1, you do not multiply by (1 + g) again ($52.00 is the double-growth error).","d":1},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes common stockholders' claim on a corporation's income and assets?","o":["They are residual claimants: they are paid only after creditors and preferred stockholders","They are paid before bondholders if the firm is liquidated","They are guaranteed a fixed dividend every year","They have no voting rights but receive priority dividends"],"a":0,"e":"Common stock is the residual claim: dividends are discretionary and, in liquidation, common holders are paid last. Fixed priority dividends describe preferred stock.","d":1},{"q":"A stock trades at $40 and is expected to pay a dividend of $2 next year. What is its expected dividend yield?","o":["5.00%","20.00%","2.00%","4.00%"],"a":0,"e":"Dividend yield = D1 / P0 = 2 / 40 = 5.00%. The 20.00% figure inverts the ratio.","d":1},{"q":"Which form of the efficient market hypothesis states that stock prices fully reflect all publicly available information?","o":["Weak form","Strong form","None of the forms","Semi-strong form"],"a":3,"e":"Weak form: past prices and volume. Semi-strong: all public information (financial statements, news, announcements). Strong: all information, including private or insider information.","d":1},{"q":"Which feature most typically distinguishes preferred stock from common stock?","o":["Preferred pays a fixed dividend with priority over common dividends and usually has no voting rights","Preferred pays a variable dividend and has full voting rights","Preferred stockholders are the residual claimants","Preferred dividends are tax-deductible to the issuing firm"],"a":0,"e":"Preferred stock is a hybrid: fixed dividends, priority over common in dividends and liquidation, and typically no vote. Preferred dividends are paid from after-tax income, not deducted like interest.","d":1},{"q":"A company's stock trades at $60 per share, and its earnings per share (EPS) are $4. What is its P/E ratio?","o":["0.07","240.0","15.0","56.0"],"a":2,"e":"P/E = price / EPS = 60 / 4 = 15. The 0.07 inverts it (that is the earnings yield).","d":1},{"q":"You buy a stock for $40, receive a $2 dividend during the year, and sell the stock for $43. What is your holding-period return?","o":["7.5%","5.0%","11.6%","12.5%"],"a":3,"e":"Return = (dividend + price change) / beginning price = (2 + 3) / 40 = 12.5%. 7.5% counts only the capital gain; 11.6% divides by the ending price.","d":1},{"q":"A company just paid a dividend (D0) of $2.00 per share. Dividends are expected to grow at 5% per year forever, and the required return is 11%. What is the stock's value today?","o":["$33.33","$35.00","$18.18","$36.75"],"a":1,"e":"D1 = D0 x (1 + g) = 2.00 x 1.05 = $2.10. P0 = 2.10 / (0.11 - 0.05) = $35.00. Using D0 directly gives $33.33 - the classic D0 vs D1 trap. $36.75 grows twice.","d":2},{"q":"A stock is priced at $40, expects a next-year dividend (D1) of $2, and dividends are expected to grow 6% annually. What is the stock's required rate of return?","o":["5.00%","6.00%","11.30%","11.00%"],"a":3,"e":"r = D1/P0 + g = 2/40 + 0.06 = 11.00% (dividend yield 5% + capital gains yield 6%). 11.30% wrongly grows the D1 again.","d":2},{"q":"A firm just paid a $1.50 dividend, expected to grow 4% per year forever. The required return is 9%. What is the expected stock price three years from now?","o":["$30.00","$35.10","$40.40","$33.75"],"a":1,"e":"P3 = D4 / (r - g) = 1.50 x 1.04^4 / 0.05 = $35.10 (equivalently, today's price $31.20 x 1.04^3). $30.00 ignores growth; the third option grows the price at r, not g; $33.75 uses D3 instead of D4.","d":2},{"q":"A preferred stock has a $100 par value and pays a 6% dividend. The required return on similar preferred stock is 7.5%. What is its market price?","o":["$100.00","$80.00","$0.45","$106.00"],"a":1,"e":"Annual dividend = 6% x 100 = $6.00. Price = D / r = 6.00 / 0.075 = $80.00. Preferred prices fall below par when market required returns exceed the stated dividend rate.","d":2},{"q":"Analysts expect a firm to earn $3.50 per share next year. Comparable firms trade at an average P/E of 14. Using P/E valuation, what is the estimated stock price?","o":["$0.25","$3.50","$49.00","$4.00"],"a":2,"e":"Price = EPS x P/E = 3.50 x 14 = $49.00. Multiples are a quick relative-valuation method: apply the peer ratio to the firm's earnings.","d":2},{"q":"A no-growth company pays a constant $4.00 dividend each year forever. The required return is 10%. What is the stock worth?","o":["$0.40","$44.00","$4.00","$40.00"],"a":3,"e":"With zero growth the stock is a perpetuity: P0 = D / r = 4.00 / 0.10 = $40.00.","d":2},{"q":"An executive consistently earns above-normal returns trading on confidential merger plans before they are announced. Which form of market efficiency does this evidence contradict?","o":["Strong form","Weak form only","Semi-strong form only","None; insider information is already reflected in prices under the semi-strong form"],"a":0,"e":"Strong-form efficiency says prices reflect ALL information, including private information. Consistent insider profits contradict it. (Insider trading on material nonpublic information is also illegal.) Semi-strong covers only public information.","d":2},{"q":"A trader earns consistent excess returns by buying stocks after specific chart patterns appear in past price data. Which form of the efficient market hypothesis does this contradict?","o":["Only the strong form","Only the semi-strong form","The weak form (and therefore the semi-strong and strong forms as well)","None; technical analysis is allowed under all three forms"],"a":2,"e":"Weak-form efficiency says past prices and volume are already reflected in current prices, so technical analysis cannot earn abnormal returns. The forms are nested: if weak form fails, the stronger forms fail too.","d":2},{"q":"A stock's next dividend is $3.00, growing 4% forever. The price is currently $50 (required return 10%). If the required return rises to 12% and nothing else changes, what is the new price?","o":["$37.50","$50.00","$25.00","$26.00"],"a":0,"e":"New P0 = 3.00 / (0.12 - 0.04) = $37.50. A higher required return lowers the price ($25.00 ignores growth).","d":2},{"q":"A stock is priced at $40, has a next-year dividend of $2, and dividends grow 6% forever. If the growth continues, what price do you expect in exactly one year?","o":["$44.40","$42.00","$40.00","$42.40"],"a":3,"e":"In constant-growth, price grows at g: P1 = P0 x (1 + g) = 40 x 1.06 = $42.40. The $44.40 figure grows price at the total return r (11%), but the dividend is paid out in cash.","d":2},{"q":"A company has cumulative preferred stock paying $4 per share annually. The company skipped the last two annual dividends. If it now wants to pay the current year's preferred dividend and then pay common dividends, how much must it pay per preferred share first?","o":["$4.00","$12.00","$8.00","$16.00"],"a":1,"e":"Cumulative preferred dividends accumulate as arrears: 2 missed years x $4 = $8 plus the current $4 = $12 per share before any common dividend can be paid.","d":2},{"q":"A stock sells for $25 and is expected to pay $1.50 next year. The required return is 12%. If dividends grow at a constant rate forever, what is that growth rate?","o":["6.0%","12.0%","18.0%","3.0%"],"a":0,"e":"g = r - D1/P0 = 0.12 - 1.50/25 = 0.12 - 0.06 = 6.0%. Dividend yield is 6%, so capital gains yield must make up the other 6%.","d":2},{"q":"What is the purpose of preemptive rights for common stockholders?","o":["To give shareholders priority over creditors in bankruptcy","To let existing shareholders buy new shares before outsiders so they can keep their proportional ownership","To guarantee a minimum dividend","To let shareholders sell their shares back to the firm at par"],"a":1,"e":"A preemptive right (when granted by the charter) protects shareholders from dilution of ownership and control when new shares are issued.","d":2},{"q":"A firm just paid D0 = $2.00. Dividends will grow 20% per year for the next 2 years, then 5% per year forever. The required return is 12%. What is the stock price today?","o":["$4.44","$43.20","$38.88","$34.29"],"a":2,"e":"D1 = $2.40, D2 = $2.88, D3 = D2 x 1.05 = $3.02. Terminal price P2 = D3 / (0.12 - 0.05) = $43.20. P0 = $2.40/1.12 + $2.88/1.12^2 + $43.20/1.12^2 = $38.88. The other choices leave out the terminal value, leave it undiscounted, or ignore the high-growth years.","d":3},{"q":"A firm expects free cash flow of $12 million next year, growing 3% forever. Its WACC is 10%. It has $40 million of debt, $10 million of preferred stock, and 5 million shares outstanding. What is the estimated value per share?","o":["$34.29","$26.29","$24.29","$24.00"],"a":2,"e":"Value of operations = FCF1/(WACC - g) = 12/0.07 = $171.43M. Equity = operations - debt - preferred = 171.43 - 40 - 10 = $121.43M, or $24.29 per share. Ignoring debt ($34.29) or preferred ($26.29) overstates the stock.","d":3},{"q":"A young company will pay its first dividend of $3.00 at the end of year 3, and dividends will then grow 4% per year forever. The required return is 10%. What is the stock worth today?","o":["$50.00","$41.32","$37.57","$45.45"],"a":1,"e":"The Gordon formula values the stream one period before the first dividend: at year 2, P2 = 3.00/(0.10 - 0.04) = $50.00. Discount two years to today: $50.00 / 1.10^2 = $41.32. Using 3 years of discounting is one too many.","d":3},{"q":"Why is the constant-growth model P0 = D1 / (r - g) not valid for a company whose dividend growth rate g is expected to exceed the required return r forever?","o":["The formula would give a price of zero","The formula still works as long as D1 is positive","The formula would give a negative or infinite value; no firm can grow faster than the economy forever, so a nonconstant-growth model is needed","The model only requires that growth be positive, not less than r"],"a":2,"e":"The denominator (r - g) must be positive, and g must be sustainable in the long run. If g is higher than r for several years, use a multi-stage (nonconstant) model and apply the Gordon formula only after growth settles to a rate below r.","d":3},{"q":"A stock market is semi-strong form efficient. An analyst announces a strategy of screening publicly available financial statements and ratios to find undervalued stocks. What should be expected?","o":["The strategy should consistently beat the market because public data is slow to be priced","Only insiders could earn excess returns, so the strategy should earn the highest returns","The strategy should not produce consistent risk-adjusted excess returns after costs because the public information is already reflected in prices","The strategy works only if past prices are also analyzed"],"a":2,"e":"Semi-strong efficiency means public information, including financial statements, is quickly reflected in price. Fundamental analysis of public data should not earn abnormal returns; only private information (violating strong form) could.","d":3},{"q":"A firm expects next year's EPS to be $5.00 and pays out 40% of earnings as dividends. It retains 60%, and its return on equity (ROE) is 15%. The required return is 12%. Using g = retention ratio x ROE, what is the stock price?","o":["$66.67","$16.67","$166.67","$72.67"],"a":0,"e":"g = 0.60 x 0.15 = 9%. D1 = 40% x 5.00 = $2.00. P0 = 2.00 / (0.12 - 0.09) = $66.67. Using full EPS as the dividend gives $166.67; growing D1 again gives $72.67.","d":3},{"q":"A firm has a 40% dividend payout ratio, a required return of 12%, and a constant dividend growth rate of 9%. What forward P/E ratio (P0 / next-year EPS) is implied by the constant-growth model?","o":["13.33","3.33","33.33","20.00"],"a":0,"e":"P0 / E1 = (D1/E1) / (r - g) = payout / (r - g) = 0.40 / 0.03 = 13.33. Using the retention ratio (0.60) gives 20.00; ignoring payout gives 33.33.","d":3},{"q":"A growth stock has D1 = $1.00 and dividends growing at 8% forever. At a required return of 10% it is valued at $50. If the required return rises to 11%, by what percentage does its value change?","o":["-1.00%","-10.00%","-50.00%","-33.33%"],"a":3,"e":"New value = 1.00 / (0.11 - 0.08) = $33.33, versus $50.00: a change of -33.33%. Growth stocks are very sensitive to r because (r - g) is small: a 1-point rise in r cuts value by one third.","d":3},{"q":"A firm just paid D0 = $1.00. Dividends will grow 25% per year for 3 years, then 6% per year forever. The required return is 11%. What is the expected stock price at the end of year 3 (P3)?","o":["$39.06","$1.95","$21.20","$41.41"],"a":3,"e":"D3 = 1.00 x 1.25^3 = $1.95; D4 = D3 x 1.06 = $2.07. P3 = D4 / (0.11 - 0.06) = $41.41. Using D3 in the Gordon formula is the D0-vs-D1 trap again; the last choice ignores the 3 years of 25% growth.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Common stock","Residual ownership with voting rights"],["Preferred stock","Fixed dividend, priority over common"],["Gordon model","Price equals D1 over r minus g"],["Dividend yield","Next dividend divided by current price"],["Capital gains yield","Expected price growth rate"],["Terminal value","Value at end of high-growth period"],["P/E ratio","Price divided by earnings per share"],["Semi-strong EMH","Prices reflect all public information"]],"traps":["Trap: Put the dividend just paid (D0) straight into P0 = D1/(r - g). Truth: Grow it first: D1 = D0(1 + g); only 'next dividend' is already D1.","Trap: Capital gains yield is the dividend yield minus growth. Truth: Under constant growth, capital gains yield equals g, and r = D1/P0 + g.","Trap: Gordon model works whenever g is positive. Truth: It needs r > g; otherwise the value is negative or infinite.","Trap: Nonconstant growth terminal value uses D_N. Truth: Use D_(N+1) = D_N(1 + g) over (r - g), then discount it back.","Trap: Failure of weak-form efficiency affects only technical analysis. Truth: The forms are nested; a weak-form failure means semi-strong and strong forms fail too."],"mnemonic":"'D-one over r-minus-g': the Gordon formula wants NEXT year's dividend. Ask 'Has it been paid yet?' If yes, it's D0 - multiply by (1 + g) once.","cram":["Preferred value = D / r (perpetuity).","Gordon: P0 = D1 / (r - g); needs r > g.","D1 = D0(1 + g); 'just paid' means D0.","Required return r = D1/P0 + g (dividend yield + capital gains yield).","Price grows at g: P1 = P0(1 + g).","Nonconstant: terminal value P_N = D_(N+1)/(r - g), discounted to today.","Equity value = operations value - debt - preferred; divide by shares.","EMH: weak = past prices, semi-strong = public, strong = all information."],"ytv":[{"title":"How to compute a stock price using the Gordon Growth Model","channel":"Eric Kelley","id":"Lh1kT15jTmA","why":"Short walkthrough of D1/(r - g) with worked numbers."},{"title":"Dividend Discount Model (DDM)","channel":"Edspira","id":"TlH3_iOHX3s","why":"Explains the DDM family including zero and constant growth."},{"title":"Gordon Model Pricing Growing Dividends","channel":"Capital Markets and Securities with Michael","id":"shYOzFhYk0Q","why":"Additional constant-growth pricing examples."}]},{"id":"u9","title":"Risk and Return","day":5,"summary":"Measure return and risk, learn why only market risk is rewarded, and use beta and CAPM to find a stock's required return.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Return: what you earned","body":"<p>Every finance decision starts with two questions: <strong>how much will I earn</strong> and <strong>how risky is it</strong>. Return is the reward; risk is the uncertainty about that reward.</p><p>The <strong>holding-period return (HPR)</strong> is the total gain over the time you owned the asset, expressed as a percent of what you paid:</p><p><code>HPR = (ending price - beginning price + cash received) / beginning price</code></p><p><strong>Example:</strong> A company buys a share of a supplier for $40. It collects a $2 dividend and sells at $44. HPR = (44 - 40 + 2) / 40 = 15%. The 10% price gain is the <em>capital gain yield</em> and the 5% dividend is the <em>dividend yield</em>; they add up to the HPR.</p><p>Returns over several periods compound. If a fund returns +20% then -10%, the two-year HPR is (1.20)(0.90) - 1 = 8%, not 10%.</p>"},{"h":"2. Expected return and standard deviation (total risk)","body":"<p>The future is uncertain, so we describe it with <strong>scenarios and probabilities</strong>. The <strong>expected return</strong> is the probability-weighted average: <code>E(r) = sum of (probability x return)</code>.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Stock X returns -8% in a recession (25% chance), 10% in a normal economy (50%), and 28% in a boom (25%). E(r) = 0.25(-8) + 0.50(10) + 0.25(28) = -2 + 5 + 7 = <strong>10%</strong>.</p><p><strong>Standard deviation</strong> measures how far outcomes typically stray from the expected return. Steps: subtract the mean from each outcome, square it, weight by probability (that sum is the <em>variance</em>), then take the square root.</p><ul><li>Recession: (-8 - 10)^2 = 324, times 0.25 = 81</li><li>Normal: (10 - 10)^2 = 0, times 0.50 = 0</li><li>Boom: (28 - 10)^2 = 324, times 0.25 = 81</li></ul><p>Variance = 162, standard deviation = square root of 162 = <strong>12.73%</strong>. A larger standard deviation means more total risk. Standard deviation counts <em>both</em> kinds of risk (market and company-specific).</p><p>The <strong>coefficient of variation (CV)</strong> = standard deviation / expected return. It is risk <em>per unit of return</em>, used when investments have different expected returns. Stock X: 12.73 / 10 = 1.27. If Stock Y has E(r) = 8% and SD = 6%, its CV is 0.75, so Y is less risky per unit of return even though it earns less. Lower CV is better.</p>"},{"h":"3. Diversification and correlation","body":"<p>The old saying is: do not put all your eggs in one basket. <strong>Diversification</strong> means holding many assets so that one asset's bad luck is offset by another's good luck.</p><p>How much help you get depends on <strong>correlation</strong>, a number from -1 to +1 describing how two assets move together.</p><ul><li><strong>+1.0:</strong> move in lockstep. No risk reduction. Portfolio SD is just the weighted average of the two SDs.</li><li><strong>0:</strong> unrelated. Good risk reduction.</li><li><strong>-1.0:</strong> move opposite. Risk can be eliminated entirely.</li></ul><p>Most stocks are positively correlated with each other (they all feel the economy) but with correlations well below +1, so combining them lowers risk. <strong>Example:</strong> a snow-removal company and an ice-cream chain have returns that partly offset by season. A portfolio of the two is steadier than either alone.</p><p><strong>Key fact:</strong> a portfolio's expected return is the weighted average of the asset returns, but its standard deviation is <em>less than</em> the weighted average of the SDs whenever correlation is below +1. That is the free lunch of diversification.</p>"},{"h":"4. Systematic vs unsystematic risk and beta","body":"<p>Total risk splits into two parts:</p><ul><li><strong>Unsystematic risk</strong> (company-specific, diversifiable): a plant fire, a lawsuit, a CEO scandal, a failed product. Spread across many stocks it washes out.</li><li><strong>Systematic risk</strong> (market risk, non-diversifiable): recessions, inflation, interest-rate moves, wars. They hit almost everything, so owning more stocks does not remove them.</li></ul><p>Because a smart investor can eliminate unsystematic risk for free, the market does <em>not</em> pay extra for bearing it. Only systematic risk earns a reward.</p><p><strong>Beta</strong> measures a stock's systematic risk: how much it moves when the market moves.</p><ul><li>Beta = 1.0: moves with the market (the market itself has beta 1.0).</li><li>Beta greater than 1 (say 1.5): amplifies market moves; aggressive (airlines, tech).</li><li>Beta less than 1 (say 0.6): dampens market moves; defensive (utilities, groceries).</li><li>Beta = 0: no market risk (the risk-free asset).</li></ul><p>Beta can be computed as <code>correlation x (SD of stock / SD of market)</code>. <strong>Example:</strong> correlation 0.8, stock SD 30%, market SD 20% gives beta = 0.8 x 1.5 = 1.2.</p><p>Notice: a stock can have a huge standard deviation but a low beta if its risk is mostly company-specific. For a diversified investor beta, not standard deviation, is what matters.</p>"},{"h":"5. Portfolio return and portfolio beta","body":"<p>For a portfolio, both the expected return and the beta are <strong>weighted averages</strong> of the individual holdings, where each weight is the fraction of the portfolio's dollars in that asset.</p><p><code>E(r portfolio) = sum of (weight x expected return)</code> and <code>beta portfolio = sum of (weight x beta)</code></p><p><strong>Example:</strong> A $100,000 portfolio has $25,000 in a utility (beta 0.6), $25,000 in a bank (beta 1.0), and $50,000 in a tech firm (beta 1.4). Weights are 25%, 25%, 50%. Portfolio beta = 0.25(0.6) + 0.25(1.0) + 0.50(1.4) = 0.15 + 0.25 + 0.70 = <strong>1.10</strong>. The portfolio is 10% more volatile than the market.</p><p><strong>Swapping a holding:</strong> beta changes by (weight swapped) x (new beta - old beta). Mixing in Treasury bills (beta 0) lowers portfolio beta proportionally: 60% in a beta-1.5 stock and 40% in T-bills gives beta 0.90.</p><p>Remember: the weighted-average shortcut works for <em>return</em> and <em>beta</em>, but <em>not</em> for standard deviation (unless correlation is +1).</p>"},{"h":"6. CAPM, the market risk premium, and the Security Market Line","body":"<p>The <strong>Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)</strong> turns beta into a required return:</p><p><code>r = rf + beta x (rm - rf)</code></p><ul><li><strong>rf</strong> = risk-free rate (Treasury yield): the reward for waiting.</li><li><strong>rm - rf</strong> = <strong>market risk premium</strong>: the extra return for holding the average stock instead of the safe asset.</li><li><strong>beta x market risk premium</strong> = the premium for this particular stock's market risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> rf = 4%, rm = 10%, beta = 1.3. Required return = 4% + 1.3 x 6% = <strong>11.8%</strong>. A stock with beta 0.5 would require only 7%.</p><p>The <strong>Security Market Line (SML)</strong> plots required return (vertical) against beta (horizontal). It starts at rf when beta = 0, passes through rm at beta = 1, and slopes upward by the market risk premium.</p><ul><li>If a stock's expected return is <strong>above</strong> the line, it offers more than required: <strong>undervalued</strong>. Buying pressure raises its price until the return falls onto the line.</li><li>If <strong>below</strong> the line: <strong>overvalued</strong>. Selling pushes the price down.</li><li><strong>On</strong> the line: fairly valued.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> rf = 3.5%, rm = 9.5%, beta = 1.5 gives required 12.5%. An analyst forecasts 11%: below the SML, so overvalued.</p><p><strong>Shifts of the SML:</strong> higher inflation expectations raise rf and move the whole line up in parallel. Greater risk aversion raises the market risk premium and makes the line steeper, hurting high-beta stocks most.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Holding period return equals ending price minus beginning price plus any dividends, all divided by the beginning price.","Expected return is a probability weighted average of the possible returns, so multiply each return by its probability and add them up.","Standard deviation measures total risk, which includes both market risk and company specific risk.","The coefficient of variation equals standard deviation divided by expected return, and a lower number means less risk per unit of return.","Diversification reduces risk as long as the correlation between assets is below positive one, and negative correlation helps the most.","Unsystematic risk is company specific and can be diversified away, but systematic market risk cannot.","Beta measures systematic risk. The market has a beta of one, a beta above one is more volatile than the market, and a beta below one is less volatile.","Portfolio expected return and portfolio beta are both weighted averages of the individual assets, but portfolio standard deviation is not.","The CAPM says required return equals the risk free rate plus beta times the market risk premium.","The market risk premium is the expected market return minus the risk free rate.","On the security market line, a stock above the line is undervalued, and a stock below the line is overvalued.","A rise in the risk free rate shifts the security market line up in parallel, while a rise in risk aversion makes the line steeper."],"examples":[{"q":"Stock X has a 25% chance of returning -8%, a 50% chance of 10%, and a 25% chance of 28%. Stock Y has an expected return of 8% and a standard deviation of 6%. Find X's expected return, standard deviation and CV, and say which stock has less risk per unit of return.","steps":["Expected return of X = 0.25(-8) + 0.50(10) + 0.25(28) = -2 + 5 + 7 = 10%.","Variance of X = 0.25(-8-10)^2 + 0.50(10-10)^2 + 0.25(28-10)^2 = 0.25(324) + 0 + 0.25(324) = 162.","Standard deviation of X = square root of 162 = 12.73%.","CV of X = 12.73 / 10 = 1.27. CV of Y = 6 / 8 = 0.75."],"answer":"X: expected 10%, SD 12.73%, CV 1.27. Y has the lower CV (0.75), so Y has less risk per unit of return."},{"q":"A $100,000 portfolio holds $25,000 in a stock with beta 0.6, $25,000 in a stock with beta 1.0, and $50,000 in a stock with beta 1.4. The risk-free rate is 4% and the market return is 10%. Find the portfolio beta and its required return.","steps":["Weights: 25,000/100,000 = 0.25; 0.25; 50,000/100,000 = 0.50.","Portfolio beta = 0.25(0.6) + 0.25(1.0) + 0.50(1.4) = 0.15 + 0.25 + 0.70 = 1.10.","Market risk premium = 10% - 4% = 6%.","Required return = 4% + 1.10 x 6% = 4% + 6.6% = 10.6%."],"answer":"Portfolio beta = 1.10; required return = 10.6%."},{"q":"rf = 3.5% and rm = 9.5%. A stock has beta 1.5 and analysts forecast a 11% return. Is it overvalued or undervalued, and what happens to its price?","steps":["Market risk premium = 9.5% - 3.5% = 6%.","Required return = 3.5% + 1.5 x 6% = 3.5% + 9% = 12.5%.","Compare: forecast 11% is less than required 12.5%, so the stock plots below the SML.","Investors will sell, pushing the price down until the expected return rises to 12.5%."],"answer":"Overvalued (below the SML); the price should fall until its expected return equals 12.5%."}],"videos":[{"title":"CAPM and security market line explained","query":"CAPM security market line beta explained"},{"title":"Systematic vs unsystematic risk and diversification","query":"systematic vs unsystematic risk diversification explained"},{"title":"Expected return and standard deviation of a portfolio","query":"expected return standard deviation probability distribution finance"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Holding-period return","b":"(Ending price - beginning price + dividends) / beginning price."},{"f":"Expected return","b":"Sum of probability x return across all scenarios."},{"f":"Standard deviation","b":"Square root of variance; measures total risk."},{"f":"Variance","b":"Probability-weighted squared deviations from the expected return."},{"f":"Coefficient of variation","b":"SD / expected return; risk per unit of return. Lower is better."},{"f":"Correlation","b":"Number from -1 to +1 showing how two assets move together."},{"f":"Diversification benefit","b":"Exists whenever correlation is below +1; largest at -1."},{"f":"Systematic risk","b":"Market-wide risk that cannot be diversified away; measured by beta."},{"f":"Unsystematic risk","b":"Company-specific risk that diversification eliminates."},{"f":"Beta","b":"Sensitivity of a stock's returns to market returns."},{"f":"Beta of the market","b":"1.0 by definition."},{"f":"Beta of a risk-free asset","b":"0."},{"f":"Portfolio beta","b":"Weighted average of the betas of the holdings."},{"f":"Portfolio expected return","b":"Weighted average of the expected returns of the holdings."},{"f":"CAPM","b":"r = rf + beta x (rm - rf)."},{"f":"Market risk premium","b":"rm - rf; extra return for bearing average market risk."},{"f":"Security Market Line","b":"Graph of required return versus beta; intercept rf, slope rm - rf."},{"f":"Stock above the SML","b":"Expected return exceeds required return: undervalued."},{"f":"Stock below the SML","b":"Expected return is less than required: overvalued."},{"f":"Effect of higher inflation expectations on SML","b":"Parallel upward shift; rf rises."},{"f":"Effect of greater risk aversion on SML","b":"Steeper slope; market risk premium rises."},{"f":"Why CAPM ignores unsystematic risk","b":"Investors can diversify it away at no cost, so it earns no premium."}],"questions":[{"q":"You buy a share of stock for $40. During the year it pays a $2 dividend and you sell it for $44. What is your holding-period return?","o":["10.0%","5.0%","5.5%","15.0%"],"a":3,"e":"HPR = (ending price - beginning price + dividend) / beginning price = (44 - 40 + 2) / 40 = 6 / 40 = 15%. Ten percent counts only the capital gain and forgets the dividend.","d":1},{"q":"Which type of risk can be eliminated by holding a well-diversified portfolio?","o":["Market risk","Unsystematic (company-specific) risk","Inflation risk that hits every stock","Interest-rate risk that hits every stock"],"a":1,"e":"Unsystematic risk (a strike, a lawsuit, a failed product) is firm-specific and cancels out across many stocks. Market-wide risk cannot be diversified away.","d":1},{"q":"A stock has a beta of 1.5. What does this mean?","o":["It will earn 1.5% per year","It is 50% less risky than the market","It tends to move 50% more than the overall market","It has 1.5 times the standard deviation of every other stock"],"a":2,"e":"Beta measures sensitivity to market movements. A beta of 1.5 means that when the market moves 10%, the stock tends to move about 15%. It is not a return and not a ratio to other stocks.","d":1},{"q":"Which correlation between two stocks gives the greatest diversification benefit?","o":["-1.0","+1.0","0.0","+0.5"],"a":0,"e":"The more negatively correlated two assets are, the more their movements offset each other. At +1.0 there is no reduction in risk at all.","d":1},{"q":"What is the beta of the overall market portfolio?","o":["1.0","0.0","0.5","It depends on the risk-free rate"],"a":0,"e":"Beta is measured relative to the market, so the market has a beta of exactly 1.0 by definition. A beta of 0 belongs to the risk-free asset.","d":1},{"q":"Standard deviation of a single stock's returns measures which kind of risk?","o":["Only market risk","Total risk (systematic plus unsystematic)","Only company-specific risk","Only default risk"],"a":1,"e":"Standard deviation captures all variability in returns. Beta, not standard deviation, isolates the market-risk part.","d":1},{"q":"According to CAPM, which risk is rewarded with a higher expected return?","o":["Total risk, measured by standard deviation","Unsystematic risk, measured by the coefficient of variation","Systematic risk, measured by beta","Business-specific risk, measured by variance"],"a":2,"e":"Investors can diversify away company-specific risk for free, so the market only pays for the risk that cannot be diversified: beta.","d":1},{"q":"The risk-free rate is 3% and the expected market return is 10%. What is the market risk premium?","o":["10%","13%","3%","7%"],"a":3,"e":"Market risk premium = rm - rf = 10% - 3% = 7%. It is the extra return investors demand for holding the average-risk stock instead of the risk-free asset.","d":1},{"q":"The risk-free rate is 4%, the expected market return is 10%, and a stock's beta is 1.3. What is the stock's required return under CAPM?","o":["13.0%","11.8%","7.8%","14.3%"],"a":1,"e":"r = 4% + 1.3 x (10% - 4%) = 4% + 7.8% = 11.8%. Using 13% multiplies beta by the market return and ignores the risk-free rate; 7.8% is only the risk-premium part.","d":2},{"q":"A stock has a 25% chance of returning -4%, a 50% chance of returning 10%, and a 25% chance of returning 24%. What is its expected return?","o":["10.0%","8.7%","13.3%","7.5%"],"a":0,"e":"E(r) = 0.25(-4) + 0.50(10) + 0.25(24) = -1 + 5 + 6 = 10%. The simple average of the three outcomes (10%) happens to match here, but the weights must be the probabilities.","d":2},{"q":"Using the same distribution (25% chance of -4%, 50% chance of 10%, 25% chance of 24%; expected return 10%), what is the standard deviation?","o":["About 98%","About 14%","About 9.90%","About 4.95%"],"a":2,"e":"Variance = 0.25(-14)^2 + 0.50(0)^2 + 0.25(14)^2 = 49 + 0 + 49 = 98. Standard deviation = square root of 98 = 9.90%. The number 98 is the variance, which is in squared percent units.","d":2},{"q":"Investment A has an expected return of 10% and a standard deviation of 15%. Investment B has an expected return of 14% and a standard deviation of 16%. Which statement is correct?","o":["A is better because its standard deviation is lower","B has less risk per unit of return (CV of 1.14 versus 1.50)","A and B are equal because their CVs are both 1.50","B is worse because its standard deviation is higher"],"a":1,"e":"CV = standard deviation / expected return. A: 15/10 = 1.50. B: 16/14 = 1.14. The lower CV means less risk per unit of expected return, so B is better despite its slightly higher standard deviation.","d":2},{"q":"You invest $40,000 in Stock A (expected return 12%) and $60,000 in Stock B (expected return 8%). What is the portfolio's expected return?","o":["10.0%","8.0%","12.0%","9.6%"],"a":3,"e":"Weights are 40% and 60%. Expected return = 0.40(12%) + 0.60(8%) = 4.8% + 4.8% = 9.6%. The simple average (10%) ignores that more money is in the lower-return stock.","d":2},{"q":"A portfolio has 25% in a stock with beta 0.6, 25% in a stock with beta 1.0, and 50% in a stock with beta 1.4. What is the portfolio beta?","o":["1.00","1.07","1.40","1.10"],"a":3,"e":"Portfolio beta is the weighted average: 0.25(0.6) + 0.25(1.0) + 0.50(1.4) = 0.15 + 0.25 + 0.70 = 1.10. The simple average of the betas (1.00) ignores the weights.","d":2},{"q":"The risk-free rate is 3% and the market return is 10%. A stock with beta 1.2 is expected (by analysts) to return 13%. Relative to the SML, this stock is","o":["Overvalued, because 13% is above the required 11.4%","Fairly valued, because 13% is above the risk-free rate","Undervalued, because 13% is above the required 11.4%","Undervalued, because beta is above 1"],"a":2,"e":"Required return = 3% + 1.2(7%) = 11.4%. The stock offers 13%, which is more than the required return, so it plots above the SML. Investors would buy it, bidding the price up until the expected return falls to 11.4%.","d":2},{"q":"The risk-free rate rises from 3% to 5% and the expected market return rises from 9% to 11%, so the market risk premium stays at 6%. By how much does the required return on a stock with beta 1.4 change?","o":["It rises 2.0 percentage points","It rises 2.8 percentage points","It rises 3.4 percentage points","It does not change"],"a":0,"e":"Old: 3 + 1.4(6) = 11.4%. New: 5 + 1.4(6) = 13.4%. The whole SML shifts up in parallel by the 2-point rise in rf. The 2.8 figure wrongly multiplies the rf change by beta.","d":2},{"q":"Investors become more risk averse and the market risk premium rises from 5% to 7% (the risk-free rate stays 3%). By how much does the required return of a stock with beta 1.5 rise?","o":["2.0 percentage points","3.0 percentage points","1.5 percentage points","7.0 percentage points"],"a":1,"e":"Required return rises by beta x change in premium = 1.5 x 2% = 3 points. A higher-beta stock is hit harder when the premium rises; a beta of 1.0 stock would rise 2 points.","d":2},{"q":"After you add many stocks from different industries to a portfolio, what kind of risk remains?","o":["Company-specific risk","Zero risk","Default risk on each individual bond","Market (systematic) risk"],"a":3,"e":"Diversification removes unsystematic risk but cannot remove risk that affects the whole economy, such as recessions, interest-rate changes, or inflation.","d":2},{"q":"You hold 50% in Stock X (standard deviation 20%) and 50% in Stock Y (standard deviation 10%). The two stocks are perfectly positively correlated (+1.0). What is the portfolio standard deviation?","o":["10%","12.2%","15%","30%"],"a":2,"e":"With correlation +1.0 the portfolio standard deviation is just the weighted average: 0.5(20) + 0.5(10) = 15%. Only when correlation is below +1.0 does the portfolio standard deviation fall below the weighted average.","d":2},{"q":"A stock has a standard deviation of 30%, the market has a standard deviation of 20%, and the correlation between them is 0.8. What is the stock's beta?","o":["0.8","1.2","1.5","0.53"],"a":1,"e":"Beta = correlation x (stock SD / market SD) = 0.8 x (30/20) = 1.2. Using 0.53 would flip the ratio (20/30).","d":2},{"q":"The risk-free rate is 4% and the expected market return is 9%. A stock has a required return of 14%. What is its beta?","o":["2.0","1.4","2.8","1.0"],"a":0,"e":"14% = 4% + beta(9% - 4%), so beta = 10% / 5% = 2.0. Dividing 14 by 5 gives 2.8, which forgets to subtract rf first.","d":2},{"q":"You hold a $100,000 portfolio with a beta of 1.0. You sell $20,000 of a stock with beta 0.8 and use the proceeds to buy a stock with beta 1.8. What is the new portfolio beta?","o":["1.00","1.80","1.20","1.10"],"a":2,"e":"Portfolio beta changes by (weight of the swap) x (new beta - old beta) = 0.20 x (1.8 - 0.8) = +0.20, so 1.20. The swapped slice's contribution moves from 0.20 x 0.8 = 0.16 to 0.20 x 1.8 = 0.36. Choosing 1.10 averages the two betas without weights.","d":3},{"q":"The risk-free rate is 3%. A stock with a beta of 0.9 has a required return of 9.3%. What is the expected market return?","o":["10%","9.3%","12.3%","7%"],"a":0,"e":"9.3 = 3 + 0.9(rm - 3), so rm - 3 = 6.3/0.9 = 7, and rm = 10%. The 7% is the market risk premium, not the market return.","d":3},{"q":"An investor puts 60% of her money in a stock with beta 1.5 and 40% in Treasury bills. If rf is 3% and rm is 9%, what is the portfolio's required return?","o":["9.0%","8.4%","10.5%","6.0%"],"a":1,"e":"Beta of T-bills is 0, so portfolio beta = 0.6(1.5) + 0.4(0) = 0.90. Required return = 3% + 0.90(6%) = 8.4%. Using 9% ignores the T-bill allocation.","d":3},{"q":"Three investments: A (expected return 12%, SD 9%), B (expected 8%, SD 4.8%), C (expected 20%, SD 16%). Which has the lowest risk per unit of return?","o":["A, with a CV of 0.75","B, with a CV of 0.60","C, because it has the highest expected return","B, because it has the lowest expected return"],"a":1,"e":"CV = SD / expected return: A = 0.75, B = 0.60, C = 0.80. B has the lowest coefficient of variation. A high expected return alone (C) does not make an asset less risky per unit of return.","d":3},{"q":"You hold 60% in Stock M (SD 20%) and 40% in Stock N (SD 30%). The correlation between them is 0.5. What is the portfolio standard deviation?","o":["About 20.8%","24.0%","About 18.0%","About 31.8%"],"a":0,"e":"Variance = (0.6^2)(20^2) + (0.4^2)(30^2) + 2(0.6)(0.4)(0.5)(20)(30) = 144 + 144 + 144 = 432. SD = square root of 432 = 20.78%. The weighted-average SD (24%) is only correct when correlation is +1.0, so diversification lowers it here.","d":3},{"q":"The risk-free rate is 3% and the expected market return is 8%. Analysts forecast a 10.5% return for a stock with beta 1.5. Where does the stock plot relative to the SML?","o":["On the SML; it is fairly valued","Above the SML; it is undervalued","Below the SML; it is overvalued","It cannot be determined without the standard deviation"],"a":0,"e":"Required return = 3% + 1.5(5%) = 10.5%, exactly the forecast return. The stock is on the line, so it is in equilibrium. Standard deviation is irrelevant to CAPM.","d":3},{"q":"Stock Q has a standard deviation of 40% but a beta of 0.6. The risk-free rate is 3% and the market return is 9%. Under CAPM, Stock Q's required return is","o":["27%, because total risk is high","9.0%, the market return","3.0%, the risk-free rate","6.6%, because only beta matters for a diversified investor"],"a":3,"e":"Required return = 3% + 0.6(6%) = 6.6%. Most of Stock Q's high standard deviation is company-specific and diversifiable, which the market does not reward.","d":3},{"q":"A fund earns +20% in year 1 and -10% in year 2. What is the total holding-period return over the two years?","o":["5.0%","10.0%","-2.0%","8.0%"],"a":3,"e":"Returns compound: (1.20)(0.90) - 1 = 1.08 - 1 = 8%. Adding the returns (+10%) or averaging (5% per year) is not the cumulative return.","d":3},{"q":"A firm's expected dividend next year is $2.00, growing at 4% forever. Its beta is 1.0 with rf = 4% and a 5% market risk premium, so the stock's required return is 9% and the price is $40. After an acquisition the beta rises to 1.4 (all else equal). What is the new price using the constant-growth model?","o":["$40.00","About $31.25","About $28.57","About $57.14"],"a":2,"e":"New required return = 4% + 1.4(5%) = 11%. P = D1 / (r - g) = 2 / (0.11 - 0.04) = $28.57. A riskier stock has a higher required return and therefore a lower price.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Beta","Sensitivity to market movements"],["Systematic risk","Market-wide, cannot be diversified away"],["Unsystematic risk","Company-specific, diversifiable"],["Market risk premium","Expected market return minus risk-free rate"],["Coefficient of variation","Standard deviation divided by expected return"],["Security Market Line","Required return plotted against beta"],["Standard deviation","Measure of total risk"],["Correlation","How two assets move together"]],"traps":["Trap: Standard deviation tells you how much a stock should earn. Truth: Standard deviation measures total risk; only beta drives the CAPM required return.","Trap: Portfolio standard deviation is the weighted average of the stocks' standard deviations. Truth: That holds only at correlation +1; otherwise it is lower. Return and beta are the ones that average.","Trap: A beta of 1.5 means a 1.5% return. Truth: Beta is a sensitivity, not a return; it means about 50% more volatile than the market.","Trap: In CAPM, required return = beta x market return. Truth: r = rf + beta x (rm - rf); subtract rf first and add it back.","Trap: A stock above the SML is overvalued. Truth: Above the SML means expected return exceeds required return, so it is undervalued (a buy)."],"mnemonic":"CAPM: 'Risk-free plus Beta times Premium' - r = rf + B x (rm - rf). Stock ABOVE the SML = a BARGAIN (undervalued); BELOW = BLOATED price (overvalued).","cram":["HPR = (P1 - P0 + dividend) / P0.","CV = SD / mean; lower CV means less risk per unit of return.","Only systematic risk (beta) earns a premium; unsystematic risk diversifies away.","Portfolio return and beta are weighted averages; portfolio SD generally is not.","CAPM: r = rf + beta x (rm - rf); market risk premium = rm - rf.","Above the SML = undervalued; below the SML = overvalued.","Higher rf shifts the SML up in parallel; higher risk aversion steepens it.","Market beta = 1.0; risk-free beta = 0."],"ytv":[{"title":"Security Market Line Explained | SML vs. CML and CAPM Example","channel":"Farhat Lectures","id":"sQjqq0K4KQ4","why":"Walks through the SML and a CAPM calculation, including over/undervalued stocks."},{"title":"CAPM: Capital Asset Pricing Model | CFA Level 1","channel":"Guillermo Izquierdo","id":"HzAB88o1VAs","why":"Clear derivation of CAPM with beta and the market risk premium."}]},{"id":"u10","title":"Cost of Capital (WACC)","day":5,"summary":"Calculate the cost of debt, preferred stock and equity, weight them at market value, and use WACC as the hurdle rate for projects.","lesson":[{"h":"1. What 'cost of capital' means","body":"<p>Every dollar a company uses to buy equipment, build a plant, or fund inventory came from someone: a bank, bondholders, preferred shareholders, or common shareholders. Each of them wants a return for letting the firm use their money. That required return is the firm's <strong>cost</strong> of that source of financing.</p><p>The <strong>weighted average cost of capital (WACC)</strong> blends all these costs into one number. It is the average rate the firm must pay its investors, so it is also the <strong>hurdle rate</strong>: a project of average risk must earn more than WACC to create value for owners.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> A manufacturer finances itself with bank and bond debt and with stock. If the blended cost of that money is 9%, then a new line that earns only 7% leaves investors worse off, while one earning 12% adds value.</p><p>The WACC formula for three sources is:</p><p><code>WACC = wd x rd x (1 - T) + wp x rp + we x re</code></p><p>where each <em>w</em> is a weight (percent of total financing), <em>rd</em> is the pre-tax cost of debt, <em>T</em> is the tax rate, <em>rp</em> is the cost of preferred and <em>re</em> is the cost of common equity. The weights must add to 100%.</p>"},{"h":"2. Cost of debt (rd) and the tax shield","body":"<p>The cost of debt is the rate the firm would pay on <em>new</em> long-term borrowing today. The best estimate is the <strong>yield to maturity (YTM)</strong> on its existing bonds, <em>not</em> the coupon rate, which reflects past market conditions.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> A 10-year, 6% coupon bond (paid semiannually) trades at $950 with a $1,000 face value. Solving for the periodic yield gives 3.347% per half-year, so YTM = 2 x 3.347% = <strong>6.69%</strong> (a financial calculator or Excel's RATE or YIELD function does this).</p><p>Interest is tax deductible, so the real cost to the firm is lower:</p><p><code>after-tax cost of debt = rd x (1 - T)</code></p><p>With T = 25%: 6.69% x 0.75 = <strong>5.02%</strong>. Intuitively, for every $1 of interest the firm pays, it saves $0.25 in taxes.</p><p>Notes: use the <em>marginal</em> tax rate (the rate on the next dollar of income). A firm that pays no taxes gets no shield, so its after-tax cost equals its pre-tax cost. Debt is usually the cheapest source because lenders have first claim on cash flow (less risk) and because of the tax shield.</p>"},{"h":"3. Cost of preferred stock (rp)","body":"<p>Preferred stock pays a fixed dividend forever and has no maturity, so it is a <strong>perpetuity</strong>. Price = dividend / required return, which rearranges to:</p><p><code>rp = Dp / Pp</code> (or <code>Dp / [Pp x (1 - F)]</code> for a new issue with flotation cost F).</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Preferred stock pays $4 per year and sells for $50. rp = 4 / 50 = <strong>8%</strong>. If the firm issues new preferred with a 4% flotation cost, it nets only $48, so rp = 4 / 48 = 8.33%.</p><p><strong>No tax adjustment:</strong> preferred dividends are paid from after-tax profits, so they are not deductible. Preferred usually costs more than debt (it is riskier) but less than common equity.</p>"},{"h":"4. Cost of common equity (re): CAPM, DDM and flotation costs","body":"<p>Common shareholders get no promised payment, but they still demand a return. Retained earnings are not free: if the firm keeps profits, shareholders expect those dollars to earn at least what they could have earned elsewhere. So <strong>cost of retained earnings = cost of equity</strong>. There are two standard ways to estimate it.</p><p><strong>Method 1: CAPM.</strong> <code>re = rf + beta x (rm - rf)</code>. Example: rf = 3.5%, beta = 1.1, market risk premium = 5.5% gives 3.5 + 1.1(5.5) = <strong>9.55%</strong>.</p><p><strong>Method 2: Dividend growth model (DDM).</strong> If P0 = D1 / (re - g), then <code>re = D1 / P0 + g</code>, a dividend yield plus growth. Example: D0 = $2.50, g = 4%, P0 = $40. D1 = 2.50 x 1.04 = $2.60 (always grow D0 by one year!). re = 2.60/40 + 4% = 6.5% + 4% = <strong>10.5%</strong>. Growth can be estimated as <em>retention ratio x ROE</em>.</p><p><strong>New equity with flotation costs.</strong> Issuing new shares costs money (underwriter fees), so the firm nets less than the market price. With flotation cost F:</p><p><code>re(new) = D1 / [P0 x (1 - F)] + g</code></p><p>Example: D1 = 2.60, P0 = 40, F = 10%, g = 4% gives 2.60 / 36 + 4% = 7.22% + 4% = <strong>11.22%</strong>. New equity always costs more than retained earnings, so the firm uses retained earnings first. Flotation costs are best handled by raising the cost (as here), not by subtracting from project cash flow, and they only apply to the new-equity portion.</p>"},{"h":"5. Capital structure weights","body":"<p>The weights say how much of the firm's financing comes from each source. Three rules:</p><ol><li>Use <strong>market values</strong>, not book values, for stock and for preferred (equity at market is often much bigger than the book number on the balance sheet). Debt market value is close to book unless rates have moved a lot, and book debt is often used as a stand-in.</li><li>Prefer the firm's <strong>target</strong> capital structure, the mix it plans to maintain over time, over a temporary mix.</li><li>Include only <strong>long-term sources</strong> that carry an explicit cost: long-term debt, preferred stock, common equity. Ignore accounts payable and accruals.</li></ol><p><strong>Example:</strong> A firm has 2 million shares at $30, 100,000 preferred shares at $50, and debt with a market value of $35 million.</p><ul><li>Equity = $60,000,000 (60%)</li><li>Preferred = $5,000,000 (5%)</li><li>Debt = $35,000,000 (35%)</li><li>Total = $100,000,000</li></ul><p>If instead you used book equity of, say, $25 million, you would overweight debt and misstate WACC.</p>"},{"h":"6. Full WACC example","body":"<p>Using the firm above: debt 35% at a 7% pre-tax yield, preferred 5% paying $4 on a $50 price, equity 60% with beta 1.1, rf = 3.5%, market risk premium = 5.5%, tax rate 25%.</p><ol><li><strong>After-tax debt:</strong> 7%(1 - 0.25) = 5.25%</li><li><strong>Preferred:</strong> 4 / 50 = 8%</li><li><strong>Equity (CAPM):</strong> 3.5% + 1.1(5.5%) = 9.55%</li><li><strong>WACC</strong> = 0.35(5.25%) + 0.05(8%) + 0.60(9.55%) = 1.8375% + 0.40% + 5.73% = <strong>7.97%</strong></li></ol><p>Any project of average risk for this firm should earn more than about 7.97%.</p><p><strong>Marginal cost of capital (MCC):</strong> when retained earnings run out, the equity cost jumps to the (higher) cost of new stock. The <em>breakpoint</em> = retained earnings available / equity weight. If $10M of retained earnings is available and equity is 60% of the structure, the breakpoint is 10 / 0.6 = $16.67M of total capital. Beyond that, WACC steps up.</p>"},{"h":"7. WACC as a hurdle rate, project risk, and common mistakes","body":"<p>WACC is the correct discount rate only for projects with the <strong>same risk as the firm's existing business</strong> and the same financing mix.</p><ul><li>A <strong>riskier</strong> division or project needs a higher rate. Example: WACC is 9% but the new venture is much riskier, so use 11% for it. A project with a 10% IRR passes at 9% but fails at 11%.</li><li>A <strong>safer</strong> project can use a lower rate.</li><li>Divisions are often given their own rates, using the betas of pure-play companies in that industry.</li></ul><p><strong>Common mistakes to avoid:</strong></p><ul><li>Using the coupon rate instead of YTM for debt.</li><li>Forgetting the (1 - T) on debt, or applying it to preferred.</li><li>Using D0 instead of D1 in the DDM.</li><li>Using book values for equity weights.</li><li>Treating retained earnings as free.</li><li>Using one company-wide WACC for every project regardless of risk.</li><li>Using flotation costs on retained earnings.</li></ul><p>Also remember: adding more debt does not automatically lower WACC. Debt is cheap, but more of it raises the risk, and therefore the required returns, of both lenders and shareholders.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["The weighted average cost of capital is the blended rate the firm must pay all of its investors, and it serves as the hurdle rate for projects of average risk.","The cost of debt should be the yield to maturity on the firm's bonds, not the old coupon rate.","The after tax cost of debt equals the pre tax cost times one minus the tax rate, because interest is tax deductible.","The cost of preferred stock equals the preferred dividend divided by the preferred price, and it is not adjusted for taxes.","The CAPM cost of equity equals the risk free rate plus beta times the market risk premium.","The dividend growth model cost of equity equals next year's dividend divided by today's price, plus the growth rate. Remember to grow the last dividend by one year.","The cost of new common stock is higher than the cost of retained earnings because flotation costs reduce the money the firm actually receives.","Retained earnings are not free. They carry an opportunity cost equal to the cost of common equity.","Use market value weights, and prefer the target capital structure, when computing WACC.","WACC equals the debt weight times the after tax cost of debt, plus the preferred weight times the cost of preferred, plus the equity weight times the cost of equity.","A project that is riskier than the firm's average should be discounted at a higher, risk adjusted rate, not at the company wide WACC.","Raising taxes lowers the after tax cost of debt and therefore lowers WACC, but adding too much debt raises the required returns on both debt and equity."],"examples":[{"q":"A 10-year, 6% coupon bond (semiannual payments, $1,000 face) sells for $950. The firm's marginal tax rate is 25%. Find the pre-tax and after-tax cost of debt.","steps":["There are 20 half-year periods, payment = $30 each, FV = $1,000, PV = -$950.","Solve for the periodic yield: 3.347% per half-year (calculator RATE / I/Y).","Annual YTM = 2 x 3.347% = 6.69% (pre-tax cost of debt).","After-tax cost = 6.69% x (1 - 0.25) = 5.02%."],"answer":"Pre-tax rd is about 6.69%; after-tax rd is about 5.02%."},{"q":"A company's stock sells for $40. It just paid D0 = $2.50, dividends grow 4% per year forever, and it must pay a 10% flotation cost on new stock. Find the cost of retained earnings and the cost of new common stock.","steps":["D1 = D0 x (1 + g) = 2.50 x 1.04 = $2.60.","Retained earnings: re = D1/P0 + g = 2.60/40 + 0.04 = 0.065 + 0.04 = 10.5%.","New stock net price = 40 x (1 - 0.10) = $36.","New equity: re(new) = 2.60/36 + 0.04 = 0.0722 + 0.04 = 11.22%."],"answer":"Retained earnings cost 10.5%; new common stock costs about 11.22%."},{"q":"Compute WACC. Equity: 2,000,000 shares at $30, beta 1.1. Preferred: 100,000 shares at $50 paying $4. Debt: market value $35 million, YTM 7%. rf = 3.5%, market risk premium = 5.5%, tax rate = 25%.","steps":["Market values: equity $60M, preferred $5M, debt $35M, total $100M. Weights: 60%, 5%, 35%.","Cost of equity (CAPM) = 3.5% + 1.1(5.5%) = 9.55%.","Cost of preferred = 4/50 = 8%. After-tax cost of debt = 7%(0.75) = 5.25%.","WACC = 0.35(5.25) + 0.05(8) + 0.60(9.55) = 1.8375 + 0.40 + 5.73 = 7.9675%."],"answer":"WACC is about 7.97%."}],"videos":[{"title":"WACC weighted average cost of capital explained","query":"WACC weighted average cost of capital explained example"},{"title":"Cost of equity CAPM and dividend growth model","query":"cost of equity CAPM dividend growth model flotation costs"},{"title":"After-tax cost of debt and YTM","query":"after-tax cost of debt yield to maturity WACC"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"WACC","b":"Blended cost of all long-term capital; the hurdle rate for average-risk projects."},{"f":"WACC formula","b":"wd rd (1 - T) + wp rp + we re."},{"f":"Pre-tax cost of debt","b":"Yield to maturity on the firm's bonds."},{"f":"After-tax cost of debt","b":"rd x (1 - T)."},{"f":"Why use YTM and not coupon?","b":"YTM reflects today's market cost of borrowing."},{"f":"Cost of preferred stock","b":"Dp / Pp (divided by net price if new issue)."},{"f":"Is preferred adjusted for tax?","b":"No; dividends are not tax deductible."},{"f":"CAPM cost of equity","b":"rf + beta (rm - rf)."},{"f":"DDM cost of equity","b":"D1 / P0 + g."},{"f":"D1 in the DDM","b":"D0 x (1 + g)."},{"f":"Growth rate estimate","b":"Retention ratio x ROE."},{"f":"Cost of new equity","b":"D1 / [P0 (1 - F)] + g."},{"f":"Flotation cost","b":"Fee to issue securities; reduces net proceeds."},{"f":"Cost of retained earnings","b":"Equals cost of common equity (opportunity cost)."},{"f":"Which weights to use?","b":"Target weights at market value."},{"f":"Why not book value weights?","b":"Book equity understates what investors' capital is worth."},{"f":"Retained-earnings breakpoint","b":"Retained earnings available / equity weight."},{"f":"Marginal cost of capital","b":"Cost of the next dollar raised; rises after breakpoint."},{"f":"Hurdle rate","b":"Minimum return a project must earn."},{"f":"Divisional cost of capital","b":"Risk-adjusted rate using that division's own risk."},{"f":"Effect of higher tax rate on WACC","b":"Lowers after-tax debt cost, so WACC falls."},{"f":"Sources excluded from WACC","b":"Accounts payable and accruals (no explicit cost)."}],"questions":[{"q":"A firm can borrow at 8%. Its marginal tax rate is 25%. What is its after-tax cost of debt?","o":["8.0%","2.0%","10.0%","6.0%"],"a":3,"e":"After-tax cost of debt = rd(1 - T) = 8%(1 - 0.25) = 6%. Interest is tax deductible, so the government effectively pays 25% of it.","d":1},{"q":"A company's preferred stock pays a $5 annual dividend and sells for $50 per share. What is the cost of preferred stock (ignoring flotation costs)?","o":["5%","10%","12.5%","45%"],"a":1,"e":"Preferred is a perpetuity, so rp = Dp / Pp = 5 / 50 = 10%.","d":1},{"q":"Why is the cost of debt multiplied by (1 - T) in the WACC formula?","o":["Debt is less risky than equity","Bondholders pay the tax on the firm's behalf","Interest expense is tax deductible, which lowers the true cost","Dividends are tax deductible"],"a":2,"e":"Interest reduces taxable income, creating a tax shield. Dividends on preferred and common stock are paid from after-tax income, so they get no such adjustment.","d":1},{"q":"Which capital structure weights are best to use in the WACC calculation?","o":["Target weights based on market values","Historical weights based on book values","Weights based on the coupon rates","Equal weights for each source"],"a":0,"e":"WACC should reflect the firm's future financing mix, and market values show what investors would require today. Book values are historical and can badly misstate the mix.","d":1},{"q":"The WACC is best described as","o":["The minimum return a project must earn to satisfy investors (the hurdle rate for average-risk projects)","The firm's accounting profit margin","The coupon rate on the firm's newest bond","The cheapest source of financing the firm has"],"a":0,"e":"WACC is the blended cost of all capital. A project of average risk must earn at least WACC or it destroys value.","d":1},{"q":"Does the firm's retained earnings have a cost?","o":["No, because the firm does not pay anything to use it","Yes, an opportunity cost equal to the cost of common equity","Yes, equal to the after-tax cost of debt","Yes, equal to the preferred dividend yield"],"a":1,"e":"Shareholders could have received the earnings and invested them elsewhere at re, so keeping them must earn at least re. Retained earnings are not free.","d":1},{"q":"rf = 3%, beta = 1.2, and the market risk premium is 5%. What is the CAPM cost of equity?","o":["6.0%","11.4%","8.0%","9.0%"],"a":3,"e":"re = 3% + 1.2(5%) = 3% + 6% = 9%. The 6% figure is only the beta times the premium.","d":1},{"q":"Which component of WACC is NOT adjusted for taxes?","o":["Cost of preferred stock","Cost of debt","Both debt and preferred","None; all are tax adjusted"],"a":0,"e":"Only interest is deductible. Preferred dividends are paid out of after-tax profit, so rp is used as is.","d":1},{"q":"A stock just paid a dividend of $2.00 (D0). Dividends are expected to grow at 5% forever and the stock sells for $42. What is the cost of equity using the dividend growth model?","o":["9.8%","5.0%","10.0%","9.5%"],"a":2,"e":"D1 = 2.00 x 1.05 = 2.10. re = D1/P0 + g = 2.10/42 + 5% = 5% + 5% = 10%. Using D0 instead of D1 gives 4.76% + 5% = 9.76% (about 9.8%), which is wrong.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's 7% coupon bonds currently have a yield to maturity of 9%. The tax rate is 30%. What is the firm's after-tax cost of debt?","o":["4.9%","9.0%","2.7%","6.3%"],"a":3,"e":"The cost of debt is today's market yield (YTM), not the old coupon rate. After tax: 9%(1 - 0.30) = 6.3%. Using the coupon gives 7%(0.7) = 4.9%.","d":2},{"q":"A firm will issue new common stock. D1 = $3.00, P0 = $50, g = 6%, and flotation costs are 8% of the issue price. What is the cost of new equity?","o":["About 12.5%","About 12.0%","About 11.6%","About 13.5%"],"a":0,"e":"re(new) = D1 / [P0(1 - F)] + g = 3 / (50 x 0.92) + 6% = 3 / 46 + 6% = 6.52% + 6% = 12.52%. Ignoring flotation gives 3/50 + 6 = 12%.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's target capital structure is 40% debt and 60% equity. The pre-tax cost of debt is 6%, the tax rate is 25%, and the cost of equity is 12%. What is its WACC?","o":["9.6%","8.4%","10.5%","9.0%"],"a":3,"e":"WACC = 0.40(6%)(0.75) + 0.60(12%) = 1.8% + 7.2% = 9.0%. Forgetting the tax adjustment gives 0.4(6) + 0.6(12) = 9.6%.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has 5 million shares selling at $12 and the market value of its debt is $40 million. What weight of debt does it use in WACC (no preferred stock)?","o":["25%","60%","50%","40%"],"a":3,"e":"Equity market value = 5M x $12 = $60M. Total = $100M. Debt weight = 40/100 = 40%.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's capital structure weights are 30% debt (pre-tax cost 7%, tax rate 20%), 10% preferred (cost 9%), and 60% common equity (cost 13%). What is the WACC?","o":["10.20%","11.20%","9.73%","10.38%"],"a":3,"e":"WACC = 0.30(7%)(0.80) + 0.10(9%) + 0.60(13%) = 1.68% + 0.90% + 7.80% = 10.38%. Skipping the tax adjustment gives 2.1 + 0.9 + 7.8 = 10.8%.","d":2},{"q":"A company reports book equity of $20 million but its market capitalization is $80 million. Debt is $20 million (book and market). In computing WACC, the equity weight should be","o":["50%","80%","20%","25%"],"a":1,"e":"Use market values: equity $80M / ($80M + $20M) = 80%. Book weights would give 50%, which understates what shareholders' capital is actually worth.","d":2},{"q":"A project of average risk has an IRR of 10%. The firm's WACC is 9.5%. The best decision is to","o":["Reject, because IRR is above the cost of debt","Reject, because WACC includes equity","Accept, because IRR exceeds the hurdle rate","Accept only if the project is financed entirely with debt"],"a":2,"e":"If the project returns more than the cost of the capital used to fund it (IRR 10% > WACC 9.5%), it adds value. The financing source does not change the hurdle rate for an average-risk project.","d":2},{"q":"A firm uses 50% debt (pre-tax 8%) and 50% equity (12%). If the tax rate rises from 25% to 40% (all costs unchanged), what happens to WACC?","o":["It rises from 9.0% to 9.6%","It falls from 9.0% to 8.4%","It stays at 9.0%","It falls from 10.0% to 9.2%"],"a":1,"e":"At 25%: 0.5(8)(0.75) + 0.5(12) = 3 + 6 = 9.0%. At 40%: 0.5(8)(0.60) + 6 = 2.4 + 6 = 8.4%. A higher tax rate makes the interest shield more valuable and lowers the after-tax cost of debt.","d":2},{"q":"A preferred issue pays a $6.00 dividend. The market price is $60 and flotation costs on a new issue are 4% of the price. What is the cost of new preferred stock?","o":["About 10.4%","10.0%","About 9.6%","About 6.4%"],"a":0,"e":"rp = Dp / [Pp(1 - F)] = 6 / (60 x 0.96) = 6 / 57.6 = 10.42%. Ignoring flotation costs gives 10%.","d":2},{"q":"A firm earns ROE of 12% and retains 50% of its earnings, so g = retention x ROE = 6%. Next year's dividend is $1.50 and the price is $30. What is the cost of equity (DDM)?","o":["11%","5%","6%","17%"],"a":0,"e":"re = D1/P0 + g = 1.50/30 + 6% = 5% + 6% = 11%.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's overall WACC is 9%. A new division operates in a business much riskier than the firm's average and managers decide a 2 percentage-point risk premium applies. A project in that division has an IRR of 10%. What should the firm do?","o":["Accept, because 10% exceeds the firm WACC of 9%","Reject, because the division's hurdle rate is 11%","Accept, because risk is already included in WACC","Reject, because the cost of debt is higher"],"a":1,"e":"Risky divisions should be evaluated at a risk-adjusted rate (9% + 2% = 11%). Using the firm-wide WACC would accept projects that do not compensate for their risk.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a component used when calculating a firm's WACC?","o":["Accounts payable to suppliers (a spontaneous liability)","Long-term bonds","Preferred stock","Retained earnings"],"a":0,"e":"WACC includes only capital that investors supply and charge explicitly for: long-term debt, preferred, and common equity (including retained earnings). Trade credit is typically treated as operating, not capital.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has 4 million shares at $25 ($100M equity, beta 1.1), bonds with a market value of $50M and a YTM of 8%, and 200,000 preferred shares worth $25 each paying a $2.00 dividend. rf = 3.5%, market risk premium = 5.5%, tax rate = 25%. What is the WACC?","o":["About 9.55%","About 8.95%","About 8.35%","About 7.80%"],"a":2,"e":"Equity cost = 3.5 + 1.1(5.5) = 9.55%. Preferred cost = 2/25 = 8%. After-tax debt = 8(0.75) = 6%. Values: debt $50M, preferred $5M, equity $100M, total $155M. Weights: 32.26%, 3.23%, 64.52%. WACC = 0.3226(6) + 0.0323(8) + 0.6452(9.55) = 1.935 + 0.258 + 6.161 = 8.35%.","d":3},{"q":"A 10-year, 5% annual-coupon bond with a $1,000 face value sells for $926.40, which gives a YTM of 6.0%. The firm's tax rate is 30%. What is the after-tax cost of debt?","o":["3.5%","6.0%","4.2%","5.0%"],"a":2,"e":"The pre-tax cost of debt is the YTM (6%), not the coupon. After tax: 6%(1 - 0.30) = 4.2%. Using the coupon gives 5%(0.7) = 3.5%.","d":3},{"q":"A firm will have $10 million of retained earnings available this year. Its target capital structure is 40% debt and 60% equity. At what total capital budget does the firm run out of retained earnings and have to issue new common stock (the retained-earnings breakpoint)?","o":["$10.00 million","$25.00 million","About $16.67 million","About $6.00 million"],"a":2,"e":"Breakpoint = retained earnings / equity weight = 10 / 0.60 = $16.67 million. Up to that budget, 60% of the funding (up to $10M) can come from retained earnings.","d":3},{"q":"Target structure: 40% debt, 60% equity. After-tax cost of debt is 4.5% (6% pre-tax, T = 25%). Cost of retained earnings is 12%; cost of new common stock is 13%. What is the WACC for capital raised ABOVE the retained-earnings breakpoint?","o":["9.6%","9.0%","9.2%","10.5%"],"a":0,"e":"Above the breakpoint, equity comes from new stock: WACC = 0.40(4.5%) + 0.60(13%) = 1.8% + 7.8% = 9.6%. Below the breakpoint it would be 1.8 + 7.2 = 9.0%. The marginal cost of capital steps up when flotation costs apply.","d":3},{"q":"A firm is 30% debt / 70% equity with pre-tax rd = 6%, T = 25%, and re = 11%. Management recapitalizes to 50% debt / 50% equity, but lenders and shareholders respond by demanding rd = 7.5% and re = 12.5%. What happens to WACC?","o":["It falls from 9.05% to about 8.0%","It rises slightly from 9.05% to about 9.06%","It falls because debt is cheaper than equity","It stays exactly the same"],"a":1,"e":"Before: 0.3(6)(0.75) + 0.7(11) = 1.35 + 7.70 = 9.05%. After: 0.5(7.5)(0.75) + 0.5(12.5) = 2.8125 + 6.25 = 9.0625%. More debt raises the cost of both debt and equity, which can offset the benefit of cheap debt.","d":3},{"q":"The firm's WACC is 10%. A division's CAPM-based required return is 11.4% (rf 3%, beta 1.4, MRP 6%). A project in that division has an IRR of 10.8%. Which decision is correct?","o":["Accept, because 10.8% exceeds the firm WACC of 10%","Accept, because the IRR is above the risk-free rate","Reject, because the IRR is below the firm's cost of equity","Reject, because 10.8% is below the division's risk-adjusted 11.4%"],"a":3,"e":"A project should be discounted at a rate that reflects its own risk. The division's required return is 11.4%, so a 10.8% IRR fails the test even though it clears the firm-wide WACC.","d":3},{"q":"D0 = $2.50, growth is 4% forever, the current price is $40, and flotation costs on new stock are 10%. What is the cost of new equity?","o":["About 10.3%","About 11.2%","About 10.4%","About 12.5%"],"a":1,"e":"D1 = 2.50(1.04) = 2.60. re(new) = 2.60 / (40 x 0.90) + 4% = 2.60/36 + 4% = 7.22% + 4% = 11.22%. Using D0 without growing it gives 2.5/36 + 4 = 10.9%; ignoring flotation gives 10.5%.","d":3},{"q":"A company has had losses for years and expects to pay no income tax in the foreseeable future. It can borrow at 8%. What is its effective after-tax cost of debt?","o":["6.0%","8.0%","0%","12.0%"],"a":1,"e":"The tax shield only has value if the firm owes taxes. With an effective tax rate of 0%, after-tax cost = 8%(1 - 0) = 8%.","d":3},{"q":"A firm's actual financing is 30% debt, but its target is 45% debt and 55% equity. Pre-tax rd = 7%, T = 28%, re = 12.5%. What WACC should it use for evaluating new projects?","o":["About 9.01%","About 10.45%","About 9.14%","About 8.50%"],"a":2,"e":"Use target weights: 0.45(7%)(0.72) + 0.55(12.5%) = 2.268% + 6.875% = 9.14%. Using the current 30/70 mix would give 0.3(5.04) + 0.7(12.5) = 10.26%, which is not the going-forward mix.","d":3}],"pairs":[["WACC","Blended cost of firm's financing"],["After-tax cost of debt","rd times one minus tax rate"],["Cost of preferred","Dividend divided by price"],["CAPM cost of equity","rf plus beta times premium"],["DDM cost of equity","D1 over P0 plus growth"],["Flotation costs","Issue fees that raise new-equity cost"],["Hurdle rate","Minimum acceptable project return"],["Target weights","Planned financing mix at market values"]],"traps":["Trap: Use the bond's coupon rate as the cost of debt. Truth: Use today's YTM; the coupon reflects old market conditions.","Trap: Apply (1 - T) to every component. Truth: Only debt gets the tax adjustment; preferred and common equity do not.","Trap: In DDM, plug in the dividend just paid. Truth: Use D1 = D0 x (1 + g).","Trap: Retained earnings cost nothing. Truth: They cost re, the shareholders' opportunity cost; new stock costs even more because of flotation.","Trap: Weight capital by book value from the balance sheet. Truth: Use market values (ideally target weights), especially for equity."],"mnemonic":"WACC = 'Weights x After-tax Costs of Capital.' Only the D gets the tax discount: Debt x (1 - T), then Preferred, then Equity - 'D gets the discount, P and E pay full price.'","cram":["WACC = wd rd(1 - T) + wp rp + we re; weights sum to 100%.","Cost of debt = YTM, then multiply by (1 - T).","Cost of preferred = Dp / Pp; no tax adjustment.","CAPM: re = rf + beta(rm - rf). DDM: re = D1/P0 + g.","New equity: re = D1 / [P0(1 - F)] + g.","Use target, market-value weights; ignore accounts payable.","Retained-earnings breakpoint = retained earnings / equity weight.","Riskier project or division needs a higher, risk-adjusted discount rate."],"ytv":[{"title":"WACC Explained for Beginners (Weighted Average Cost of Capital)","channel":"Ryan O'Connell, CFA, FRM","id":"crMSEf3_8X0","why":"Step-by-step WACC build-up with cost of equity, debt and weights."},{"title":"Weighted Average Cost of Capital (WACC) Explained","channel":"Ryan O'Connell, CFA, FRM","id":"q7MF4vCUDSI","why":"Second angle on WACC with worked examples."},{"title":"What Is WACC? Weighted Average Cost of Capital in 2 Minutes","channel":"Steve Willis ACCA","id":"j_3_ISs18Gc","why":"Fast two-minute refresher before the exam."}]},{"id":"u11","title":"Capital Budgeting","day":6,"summary":"Evaluate projects with NPV, IRR, MIRR, PI and payback, build correct incremental cash flows, and handle risk and real options.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Capital budgeting and Net Present Value (NPV)","body":"<p><strong>Capital budgeting</strong> is how a firm decides which long-term investments (new plants, machines, product lines) to undertake. The goal is to pick projects that increase the value of the firm.</p><p><strong>NPV</strong> converts every future cash flow to today's dollars using the required return (usually WACC) and subtracts the initial cost:</p><p><code>NPV = CF0 + CF1/(1+r) + CF2/(1+r)^2 + ... + CFn/(1+r)^n</code></p><ul><li>NPV greater than 0: accept (adds value).</li><li>NPV less than 0: reject.</li><li>NPV equals 0: indifferent (earns exactly the required return).</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> A bakery buys a new oven for $100,000. It expects cash inflows of $30,000, $35,000, $40,000, $30,000 and $25,000 over five years. At 10%, the present values are 27,273 + 28,926 + 30,053 + 20,490 + 15,523 = $122,264. NPV = 122,264 - 100,000 = <strong>$22,264</strong>. Accept.</p><p><strong>Why NPV is best:</strong> it uses all cash flows, accounts for timing (time value of money), measures value in dollars (the number that matters to shareholders), and assumes cash flows are reinvested at the realistic required return.</p>"},{"h":"2. Internal Rate of Return (IRR) and MIRR","body":"<p><strong>IRR</strong> is the discount rate that makes NPV exactly zero. It is the project's own break-even rate of return, found by trial and error or with a calculator or Excel's <code>=IRR()</code>.</p><p><strong>Rule:</strong> for an independent project, accept if IRR is greater than the required return (WACC).</p><p>For the oven project above, IRR = <strong>18.59%</strong>, which is above 10%, so accept, consistent with the NPV rule.</p><p><strong>Problems with IRR:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Multiple IRRs:</strong> if cash flows change sign more than once (for example -100, +230, -132), there can be more than one IRR (here 10% and 20%). The number is meaningless.</li><li><strong>Reinvestment assumption:</strong> IRR assumes interim cash flows are reinvested at the IRR itself, which is too optimistic for a very high IRR. NPV assumes reinvestment at the required return, which is more realistic.</li><li><strong>Conflicts with NPV</strong> for mutually exclusive projects (section 4).</li></ul><p><strong>MIRR (modified IRR)</strong> fixes both flaws: compound all inflows forward to the end of the project at the WACC (the terminal value), discount all outflows back to time 0 at the WACC, and find the single rate that links them. <strong>Example:</strong> cost $1,000, inflows $500 for 3 years, WACC 10%. Terminal value = 500(1.21) + 500(1.1) + 500 = 1,655. MIRR = (1,655/1,000)^(1/3) - 1 = <strong>18.3%</strong>.</p>"},{"h":"3. Profitability index, payback and discounted payback","body":"<p><strong>Profitability index (PI)</strong> = PV of future cash flows / initial investment. Accept if PI is greater than 1.0 (same decision as NPV greater than 0). It shows value per dollar invested and is handy when capital is limited. Oven project: 122,264 / 100,000 = 1.22.</p><p><strong>Payback period</strong> = years to recover the initial investment from cash inflows. Oven: cumulative inflows are 30,000, 65,000, 105,000, so payback = 2 + (35,000/40,000) = <strong>2.875 years</strong>.</p><ul><li>Flaws: ignores the time value of money, ignores cash flows after the cutoff, and the cutoff is arbitrary.</li><li>Strengths: simple, shows liquidity and risk (how quickly money comes back).</li></ul><p><strong>Discounted payback</strong> uses the present value of each cash flow. Oven at 10%: cumulative PV is -72,727, -43,802, -13,749, +6,741, so discounted payback = 3 + 13,749/20,490 = <strong>3.67 years</strong>. It fixes the time-value flaw but still ignores flows after payback and can reject positive-NPV projects.</p><p>Exam tip: discounted payback is always longer than regular payback.</p>"},{"h":"4. Independent vs mutually exclusive projects, NPV profile and crossover rate","body":"<p><strong>Independent projects</strong> can all be accepted if each is good; NPV and IRR always give the same answer. <strong>Mutually exclusive projects</strong> compete (you can only build one plant on that lot). Here NPV and IRR can disagree, usually because of differences in <em>size</em> or in the <em>timing</em> of cash flows.</p><p>An <strong>NPV profile</strong> graphs a project's NPV against the discount rate. It intercepts the vertical axis at the sum of undiscounted cash flows (NPV at 0%), slopes downward, and crosses the horizontal axis at the IRR.</p><p>The <strong>crossover rate</strong> is the discount rate where two projects' profiles cross, so both have the same NPV. It is the IRR of the difference in their cash flows.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Project A: -1,000, 500, 400, 300, 100 (IRR 14.49%). Project B: -1,000, 100, 300, 400, 700 (IRR 14.11%). The crossover is 13.49%.</p><ul><li>At 10%: NPV(A) = $78.82; NPV(B) = $117.48. B is better by NPV, but A has the higher IRR: conflict.</li><li>At 15% (above the crossover): NPV(A) = -8.33, NPV(B) = -22.97. They agree.</li></ul><p><strong>Rule:</strong> when they conflict, <strong>choose NPV</strong>. Back-loaded projects (like B) are hurt most when the discount rate rises.</p><p><strong>Unequal lives:</strong> if projects can be repeated, compare the <em>equivalent annual annuity</em> (EAA = NPV / annuity factor) or use a common life.</p>"},{"h":"5. Relevant (incremental) cash flows","body":"<p>Only <strong>incremental cash flows</strong>, those that change because of the decision, matter. Work with after-tax cash, not accounting profit.</p><ul><li><strong>Sunk costs: exclude.</strong> Money already spent (a market study, past R and D) cannot be recovered either way.</li><li><strong>Opportunity costs: include.</strong> If you use land you already own, count the after-tax amount you could have sold it for.</li><li><strong>Side effects: include.</strong> Cannibalization of existing products reduces cash flows (after tax); complementary sales may add to them.</li><li><strong>Change in net working capital:</strong> extra inventory and receivables (less extra payables) are an outflow at the start; the NWC is recovered at the end.</li><li><strong>Financing costs: exclude.</strong> Interest and dividends are already captured in the discount rate. Counting them double-counts.</li></ul><p><strong>Operating cash flow</strong> each year: <code>OCF = (Revenue - Cash costs)(1 - T) + T x Depreciation</code>. The second term is the <strong>depreciation tax shield</strong>: depreciation is non-cash but saves taxes. <strong>Example:</strong> $80,000 depreciation at T = 25% saves $20,000 per year.</p><p><strong>Initial outlay</strong> = equipment cost + shipping and installation (these are capitalized and depreciated) + increase in NWC - after-tax proceeds from selling old equipment.</p><p><strong>Terminal (year-N) cash flow</strong> = OCF + recovery of NWC + after-tax salvage. After-tax salvage = Salvage - T x (Salvage - Book value). Example: sold for $50,000 with a $20,000 book value at 25%: 50,000 - 0.25(30,000) = $42,500.</p>"},{"h":"6. Full worked project example","body":"<p>A firm considers a new machine. Facts: equipment $500,000 plus $50,000 shipping and installation (depreciable basis $550,000, straight-line over 5 years to zero). Revenue increases by $300,000 per year and cash costs by $120,000 per year. NWC rises $30,000 at the start and is recovered at the end. The machine sells for $60,000 in year 5 (book value zero). Tax rate 25%, WACC 10%. A $25,000 feasibility study already paid is a sunk cost and is ignored.</p><ol><li>Annual depreciation = 550,000 / 5 = $110,000.</li><li>Initial outlay (t = 0) = -550,000 - 30,000 = <strong>-$580,000</strong>.</li><li>OCF = (300,000 - 120,000)(0.75) + 0.25(110,000) = 135,000 + 27,500 = <strong>$162,500</strong> each year.</li><li>After-tax salvage = 60,000 - 0.25(60,000 - 0) = $45,000.</li><li>Year 5 total = 162,500 + 30,000 (NWC) + 45,000 = <strong>$237,500</strong>.</li><li>NPV at 10% = -580,000 + 162,500 x PVIFA(10%, 4) + 237,500/1.1^5 ... computed term by term = <strong>$82,572</strong>. IRR is about 15.14%.</li></ol><p>NPV is positive and IRR exceeds WACC, so accept.</p>"},{"h":"7. Risk analysis, real options and Excel","body":"<p>Forecasts are uncertain, so we test how robust the NPV is.</p><ul><li><strong>Sensitivity analysis:</strong> change one input (sales, cost, discount rate) at a time and see how NPV moves. Shows which variable matters most.</li><li><strong>Scenario analysis:</strong> change several inputs together into worst, base and best cases, each with a probability. Expected NPV = sum of probability x NPV. Example: 25% at -40,000, 50% at 30,000, 25% at 110,000 gives 32,500.</li><li><strong>Simulation (Monte Carlo):</strong> repeat the model thousands of times with random inputs to get a distribution of NPVs.</li></ul><p><strong>Real options</strong> are choices management can make after the project starts: <em>expand</em> if it goes well, <em>abandon</em> if it goes badly, <em>delay</em> the start, or switch inputs. A static NPV ignores these, so it can understate value. A project with NPV -2 million and an expansion option worth 3 million is worth +1 million.</p><p><strong>Excel tips:</strong></p><ul><li><code>=NPV(rate, CF1:CFn)</code> discounts the first cell by one period, so it treats it as year 1. Always add the time-0 cash flow separately: <code>=NPV(rate, B2:B5) + B1</code>.</li><li><code>=IRR(B1:B5)</code> takes all flows including the time-0 cash flow.</li><li>Also useful: <code>=PMT</code>, <code>=RATE</code>, and <code>=MIRR(range, finance_rate, reinvest_rate)</code>.</li></ul>"}],"keyPoints":["Net present value is the present value of all future cash flows minus the initial investment, and a project should be accepted if NPV is greater than zero.","The internal rate of return is the discount rate that makes NPV equal to zero, and an independent project is accepted if IRR is greater than the required return.","NPV is considered the best method because it measures the dollar increase in shareholder wealth and uses a realistic reinvestment assumption.","IRR can give multiple answers when cash flows change sign more than once, and it assumes cash flows are reinvested at the IRR itself.","The modified IRR fixes the reinvestment problem by compounding inflows forward at the cost of capital.","The profitability index equals the present value of inflows divided by the initial investment, and a value above one means accept.","Payback ignores the time value of money and ignores cash flows after the payback date. Discounted payback fixes only the first problem.","For mutually exclusive projects, when NPV and IRR conflict, choose the project with the higher NPV.","The crossover rate is the discount rate at which two projects have the same NPV, and it is the IRR of the difference in their cash flows.","Sunk costs are excluded from capital budgeting, while opportunity costs, side effects like cannibalization, and changes in net working capital are included.","Operating cash flow equals revenue minus cash costs times one minus the tax rate, plus the tax rate times depreciation.","In Excel, the NPV function treats the first cell as period one, so you must add the time zero cash flow separately."],"examples":[{"q":"A project costs $100,000 and produces cash flows of $30,000, $35,000, $40,000, $30,000 and $25,000 in years 1 to 5. The required return is 10%. Find the NPV, IRR (approx.), PI and payback, and decide.","steps":["PVs at 10%: 27,273 + 28,926 + 30,053 + 20,490 + 15,523 = 122,264.","NPV = 122,264 - 100,000 = $22,264.","PI = 122,264 / 100,000 = 1.22. IRR by trial or Excel: about 18.59%.","Payback: cumulative 30,000; 65,000; 105,000. Payback = 2 + 35,000/40,000 = 2.875 years."],"answer":"NPV = $22,264, PI = 1.22, IRR about 18.59%, payback 2.875 years. Accept (NPV greater than 0, IRR greater than 10%)."},{"q":"Mutually exclusive projects: A = (-1,000, 500, 400, 300, 100) and B = (-1,000, 100, 300, 400, 700). WACC is 10%. Which should be chosen? Find the crossover rate.","steps":["NPV(A) at 10% = $78.82; NPV(B) at 10% = $117.48. IRR(A) = 14.49%; IRR(B) = 14.11%.","Rankings conflict: IRR prefers A, NPV prefers B.","Crossover = IRR of (B - A) = (0, -400, -100, 100, 600) = 13.49%.","10% is below the crossover, so the conflict exists. Follow NPV."],"answer":"Choose B (higher NPV, $117.48). The crossover rate is about 13.49%."},{"q":"A new machine costs $500,000 plus $50,000 installation (straight-line over 5 years to zero). Revenue rises $300,000/yr; cash costs rise $120,000/yr. NWC rises $30,000 and is recovered at the end. The machine is sold for $60,000 in year 5. Tax = 25%, WACC = 10%. A $25,000 study was already paid. Find the cash flows and NPV.","steps":["Ignore the $25,000 study (sunk). Depreciation = 550,000/5 = 110,000.","t = 0: -550,000 - 30,000 = -580,000.","OCF = (300,000 - 120,000)(0.75) + 0.25(110,000) = 135,000 + 27,500 = 162,500 for years 1-5.","Year 5 extra: NWC 30,000 + after-tax salvage 60,000(1 - 0.25) = 45,000, so year 5 total = 237,500.","NPV = -580,000 + 162,500(PVIFA 10%, 4 yrs = 3.16987) + 237,500/(1.1^5) = -580,000 + 515,104 + 147,468 = $82,572."],"answer":"NPV is about $82,572 (IRR about 15.14%); accept."}],"videos":[{"title":"NPV and IRR explained with decision rules","query":"NPV and IRR explained capital budgeting decision rules"},{"title":"NPV vs IRR conflict and crossover rate","query":"NPV vs IRR conflict crossover rate mutually exclusive projects"},{"title":"Incremental cash flows depreciation tax shield capital budgeting","query":"capital budgeting incremental cash flows depreciation tax shield salvage NWC"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"NPV","b":"PV of inflows minus initial cost; accept if greater than 0."},{"f":"IRR","b":"Discount rate that makes NPV equal zero."},{"f":"IRR decision rule","b":"Accept if IRR is greater than required return."},{"f":"Why NPV is best","b":"Measures dollar value added; realistic reinvestment rate."},{"f":"Multiple IRR problem","b":"Occurs when cash flows change sign more than once."},{"f":"Reinvestment assumption: NPV","b":"Cash flows reinvested at the required return."},{"f":"Reinvestment assumption: IRR","b":"Cash flows reinvested at the IRR itself."},{"f":"MIRR","b":"IRR using terminal value compounded at the WACC."},{"f":"Profitability index","b":"PV of future cash flows / initial investment."},{"f":"PI rule","b":"Accept if PI is greater than 1.0."},{"f":"Payback period","b":"Years needed to recover the initial investment."},{"f":"Payback flaws","b":"Ignores time value and cash flows after payback."},{"f":"Discounted payback","b":"Years to recover investment using PV of cash flows."},{"f":"Mutually exclusive projects","b":"Competing projects; only one can be chosen."},{"f":"NPV vs IRR conflict","b":"Pick the higher NPV."},{"f":"NPV profile","b":"Graph of NPV against discount rate."},{"f":"Crossover rate","b":"Rate at which two projects have equal NPV."},{"f":"Sunk cost","b":"Already spent; ignore in the analysis."},{"f":"Opportunity cost","b":"Value of next-best use of an asset; include."},{"f":"Depreciation tax shield","b":"T x depreciation; non-cash but saves taxes."},{"f":"After-tax salvage","b":"Salvage - T x (Salvage - book value)."},{"f":"Excel NPV function","b":"Discounts first cell one period; add CF0 outside."}],"questions":[{"q":"A project has a positive NPV. What does the NPV rule say to do?","o":["Reject it, because NPV must exceed the initial investment","Accept it only if the payback is under 3 years","Reject it unless IRR is below WACC","Accept it, because it adds value to shareholders"],"a":3,"e":"NPV is the dollar change in firm value. If NPV is greater than zero, the project earns more than the required return and increases shareholder wealth.","d":1},{"q":"The internal rate of return (IRR) is best defined as","o":["The firm's cost of capital","The discount rate that makes the project's NPV equal to zero","The average accounting profit divided by book investment","The rate at which payback occurs"],"a":1,"e":"IRR is the project's own break-even return: the rate at which PV of inflows equals the initial cost. Accept (for independent projects) if IRR is greater than the required return.","d":1},{"q":"A company already spent $25,000 on a market study before deciding whether to build a new plant. How should this $25,000 be treated in the NPV analysis?","o":["Included as a cash outflow at time 0","Ignored, because it is a sunk cost","Included as an outflow spread over the project life","Included as a tax shield only"],"a":1,"e":"Sunk costs have already been spent and will not change whether you accept or reject. Only incremental (future, decision-dependent) cash flows belong in the analysis.","d":1},{"q":"In Excel, =NPV(rate, range) is used with the initial outlay in the first cell of the range. What is the usual mistake?","o":["NPV treats the first cell as occurring in period 1, so the time-0 cash flow must be added outside the function","NPV ignores negative numbers","NPV uses the IRR as the discount rate","NPV automatically subtracts the initial investment"],"a":0,"e":"Excel's NPV discounts every value in the range, starting with one period of discounting for the first value. Correct use is =NPV(rate, CF1:CFn) + CF0.","d":1},{"q":"Which is a major weakness of the simple payback period?","o":["It requires a discount rate that is hard to estimate","It cannot be calculated for projects with positive cash flows","It ignores the time value of money and any cash flows after the payback date","It always favors long-lived projects"],"a":2,"e":"Payback counts dollars as if they were equal across time and stops looking once the investment is recovered. Discounted payback fixes the first flaw but not the second.","d":1},{"q":"Two projects are mutually exclusive. What does the NPV rule say?","o":["Choose the project with the highest IRR","Choose the project with the shortest payback","Accept both if both have positive NPV","Choose the project with the highest positive NPV"],"a":3,"e":"Mutually exclusive means you can only pick one. Pick the one adding the most value (highest NPV), even if its IRR is lower.","d":1},{"q":"The profitability index (PI) is the PV of future cash flows divided by the initial investment. A project should be accepted if PI is","o":["Greater than 1.0","Less than 1.0","Equal to the IRR","Greater than the WACC"],"a":0,"e":"PI greater than 1 means PV of inflows exceeds cost, which is the same as a positive NPV.","d":1},{"q":"A company already owns land that could be sold for $400,000. It plans to use the land for a new warehouse. How should the land be treated in the analysis?","o":["Ignore it, since the firm already owns the land","Include only the original purchase price","Include it only if it is sold for more than book value","Include $400,000 as an opportunity cost (after taxes) at time 0"],"a":3,"e":"By using the land the firm gives up the sale proceeds. Opportunity costs are real, relevant costs even though no cash is paid out.","d":1},{"q":"Project cost $50,000 today and returns $20,000 at the end of each of the next 3 years. The required return is 8%. What is the NPV?","o":["About $10,000","About $1,542","About -$1,542","About $9,458"],"a":1,"e":"PV of the annuity = 20,000 x 2.5771 = 51,542. NPV = 51,542 - 50,000 = $1,542. $10,000 is the undiscounted gain (60,000 - 50,000).","d":2},{"q":"A project costs $90,000 and returns $30,000, $40,000 and $50,000 in years 1, 2 and 3. What is the payback period?","o":["2.4 years","2.0 years","3.0 years","2.8 years"],"a":0,"e":"After 2 years $70,000 has been recovered. The remaining $20,000 is recovered in year 3: 20,000 / 50,000 = 0.4. Payback = 2.4 years.","d":2},{"q":"Using the same project ($90,000 cost; $30,000, $40,000, $50,000 in years 1-3) at a 10% discount rate, what is the discounted payback period?","o":["2.40 years","3.00 years, since discounting never changes payback","About 2.79 years","About 2.00 years"],"a":2,"e":"PVs: 27,273; 33,058; 37,566. After 2 years the PV recovered is 60,331, leaving 29,669. 29,669 / 37,566 = 0.79. Discounted payback is 2.79 years, longer than the simple 2.4.","d":2},{"q":"The present value of a project's future cash inflows is $120,000 and the initial investment is $100,000. What are the PI and NPV?","o":["PI = 1.20; NPV = $20,000","PI = 0.83; NPV = $20,000","PI = 1.20; NPV = $120,000","PI = 0.20; NPV = $20,000"],"a":0,"e":"PI = 120,000 / 100,000 = 1.20. NPV = 120,000 - 100,000 = 20,000. PI above 1 and NPV above 0 always agree.","d":2},{"q":"A project requires $1,000 today, produces no cash in year 1, and pays $1,210 at the end of year 2. What is the IRR?","o":["21%","10%","11%","5%"],"a":1,"e":"Solve 1,000 = 1,210 / (1 + r)^2, so (1 + r)^2 = 1.21 and r = 10%. 21% is the total two-year return, not the annual rate.","d":2},{"q":"Depreciation on new equipment is $80,000 per year and the tax rate is 25%. What is the annual depreciation tax shield?","o":["$80,000","$60,000","$20,000","$100,000"],"a":2,"e":"Depreciation is a non-cash expense, but it lowers taxable income. Tax shield = T x depreciation = 0.25 x 80,000 = $20,000 in saved taxes each year.","d":2},{"q":"A project adds $400,000 of sales and $250,000 of cash operating costs. Depreciation is $60,000 and the tax rate is 30%. What is the operating cash flow?","o":["$123,000","$105,000","$150,000","$90,000"],"a":0,"e":"EBIT = 400 - 250 - 60 = 90; tax = 27; NOPAT = 63; OCF = 63 + 60 = $123,000. (Equivalently (150,000)(0.7) + 0.3(60,000) = 105,000 + 18,000.) $105,000 omits the depreciation tax shield.","d":2},{"q":"Equipment with a book value of $20,000 is sold for $50,000. The tax rate is 25%. What is the after-tax salvage cash flow?","o":["$50,000","$37,500","$42,500","$30,000"],"a":2,"e":"Gain = 50,000 - 20,000 = 30,000; tax = 0.25 x 30,000 = 7,500; after-tax proceeds = 50,000 - 7,500 = $42,500.","d":2},{"q":"A new product will take away $40,000 per year of pre-tax profit from an existing product (cannibalization). The tax rate is 25%. How should this be treated?","o":["Ignore it because it is not a new cash flow","Reduce the project's cash flows by $40,000 per year","Add $30,000 per year to the project's cash flows","Reduce the project's after-tax cash flows by $30,000 per year"],"a":3,"e":"Side effects on other products are incremental. After tax the lost profit is 40,000 x (1 - 0.25) = $30,000 per year, which reduces the new project's cash flow.","d":2},{"q":"A project requires inventory to rise by $20,000, and accounts payable to rise by $5,000. What is the change in net working capital and how is it treated?","o":["$15,000 investment at time 0, recovered at the end of the project","$25,000 expense every year","$15,000 expense, deducted for taxes in year 1","$20,000 outflow that is never recovered"],"a":0,"e":"Change in NWC = increase in current operating assets - increase in current operating liabilities = 20,000 - 5,000 = 15,000. It is a cash outflow at the start and is usually recovered when the project ends.","d":2},{"q":"A project costs $1,000 and produces $500 at the end of each of the next 3 years. The reinvestment (and financing) rate is 10%. What is the MIRR?","o":["About 23.4%","10.0%","About 50%","About 18.3%"],"a":3,"e":"Terminal value of inflows at year 3 = 500(1.1)^2 + 500(1.1) + 500 = 605 + 550 + 500 = 1,655. MIRR = (1,655 / 1,000)^(1/3) - 1 = 18.3%. Ordinary IRR is 23.4% and assumes reinvestment at that high rate.","d":2},{"q":"Projects X and Y are mutually exclusive. The IRR of X is 16% and of Y is 14%, the crossover rate is 12%, and the WACC is 9%. At 9% NPV(X) = $1,200 and NPV(Y) = $2,100. Which should be selected?","o":["X, because it has the higher IRR","X, because it is above the crossover rate","Neither, because the IRRs are close","Y, because it has the higher NPV at the WACC"],"a":3,"e":"When the discount rate (9%) is below the crossover rate (12%), the NPV and IRR rankings conflict. NPV maximizes wealth, so choose the project with the higher NPV: Y.","d":2},{"q":"A manager changes the sales forecast alone from base case, holds everything else constant, and records the new NPV. This is","o":["Scenario analysis","Sensitivity analysis","Monte Carlo simulation","Break-even payback analysis"],"a":1,"e":"Sensitivity analysis varies one input at a time. Scenario analysis changes several inputs together (best, base, worst). Simulation draws many random combinations.","d":2},{"q":"A project has cash flows of -$100 at time 0, +$230 at time 1 and -$132 at time 2. What is true about its IRR?","o":["The IRR is unique at 15%","There is no IRR since flows are negative at the end","There are two IRRs (10% and 20%) because the signs change twice","The IRR is 130%"],"a":2,"e":"At 10%: -100 + 209.09 - 109.09 = 0; at 20%: -100 + 191.67 - 91.67 = 0. Non-normal cash flows (more than one sign change) can produce multiple IRRs, which is why NPV (or MIRR) is preferred.","d":3},{"q":"In Excel, cell B1 contains -200,000 (time 0) and B2:B5 contain $70,000 each (years 1-4). The required return is 10%. Which formula gives the correct NPV and what is it?","o":["=NPV(10%,B1:B5), about $19,901","=NPV(10%,B2:B5), about $221,891","=NPV(10%,B2:B5)+B1, about $21,891","=NPV(10%,B1:B5)+B1, about -$180,099"],"a":2,"e":"The inflows annuity is worth 70,000 x 3.1699 = 221,891 and subtracting 200,000 gives 21,891. Including B1 inside the function discounts the time-0 flow one extra period, giving 19,901. Leaving B1 out of the formula omits the investment.","d":3},{"q":"Mutually exclusive Project X (3-year life) has NPV $10,000 and Project Y (5-year life) has NPV $14,000, both at 10%. Projects can be repeated. Using the equivalent annual annuity (EAA), which is better?","o":["X, with an EAA of about $4,021 versus about $3,693 for Y","Y, because it has the higher NPV","Y, because it lasts longer","They are equal"],"a":0,"e":"EAA = NPV / annuity factor. X: 10,000 / 2.4869 = 4,021. Y: 14,000 / 3.7908 = 3,693. With unequal lives and repeatable projects, compare per-year value; X wins.","d":3},{"q":"A project has a conventional NPV of -$2 million. Management believes the project gives the firm an option to expand into a new market, valued at $3 million. What is the NPV including the real option?","o":["-$5 million","+$1 million, so the project may be worth accepting","-$2 million; real options are ignored","+$3 million"],"a":1,"e":"Strategic NPV = base NPV + value of options = -2 + 3 = +1 million. Real options (expand, abandon, delay) add value that a static DCF misses.","d":3},{"q":"The firm buys new equipment for $400,000 and pays $20,000 for installation. Net working capital must rise by $30,000. An old machine is sold for $60,000; its book value is $40,000. The tax rate is 25%. What is the net initial outlay at time 0?","o":["$390,000","$395,000","$450,000","$420,000"],"a":1,"e":"Cost = 400,000 + 20,000 = 420,000; plus NWC 30,000 = 450,000. Old machine: gain 20,000, tax 5,000, after-tax proceeds 55,000. Net outlay = 450,000 - 55,000 = $395,000.","d":3},{"q":"Scenario analysis: Worst case (25% probability) NPV = -$40,000; Base case (50%) NPV = $30,000; Best case (25%) NPV = $110,000. What is the expected NPV?","o":["$32,500","$33,333","$30,000","$25,000"],"a":0,"e":"Expected NPV = 0.25(-40,000) + 0.50(30,000) + 0.25(110,000) = -10,000 + 15,000 + 27,500 = $32,500. The simple average of the three NPVs ($33,333) ignores the probabilities.","d":3},{"q":"A project has cash flows of -$1,000 at time 0 and +$400, +$500, +$600 in years 1 to 3. What is the NPV at a discount rate of 0%, and what happens to NPV as the discount rate rises?","o":["$1,500; NPV rises as the rate rises","$500; NPV falls as the rate rises","$500; NPV stays constant","$0; NPV falls to zero"],"a":1,"e":"At 0% there is no discounting: -1,000 + 400 + 500 + 600 = 500 (the y-intercept of the NPV profile). For conventional projects, NPV falls as the rate rises and crosses zero at the IRR.","d":3},{"q":"Project S requires $100 and has NPV = $20 and IRR = 25%. Project L requires $1,000 and has NPV = $90 and IRR = 15%. They are mutually exclusive and the WACC is 10%. Which should the firm choose?","o":["S, because it has the higher IRR","S, because it has a higher return per dollar","Neither; scale differences make them incomparable","L, because it has the higher NPV"],"a":3,"e":"IRR is a percentage and ignores scale. Shareholders get $90 of value from L versus $20 from S. Unless capital is rationed, choose the higher NPV: L.","d":3},{"q":"A project requires $1,000,000 now and will produce $150,000 per year forever. The required return is 12%. What is the NPV?","o":["$150,000","$1,250,000","$250,000","$-50,000"],"a":2,"e":"PV of a perpetuity = 150,000 / 0.12 = 1,250,000. NPV = 1,250,000 - 1,000,000 = $250,000. IRR here is 15% (150,000 / 1,000,000).","d":3}],"pairs":[["NPV","PV of inflows minus investment"],["IRR","Rate where NPV equals zero"],["MIRR","IRR with realistic reinvestment rate"],["Profitability index","PV of inflows per dollar invested"],["Payback period","Time to recover initial cost"],["Crossover rate","Discount rate equating two projects' NPVs"],["Sunk cost","Past spending, irrelevant to decision"],["Real option","Management's flexibility after project starts"]],"traps":["Trap: Pick the project with the higher IRR when projects are mutually exclusive. Truth: When NPV and IRR conflict, choose the higher NPV.","Trap: Include the money already spent on a feasibility study. Truth: Sunk costs are ignored; opportunity costs and side effects are included.","Trap: Excel =NPV(r, B1:B5) with the initial outlay in B1 is correct. Truth: NPV discounts the first cell one period; use =NPV(r, B2:B5)+B1.","Trap: Depreciation is a cash outflow, so subtract it from cash flow. Truth: It is non-cash; only its tax shield (T x Dep) matters.","Trap: Payback of 2 years proves the project is good. Truth: Payback ignores time value and all cash flows after the cutoff."],"mnemonic":"'NPV is King, IRR is a Prince': when they disagree on mutually exclusive projects, King NPV rules. For cash flow: OCF = (R - C)(1 - T) + T x Dep - 'Take the after-tax profit, add back the Tax shield.'","cram":["NPV greater than 0 means accept; for mutually exclusive projects, pick the highest NPV.","IRR is the rate where NPV = 0; unreliable with multiple sign changes.","PI = PV inflows / cost; accept if greater than 1.","Payback ignores time value and later flows; discounted payback is longer than payback.","Crossover rate = IRR of the difference in cash flows.","OCF = (Rev - Cash costs)(1 - T) + T x Depreciation.","After-tax salvage = Salvage - T(Salvage - Book value); recover NWC at the end.","Excel: =NPV(r, CF1:CFn) + CF0."],"ytv":[{"title":"Capital Budgeting: NPV, IRR, MIRR, PI and Payback","channel":"Ed Kaplan","id":"TXEmehQKSYE","why":"Covers every method in this unit in one place."},{"title":"NPV vs IRR Explained","channel":"Professor Capko","id":"bjePYwQ8hfU","why":"Explains why NPV and IRR can conflict."},{"title":"NPV and IRR explained","channel":"The Finance Storyteller","id":"Fw5-wccViOM","why":"Plain-English walkthrough of the two core rules."}]},{"id":"u12","title":"Capital Structure, Leverage and Payout","day":6,"summary":"Understand operating and financial leverage, capital structure theories that explain the optimal debt mix, and how firms set dividends and buybacks.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Business risk vs financial risk","body":"<p>A company's risk has two layers, and the exam wants you to separate them.</p><ul><li><strong>Business risk</strong> is the uncertainty in operating income (EBIT) caused by the nature of the business: demand swings, input prices, competition, and how heavy its fixed costs are. It exists even in a firm with no debt.</li><li><strong>Financial risk</strong> is the <em>extra</em> risk placed on common shareholders when the firm uses debt. Interest is a fixed obligation that must be paid whether business is good or bad, so earnings per share (EPS) swing more than EBIT.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> A ski resort (heavily weather-dependent) has high business risk. If it also borrows heavily, it layers high financial risk on top of high business risk. A grocery chain has low business risk and can safely carry more debt.</p><p><strong>Leverage</strong> is the use of fixed-cost items to magnify results. <em>Operating leverage</em> uses fixed operating costs; <em>financial leverage</em> uses fixed financing costs (interest). Both are magnifiers: they raise profit when things go well and magnify losses when they go badly.</p>"},{"h":"2. Operating leverage and break-even","body":"<p><strong>Operating leverage</strong> exists when a firm has fixed operating costs (rent, salaried staff, depreciation). Once fixed costs are covered, each extra sale adds mostly profit.</p><p><strong>Degree of operating leverage:</strong></p><p><code>DOL = % change in EBIT / % change in sales = Q(P - V) / [Q(P - V) - F]</code></p><p>where Q = units, P = price, V = variable cost per unit, F = fixed operating costs, and Q(P - V) is the <em>contribution margin</em>.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> A manufacturer sells Q = 10,000 units at P = $50 with V = $30 and F = $100,000. Contribution = 10,000 x 20 = $200,000. EBIT = 200,000 - 100,000 = $100,000. DOL = 200,000 / 100,000 = <strong>2.0</strong>. A 10% rise in sales raises EBIT 20%; a 10% fall cuts EBIT 20%.</p><p><strong>Operating break-even</strong> (EBIT = 0): <code>Q = F / (P - V)</code> = 100,000 / 20 = <strong>5,000 units</strong>. DOL is highest near break-even: at 6,000 units, contribution = 120,000, EBIT = 20,000, so DOL = 6.0.</p><p>More fixed costs and fewer variable costs = higher DOL = higher business risk.</p>"},{"h":"3. Financial leverage and total leverage","body":"<p><strong>Degree of financial leverage:</strong></p><p><code>DFL = % change in EPS / % change in EBIT = EBIT / (EBIT - Interest)</code></p><p>Using the same manufacturer with $40,000 of interest: DFL = 100,000 / 60,000 = <strong>1.67</strong>. A 10% increase in EBIT raises EPS 16.7%.</p><p><strong>Degree of total leverage:</strong></p><p><code>DTL = DOL x DFL = % change in EPS / % change in sales</code></p><p>Here DTL = 2.0 x 1.67 = <strong>3.33</strong>. If sales rise 10%, EPS rises about 33.3%. If sales fall 10%, EPS falls about 33.3%. Remember <strong>multiply</strong>, do not add.</p><p>A firm with no debt has DFL = 1.0. If EBIT only just covers interest, EBIT - Interest is near zero and DFL becomes huge: tiny changes in EBIT wipe out EPS. Debt makes the firm riskier to equity holders even if it does not change EBIT.</p>"},{"h":"4. The EPS-EBIT analysis","body":"<p>To compare financing plans (all equity vs debt), plot or calculate EPS at different EBIT levels. Plans differ in two ways: the number of shares and the fixed interest. The <strong>indifference point</strong> is the EBIT where EPS is the same for both plans.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> A firm with 1,000,000 shares needs $10 million. Plan E: sell 400,000 shares at $25. Plan D: borrow at 8% ($800,000 interest). Tax rate 25%.</p><p>Set EPS equal: <code>EBIT(1 - T)/1,400,000 = (EBIT - 800,000)(1 - T)/1,000,000</code>. The tax factor cancels. EBIT/1.4 = (EBIT - 0.8)/1, so EBIT = 1.4 EBIT - 1.12, giving EBIT = <strong>$2.8 million</strong>.</p><p>Check: at EBIT = $2.8M, Plan E EPS = 2.8M x 0.75 / 1.4M = $1.50; Plan D EPS = 2.0M x 0.75 / 1.0M = $1.50. At EBIT = $4M: Plan E = $2.14, Plan D = $2.40.</p><p>Lesson: <strong>above</strong> the indifference EBIT, debt gives higher EPS (leverage helps). <strong>Below</strong> it, stock is better. But higher EPS under debt comes with higher risk, so EPS alone does not decide the best structure; value (WACC) does.</p>"},{"h":"5. Capital structure theories and the optimal structure","body":"<p><strong>MM without taxes (1958):</strong> in perfect markets the value of a firm is independent of how it is financed: <code>VL = VU</code>. Adding cheap debt raises the cost of equity by just enough to leave WACC unchanged.</p><p><strong>MM with corporate taxes (1963):</strong> interest is deductible, creating a <strong>tax shield</strong>. For permanent debt D at tax rate T:</p><p><code>VL = VU + T x D</code></p><p>Example: VU = $100M, D = $40M, T = 25%: VL = 100 + 0.25 x 40 = <strong>$110M</strong>. Taken literally this says use 100% debt, which does not happen in real life because of bankruptcy costs.</p><p><strong>Trade-off theory:</strong> firms balance the tax benefit of debt against the expected costs of financial distress (legal fees, lost customers, forced asset sales). Value rises with debt, peaks, then falls. The peak is the <strong>optimal capital structure</strong>, and it is the point where <strong>WACC is minimized and firm value is maximized</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pecking order theory:</strong> because managers know more than outsiders (<em>asymmetric information</em>), firms prefer <em>internal funds, then debt, then new equity</em>. There is no target ratio; the mix is a result of financing needs.</p><p><strong>Signaling theory:</strong> a new stock issue signals that managers think shares are overvalued, so the price often falls on announcement. Taking on more debt signals confidence that cash flows will cover payments. Firms keep <em>reserve borrowing capacity</em> so they can fund good projects without issuing stock.</p>"},{"h":"6. Dividend policy","body":"<p>Dividend policy answers: how much of earnings do we pay out versus keep for growth?</p><ul><li><strong>Dividend irrelevance (MM):</strong> in perfect markets, payout does not affect value; value depends on investment decisions and earning power. Investors who want cash can sell shares.</li><li><strong>Bird-in-the-hand:</strong> investors prefer a sure dividend to uncertain capital gains, so higher payout raises value (and lowers required return).</li><li><strong>Tax preference:</strong> where dividends are taxed more heavily than capital gains, investors prefer low payout.</li><li><strong>Clientele effect:</strong> investors choose stocks whose payout fits their needs and taxes (retirees like dividends; high-bracket investors like growth).</li><li><strong>Signaling:</strong> dividend increases signal confidence; cuts signal trouble.</li></ul><p><strong>Residual dividend policy:</strong> pay out only what is left after funding all positive-NPV projects with the target mix.</p><p><code>Dividend = Net income - (Target equity ratio x Capital budget)</code></p><p>Example: net income $10M, capital budget $12M, target 60% equity / 40% debt. Equity needed = 0.60 x 12 = $7.2M. Dividend = 10 - 7.2 = <strong>$2.8M</strong> (28% payout). If the budget were $20M, equity needed ($12M) would exceed net income and the dividend would be $0. The drawback: dividends become volatile, so most firms prefer a stable regular dividend.</p>"},{"h":"7. Repurchases, splits, stock dividends and key dates","body":"<p><strong>Stock repurchase</strong> (buyback): the firm uses cash to buy its own shares. Shares outstanding fall, EPS rises, and shareholders who want cash can sell, while others defer taxes. Example: 1,000,000 shares, net income $5M (EPS $5.00); buying back 100,000 shares gives EPS = 5M / 900,000 = $5.56. Buybacks are flexible (no commitment like a regular dividend) and can signal undervaluation, but the price should not rise merely because EPS rises; the firm also lost the cash.</p><p><strong>Stock split</strong> (for example 2-for-1): more shares, lower price, same total value. A 3-for-2 split turns 200 shares at $90 into 300 shares at $60. Used to keep the price in a popular trading range.</p><p><strong>Stock dividend</strong> (for example 10%): the firm issues additional shares to holders instead of cash. Holders get 10% more shares, price drops about 1/1.10; no value is created.</p><p><strong>Key dividend dates (in order):</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Declaration date:</strong> board announces the dividend and creates a legal liability.</li><li><strong>Ex-dividend date:</strong> buyers on or after this date do not receive the dividend; price typically drops by about the dividend amount. In the US (since the move to T+1 settlement) it is now generally the same day as the record date.</li><li><strong>Record date:</strong> holders of record on this date are entitled to the dividend.</li><li><strong>Payment date:</strong> the cash is paid.</li></ol>"}],"keyPoints":["Business risk is the uncertainty about operating income that exists even with no debt, while financial risk is the extra risk to shareholders caused by using debt.","Operating leverage comes from fixed operating costs, and the degree of operating leverage equals the percent change in EBIT divided by the percent change in sales.","Operating break even quantity equals fixed costs divided by price minus variable cost per unit.","The degree of financial leverage equals EBIT divided by EBIT minus interest.","The degree of total leverage equals the degree of operating leverage multiplied by the degree of financial leverage.","Modigliani and Miller with no taxes say that capital structure does not affect firm value.","With corporate taxes, the value of a levered firm equals the unlevered value plus the tax rate times the debt.","Trade off theory says firms balance the tax benefit of debt against the costs of financial distress, and the optimal capital structure is where WACC is lowest and firm value is highest.","Pecking order theory says firms prefer internal funds first, then debt, and new equity last, because of information asymmetry.","A residual dividend policy pays out only the net income left over after funding the equity portion of the capital budget.","The bird in the hand theory says investors prefer dividends, while MM say dividend policy is irrelevant, and the clientele effect says investors choose stocks matching their payout needs.","A stock split or stock dividend gives more shares without changing firm value, and a repurchase reduces shares outstanding and raises earnings per share."],"examples":[{"q":"A firm sells 10,000 units at $50 with variable cost $30 per unit and fixed operating costs of $100,000. Interest expense is $40,000. Find the break-even quantity, DOL, DFL, DTL, and the percentage change in EPS if sales rise 10%.","steps":["Contribution = 10,000 x (50 - 30) = 200,000. EBIT = 200,000 - 100,000 = 100,000.","Break-even Q = F / (P - V) = 100,000 / 20 = 5,000 units.","DOL = 200,000 / 100,000 = 2.0. DFL = EBIT / (EBIT - I) = 100,000 / 60,000 = 1.67.","DTL = 2.0 x 1.67 = 3.33. EPS change = 3.33 x 10% = 33.3%."],"answer":"Break-even 5,000 units; DOL 2.0; DFL 1.67; DTL 3.33; EPS rises about 33.3%."},{"q":"A firm with 1,000,000 shares needs $10 million. Plan E sells 400,000 new shares at $25. Plan D borrows at 8%. Tax rate is 25%. Find the EBIT at which EPS is the same, and which plan is better at EBIT of $4 million.","steps":["Interest under Plan D = 0.08 x 10,000,000 = 800,000. Shares: Plan E 1,400,000; Plan D 1,000,000.","Set EBIT(0.75)/1,400,000 = (EBIT - 800,000)(0.75)/1,000,000; tax cancels.","EBIT/1.4 = EBIT - 0.8 (in millions) so 0.4 EBIT = 1.12 and EBIT = 2.8 million.","At EBIT = 4M: Plan E EPS = 4M(0.75)/1.4M = 2.14; Plan D EPS = 3.2M(0.75)/1.0M = 2.40."],"answer":"Indifference EBIT = $2.8 million. At $4 million EBIT, Plan D (debt) gives the higher EPS ($2.40 vs $2.14)."},{"q":"Under a residual dividend policy, a firm has net income of $10 million, a capital budget of $12 million, and a target capital structure of 40% debt and 60% equity. What dividend and payout ratio result? What if the capital budget is $20 million?","steps":["Equity needed = 60% x 12M = 7.2M.","Dividend = Net income - equity needed = 10M - 7.2M = 2.8M.","Payout ratio = 2.8 / 10 = 28%.","If budget = 20M: equity needed = 12M, greater than net income of 10M, so dividend = 0 and the firm must also issue about 2M of new equity."],"answer":"Dividend = $2.8 million (28% payout). With a $20 million budget the dividend is $0."}],"videos":[{"title":"Degree of operating, financial and total leverage","query":"degree of operating leverage financial leverage total leverage explained"},{"title":"Modigliani Miller capital structure theory with taxes trade-off pecking order","query":"Modigliani Miller trade-off theory pecking order capital structure explained"},{"title":"Dividend policy, stock buybacks and splits","query":"dividend policy residual dividend clientele effect stock repurchase explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Business risk","b":"Uncertainty of EBIT with no debt."},{"f":"Financial risk","b":"Extra risk to shareholders from using debt."},{"f":"Operating leverage","b":"Use of fixed operating costs; magnifies EBIT changes."},{"f":"DOL","b":"% change EBIT / % change sales = Q(P-V)/[Q(P-V)-F]."},{"f":"Operating break-even","b":"Q = F / (P - V)."},{"f":"DFL","b":"EBIT / (EBIT - Interest)."},{"f":"DTL","b":"DOL x DFL; % change EPS / % change sales."},{"f":"EPS-EBIT indifference point","b":"EBIT where EPS is equal under two financing plans."},{"f":"MM without taxes","b":"VL = VU; capital structure irrelevant."},{"f":"MM with taxes","b":"VL = VU + T x D."},{"f":"Interest tax shield","b":"T x interest; value T x D for permanent debt."},{"f":"Trade-off theory","b":"Balance tax shield against distress costs."},{"f":"Pecking order theory","b":"Retained earnings, then debt, then new equity."},{"f":"Signaling theory","b":"Financing choices convey managers' private information."},{"f":"Optimal capital structure","b":"Mix that minimizes WACC and maximizes firm value."},{"f":"Residual dividend policy","b":"Dividend = NI - (equity ratio x capital budget)."},{"f":"Dividend irrelevance","b":"Payout does not affect value (MM, perfect markets)."},{"f":"Bird-in-the-hand","b":"Investors prefer sure dividends over uncertain gains."},{"f":"Clientele effect","b":"Investors pick stocks matching their payout/tax needs."},{"f":"Stock repurchase","b":"Fewer shares outstanding; EPS rises."},{"f":"Stock split vs stock dividend","b":"Both add shares, cut price; total value unchanged."},{"f":"Dividend date order","b":"Declaration, ex-dividend, record, payment."}],"questions":[{"q":"Business risk is best defined as","o":["The uncertainty about operating income (EBIT) that exists even if the firm uses no debt","The extra risk to shareholders from using borrowed money","The risk that bondholders will not be repaid","The risk that interest rates will rise"],"a":0,"e":"Business risk comes from the nature of the firm's operations (demand, costs, competition, operating leverage). The extra risk from debt is financial risk.","d":1},{"q":"Financial risk arises primarily from","o":["The firm's fixed production costs","Fluctuations in the economy","The firm's use of debt (fixed interest obligations)","Competition in the firm's industry"],"a":2,"e":"Financial leverage adds fixed interest payments, so earnings available to shareholders swing more widely when EBIT changes.","d":1},{"q":"A company has high fixed costs and low variable costs. Compared with a company with low fixed and high variable costs, it has","o":["Lower operating leverage","Higher operating leverage","Lower business risk","Higher financial leverage"],"a":1,"e":"More fixed costs mean a small change in sales produces a big change in EBIT. That is high operating leverage (and usually higher business risk).","d":1},{"q":"Which date is the first day on which a buyer of the stock will NOT receive the declared dividend?","o":["The declaration date","The payment date","The ex-dividend date","The date the board meets next"],"a":2,"e":"Buyers on or after the ex-dividend date are not entitled to the dividend. The declaration date is when the board announces it; payment is when cash is sent.","d":1},{"q":"According to Modigliani and Miller's original proposition (no taxes, no bankruptcy costs), a firm's value","o":["Rises with more debt","Is unaffected by its capital structure","Falls with more debt","Is maximized at 100% debt"],"a":1,"e":"In perfect markets with no taxes, the way cash flows are split between debt and equity does not change total value. Cheap debt is offset by rising cost of equity.","d":1},{"q":"Under MM with corporate taxes, the present value of the interest tax shield on permanent debt D at tax rate T is","o":["D x (1 - T)","T x EBIT","D / T","T x D"],"a":3,"e":"For permanent debt the PV of the tax shield is T x D. VL = VU + T x D.","d":1},{"q":"A 2-for-1 stock split will","o":["Double the firm's market value","Double the dividend per share","Reduce earnings for the whole firm","Double the number of shares and cut the price roughly in half, leaving total value unchanged"],"a":3,"e":"A split just cuts the pie into more slices. Cash and total equity value do not change.","d":1},{"q":"The pecking order theory says managers prefer to finance projects with this ranking","o":["New equity first, then debt, then internal funds","Debt first, then internal funds, then equity","Preferred stock first, then debt, then equity","Internal funds first, then debt, then new equity"],"a":3,"e":"Because of information asymmetry, issuing new equity looks like a bad signal, so firms use retained earnings first, then debt, and issue stock only as a last resort.","d":1},{"q":"A firm sells 20,000 units at $25 each. Variable cost is $15 per unit and fixed operating costs are $100,000. What is the degree of operating leverage (DOL) at this output?","o":["1.5","2.0","0.5","3.0"],"a":1,"e":"Contribution = 20,000 x (25 - 15) = 200,000. EBIT = 200,000 - 100,000 = 100,000. DOL = 200,000 / 100,000 = 2.0. A 10% rise in sales raises EBIT 20%.","d":2},{"q":"Fixed operating costs are $240,000, price is $40 and variable cost is $25 per unit. What is the operating break-even quantity?","o":["6,000 units","9,600 units","16,000 units","24,000 units"],"a":2,"e":"Break-even Q = F / (P - V) = 240,000 / 15 = 16,000 units. At that volume EBIT is zero.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has EBIT of $500,000 and interest expense of $200,000. What is its degree of financial leverage (DFL)?","o":["2.50","1.67","1.40","0.60"],"a":1,"e":"DFL = EBIT / (EBIT - Interest) = 500,000 / 300,000 = 1.67. A 10% rise in EBIT lifts EPS about 16.7%.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's DOL is 1.5 and its DFL is 2.0. If sales increase 8%, by approximately how much will EPS increase?","o":["12%","16%","24%","3.5%"],"a":2,"e":"DTL = DOL x DFL = 1.5 x 2.0 = 3.0. EPS change = 3.0 x 8% = 24%. Adding instead of multiplying the two leverage figures gives 3.5, which is wrong.","d":2},{"q":"An unlevered firm is worth $200 million. It issues $50 million of permanent debt. The tax rate is 30%. According to MM with taxes, what is the value of the levered firm?","o":["$200 million","$250 million","$215 million","$235 million"],"a":2,"e":"VL = VU + T x D = 200 + 0.30 x 50 = $215 million. Without taxes the answer would be $200 million.","d":2},{"q":"Trade-off theory says the optimal capital structure is reached when","o":["The firm has 100% debt","The marginal benefit of the interest tax shield equals the marginal cost of financial distress","The firm has zero bankruptcy costs","Cost of equity equals cost of debt"],"a":1,"e":"More debt adds tax benefits but also raises the expected cost of bankruptcy. The best mix balances the two at the point where firm value is highest.","d":2},{"q":"Net income is $8 million, the capital budget is $10 million, and the target capital structure is 50% debt and 50% equity. Under a residual dividend policy, what dividend is paid?","o":["$8 million","$5 million","$3 million","$0"],"a":2,"e":"Equity needed = 50% x 10 = $5M. Dividend = net income - equity needed = 8 - 5 = $3M. The rest of the funding ($5M) comes from new debt.","d":2},{"q":"Which theory says investors prefer a dividend today to uncertain future capital gains, so higher payout raises stock value?","o":["Dividend irrelevance theory","Bird-in-the-hand theory","Residual theory","Pecking order theory"],"a":1,"e":"Bird-in-the-hand argues a dollar of dividend is safer than a dollar of retained earnings. MM counter that investors can create their own dividends, so payout is irrelevant.","d":2},{"q":"Retirees who need steady income tend to hold high-dividend stocks, while high-bracket young investors prefer low-dividend growth stocks. This is the","o":["Signaling effect","Agency effect","Wealth effect","Clientele effect"],"a":3,"e":"Different groups of investors are attracted to firms with dividend policies matching their needs and tax situations. A sudden policy change can drive clientele away.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has 1,000,000 shares and net income of $5,000,000 (EPS $5.00). It repurchases 100,000 shares. Net income does not change. What is the new EPS?","o":["$5.00","$4.50","About $5.56","About $4.55"],"a":2,"e":"New shares = 900,000. EPS = 5,000,000 / 900,000 = $5.56. Buybacks reduce shares and raise EPS, but this is not 'free': the firm spent cash to do it.","d":2},{"q":"An investor owns 1,000 shares and the firm pays a 10% stock dividend. How many shares does the investor own afterward and what happens to the share price, approximately?","o":["1,100 shares; price falls by about 9% (price / 1.10)","1,000 shares; price unchanged","1,100 shares; price unchanged","1,000 shares plus $10 per share in cash"],"a":0,"e":"A 10% stock dividend gives 100 more shares, but the firm's value has not changed, so the price drops to roughly P / 1.10 (a 9.1% drop). The investor's total value is unchanged.","d":2},{"q":"A firm raises its regular dividend by 15% and its stock price rises on the announcement. This is best explained by","o":["The clientele effect","Signaling: managers raise dividends when they are confident about future cash flows","MM's irrelevance proposition","The tax shield on dividends"],"a":1,"e":"Dividends are costly to cut, so a hike credibly signals management's optimism. MM would predict no reaction if no new information were conveyed.","d":2},{"q":"Which date determines who is the holder of record entitled to receive the dividend?","o":["The date of record","The date of declaration","The date of payment","The date of the annual meeting"],"a":0,"e":"Those whose names appear on the company's books on the record date get the dividend. The sequence is declaration, ex-dividend / record, then payment.","d":2},{"q":"A firm has a DOL of 2.4 and a DFL of 1.5. Sales fall by 15%. By approximately what percent will EPS change?","o":["Fall by 54%","Fall by 36%","Fall by 22.5%","Fall by 5.85%"],"a":0,"e":"DTL = 2.4 x 1.5 = 3.6. EPS change = 3.6 x (-15%) = -54%. The 36% figure stops at EBIT (2.4 x 15%) and ignores financial leverage.","d":3},{"q":"A firm with 400,000 shares needs $6 million. Plan 1: sell 200,000 new shares at $30. Plan 2: borrow $6 million at 10% interest ($600,000). Ignore taxes. At what EBIT are EPS equal under the two plans?","o":["$1.8 million","$1.2 million","$2.4 million","$3.0 million"],"a":0,"e":"Set EBIT / 600,000 = (EBIT - 600,000) / 400,000. So 400,000 EBIT = 600,000 EBIT - 360 billion, giving EBIT = 1,800,000. At that level EPS = $3.00 in both plans. Above $1.8M the debt plan gives higher EPS.","d":3},{"q":"The firm's WACC at different debt ratios is: 0% debt - 11.0%; 20% - 9.8%; 40% - 9.2%; 60% - 9.6%. Which capital structure is optimal and why?","o":["40% debt, because it has the lowest WACC and therefore the highest firm value","0% debt, because it has the least risk","60% debt, because debt is cheaper than equity","20% debt, because the tax shield is largest there"],"a":0,"e":"The optimal capital structure minimizes WACC, which maximizes firm value. Beyond 40% the rising cost of distress outweighs the tax benefits and WACC rises again.","d":3},{"q":"A levered firm is worth $130 million. An identical unlevered firm is worth $120 million. The levered firm has $40 million of permanent debt and no bankruptcy costs. What corporate tax rate is implied by MM with taxes?","o":["10%","33%","7.7%","25%"],"a":3,"e":"VL - VU = T x D, so 10 = T x 40 and T = 25%. 10% would divide by VU, and 33% divides by the wrong base.","d":3},{"q":"A firm sells 10,000 units at $50 with variable costs of $30 and fixed costs of $100,000 (DOL = 2.0). If output falls to 6,000 units, what is its DOL at the new output level?","o":["2.0","1.2","3.3","6.0"],"a":3,"e":"Contribution = 6,000 x 20 = 120,000; EBIT = 120,000 - 100,000 = 20,000; DOL = 120,000 / 20,000 = 6.0. DOL rises sharply as the firm gets close to break-even (5,000 units).","d":3},{"q":"A firm has 10 million shares at $40 and net income of $30 million. It spends $20 million repurchasing shares at $40 and income is unchanged. What is EPS after the buyback?","o":["About $3.16","$3.00","$3.33","About $2.85"],"a":0,"e":"Shares repurchased = 20,000,000 / 40 = 500,000, leaving 9,500,000. EPS = 30,000,000 / 9,500,000 = $3.16 (before: $3.00).","d":3},{"q":"A firm with net income of $50 million pays $2.00 per share on 5 million shares as dividends and also spends $15 million on repurchases. What is its total payout ratio?","o":["20%","50%","30%","80%"],"a":1,"e":"Dividends = 2 x 5M = $10M. Total payout = 10M + 15M = $25M. Total payout ratio = 25 / 50 = 50%. The dividend payout ratio alone is 20%.","d":3},{"q":"A firm has EBIT of $300,000 and interest of $100,000. If EBIT falls 30%, by approximately what percent does EPS fall?","o":["45%","30%","20%","90%"],"a":0,"e":"DFL = 300,000 / (300,000 - 100,000) = 1.5. EPS falls 1.5 x 30% = 45%. Check: new EBIT = 210,000, EBT = 110,000 versus 200,000, a 45% fall.","d":3},{"q":"An investor owns 200 shares priced at $90. The firm declares a 3-for-2 stock split. Afterward, the investor holds","o":["300 shares worth $90 each ($27,000 total)","200 shares worth $135 each","133 shares worth $135 each","300 shares worth $60 each ($18,000 total, unchanged)"],"a":3,"e":"A 3-for-2 split gives 1.5 new shares per old share: 200 x 1.5 = 300 shares. Price becomes 90 / 1.5 = $60. Total value stays $18,000.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Business risk","Uncertainty of operating income"],["Financial risk","Added risk from using debt"],["DOL","Percent change EBIT over percent change sales"],["DFL","EBIT divided by EBIT minus interest"],["Interest tax shield","Tax rate times debt (permanent)"],["Pecking order","Internal funds, then debt, then equity"],["Residual dividend","Net income left after funding equity needs"],["Ex-dividend date","Buyers on or after miss dividend"]],"traps":["Trap: DTL = DOL + DFL. Truth: DTL = DOL x DFL; multiply the two leverage measures.","Trap: MM say debt never matters. Truth: Only in a no-tax world; with taxes VL = VU + T x D, and trade-off theory adds bankruptcy costs.","Trap: More debt always lowers WACC because debt is cheaper. Truth: Past the optimum, distress costs raise both debt and equity costs and WACC climbs.","Trap: A stock split or stock dividend creates value. Truth: Total value is unchanged; there are just more shares at a lower price.","Trap: The payment date decides who gets the dividend. Truth: The record date (tied to the ex-dividend date) decides eligibility."],"mnemonic":"DTL = DOL x DFL: 'Operating times Financial equals Total.' Dividend dates: 'D-E-R-P' - Declare, Ex-dividend, Record, Pay.","cram":["DOL = contribution / EBIT; break-even Q = F / (P - V).","DFL = EBIT / (EBIT - interest); DTL = DOL x DFL.","MM without taxes: capital structure is irrelevant; with taxes VL = VU + T x D.","Optimal capital structure minimizes WACC and maximizes firm value (trade-off theory).","Pecking order: internal funds, then debt, then new equity.","Residual dividend = net income - equity ratio x capital budget.","Buybacks raise EPS; splits and stock dividends do not change firm value.","Date order: declaration, ex-dividend, record, payment."],"ytv":[{"title":"Operating Leverage and Financial Leverage","channel":"RK Varsity","id":"fjoMxS6XcgA","why":"Covers DOL, DFL and how they combine."},{"title":"Degree of Operating Leverage explained","channel":"Banks& Markets","id":"sinTdqZImoY","why":"Short worked example of DOL."},{"title":"How to Understand Degree of Financial Leverage (DFL)?","channel":"Simple Explain","id":"vmestdqU_hc","why":"Intuition for financial leverage and EPS."}]}];